I hope to see you there.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.490
And as always, thank you for
your interest in science.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.190
And now, for my discussion
with Mark Zuckerberg

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:35.750
and Dr. Priscilla Chan.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:37.700
Priscilla, Mark, so
great to meet you.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.920
And thank you for having
me here in your home.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.360
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Oh, Thanks
for having us on the podcast.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.235
PRISCILLA CHAN: Yeah.

00:05:42.235 --> 00:05:44.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd like to
talk about the CZI, the Chan

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:46.130
Zuckerberg Initiative.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.080
I learned about this
a few years ago,

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.920
when my lab was-- and
still is now-- at Stanford,

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.710
as a very exciting
philanthropic effort

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.740
that has a truly big mission.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.443
I can't imagine
a bigger mission.

00:05:59.443 --> 00:06:01.610
So maybe you could tell us
what that big mission is.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.190
And then we can get into
some of the mechanics of how

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:09.210
that big mission can
become a reality.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:14.150
PRISCILLA CHAN: So like
you're mentioning, in 2015,

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.820
we launched the Chan
Zuckerberg Initiative.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.790
And what we were
hoping to do at CZI

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:22.820
was think about how do we build
a better future for everyone

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.400
and looking for ways
where we can contribute

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.370
the resources that we have
to bring philanthropically

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:31.250
and the experiences that
Mark and I have had,

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.260
for me as a physician
and educator,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.840
for Mark as an
engineer, and then

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.340
our ability to bring teams
together to build the builders.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.820
Mark has been a builder
throughout his career.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.890
And what could we
do if we actually

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:50.390
put together a team to build
tools, do great science?

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.000
And so within our
science portfolio,

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.870
we've really been focused
on what some people think

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:59.630
is either an incredibly
audacious goal

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:02.000
or an inevitable goal.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.530
But I think about
it as something

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.920
that will happen if we
continue focusing on it, which

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.090
is to be able to cure,
prevent, or manage

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.890
all disease by the
end of the century.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:11.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: All disease?

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:12.580
PRISCILLA CHAN: All disease.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.740
So that's important, right?

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.260
And so a lot of times, people
ask like, which disease?

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.660
And the whole point is that
there is not one disease.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:23.940
And it's really about taking
a step back to where I always

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.400
found the most hope
as a physician, which

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.760
is new discoveries
and new opportunities

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.120
and new ways of understanding
how to keep people well come

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.350
from basic science.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:41.280
So our strategy at CZI is really
to build tools, fund science,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.210
change the way basic
scientists can see the world

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:49.350
and how they can move
quickly in their discoveries.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.640
And so that's what
we launched in 2015.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.990
We do work in three ways.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:58.680
We fund great scientists.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.970
We build tools-- right
now, software tools

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:07.050
to help move science along and
make it easier for scientists

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.040
to do their work.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.690
And we do science.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:12.060
You mentioned Stanford
being an important pillar

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:13.320
for our science work.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:17.880
We've built what we call
biohubs, institutes where teams

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:22.680
can take on grand
challenges to do work that

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.170
wouldn't be possible
in a single lab

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.880
or within a single discipline.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.040
And our first
biohub was launched

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.270
in San Francisco, a
collaboration between Stanford,

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.880
UC Berkeley, and UCSF.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.559
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Amazing.

00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:42.450
Curing all diseases implies
that there will either

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.060
be a ton of knowledge gleaned
from this effort, which

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.610
I'm certain there will be--
and there already has been.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:51.060
We can talk about some of those
early successes in a moment.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.210
But it also sort of implies
that if we can understand

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:59.040
some basic operations
of diseases and cells

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.600
that transcend autism,
Huntington's, Parkinson's,

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.410
cancer and any other
disease that perhaps there

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:11.310
are some core principles that
would make the big mission

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.100
a real reality, so to speak.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.860
What I'm basically saying is,
how are you attacking this?

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.730
My belief is that the cell sits
at the center of all discussion

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:24.790
about disease, given that
our body is made up of cells

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:26.270
and different types of cells.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.470
So maybe you could
just illuminate for us

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:34.660
a little bit of what the
cell is, in your mind,

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:38.260
as it relates to disease and
how one goes about understanding

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.260
disease in the context of cells
because, ultimately, that's

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:42.280
what we're made up of.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:42.760
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:45.010
Well, let's get to the
cell thing in a moment.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.500
But just even taking
a step back from that,

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:49.963
we don't think,
at CZI, that we're

00:09:49.963 --> 00:09:51.880
going to cure, prevent
or manage all diseases.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.940
The goal is to basically
give the scientific community

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.430
and scientists around
the world the tools

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.050
to accelerate the
pace of science.

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:01.630
And we spent a lot
of time, when we

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.170
were getting started
with this, looking

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.750
at the history of science and
trying to understand the trends

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.292
and how they've
played out over time.

00:10:08.292 --> 00:10:11.640
And if you look over
this very long-term arc,

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.150
most large-scale
discoveries are preceded

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.030
by the invention of a new tool
or a new way to see something.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.530
And it's not just
in biology, right?

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.020
It's like having
a telescope came

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:26.400
before a lot of discoveries
in astronomy and astrophysics.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:30.360
But similarly, the microscope
and just different ways

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.100
to observe things or
different platforms,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.500
like the ability to do
vaccines preceded the ability

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:37.870
to cure a lot of
different things.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.555
So this is the engineering part
that you were talking about,

00:10:40.555 --> 00:10:41.430
about building tools.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:45.960
We view our goal is to
try to bring together

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:48.900
some scientific and engineering
knowledge to build tools

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.150
that empower the whole field.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.060
And that's the big arc
and a lot of the things

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:57.090
that we're focused on, including
the work in single cell

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:02.033
and cell understanding,
which you can jump in and get

00:11:02.033 --> 00:11:02.950
into that if you want.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:05.440
But yeah, I think I
think we generally

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.390
agree with the
premise that if you

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:10.910
want to understand this
stuff from first principles--

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.500
people study organs a lot right.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.150
You study how things
present across the body.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:19.360
But there's not a very
widespread understanding

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:21.490
of how each cell operates.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:24.648
And this is a big part of
some of the initial work

00:11:24.648 --> 00:11:27.190
that we tried to do on the Human
Cell Atlas and understanding

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:28.840
what are the different cells.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.730
And there's a bunch
more work that we want

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:33.310
to do to carry that forward.

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:36.670
But overall, I think, when we
think about the next 10 years

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:39.910
here of this long arc to
try to empower the community

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:44.710
to be able to cure, prevent
or manage all diseases,

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:46.810
we think that the next
10 years should really

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.080
be primarily about being
able to measure and observe

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:51.373
more things in human biology.

00:11:51.373 --> 00:11:52.790
There are a lot
of limits to that.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:55.332
It's like you want to look at
something through a microscope,

00:11:55.332 --> 00:11:57.250
you can't usually
see living tissues

00:11:57.250 --> 00:12:00.260
because it's hard to see through
skin or things like that.

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:03.460
So there are a lot of
different techniques

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.390
that will help us
observe different things.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.990
And this is where the
engineering background

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:09.760
comes in a bit because--

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:12.700
I mean, when I think about this
is from the perspective of how

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:15.340
you'd write code or
something, the idea of trying

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.190
to debug or fix a code base,
but not be able to step

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.020
through the code
line by line, it's

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:21.280
not going to happen, right?

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:25.175
And at the beginning of any
big project that we do at Meta,

00:12:25.175 --> 00:12:27.550
we like to spend a bunch of
the time up front just trying

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:29.350
to instrument things
and understand

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:31.142
what are we going to
look at and how are we

00:12:31.142 --> 00:12:33.940
going to measure things so
we know we're making progress

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:35.120
and know what to optimize.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.360
And this is such a
long-term journey

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.000
that we think that it actually
makes sense to take the next 10

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:43.720
years to build those
kinds of tools for biology

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:48.520
and understanding just how the
human body works in action.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:49.988
And a big part of
that is, cells.

00:12:49.988 --> 00:12:50.530
I don't know.

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.915
Do you want to jump and talk
about some of the efforts?

00:12:52.915 --> 00:12:53.200
PRISCILLA CHAN: Sure.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could I just
interrupt briefly and just ask

00:12:55.700 --> 00:13:00.550
about the different
interventions, so to speak,

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:05.320
that CZI is in a unique
position to bring to the quest

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:06.710
to cure all diseases?

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:07.940
So I can think of--

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:11.920
I mean, I know, as a scientist,
that money is necessary but not

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:12.703
sufficient, right?

00:13:12.703 --> 00:13:14.620
When you have money, you
can hire more people.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:16.520
You can try different things.

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:17.350
So that's critical.

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:19.058
But a lot of philanthropy
includes money.

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:26.030
The other component is you
want to be able to see things,

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:27.320
as you pointed out.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.960
So you want to know that
normal disease process--

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:31.250
like, what is a healthy cell?

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:32.645
What's a diseased cell?

00:13:32.645 --> 00:13:35.390
Are cells constantly being
bombarded with challenges

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:36.590
and then repairing those?

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:38.370
And then what we
call cancer is just

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:39.920
a runaway train of
those challenges

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.840
not being met by the cell
itself or something like that?

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.210
So better imaging tools.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:47.510
And then it sounds like there's
not just a hardware component,

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:48.860
but a software component.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:50.100
This is where AI comes in.

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.520
So maybe, at some point,
we can break this up

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.060
into two, three
different avenues.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.330
One is understanding
disease processes

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:57.410
and healthy processes.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:58.550
We'll lump those together.

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:01.190
Then there's hardware--
so microscopes,

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:04.250
lenses, digital
deconvolution, ways

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:08.240
of seeing things in bolder
relief and more precision.

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:11.930
And then there's how
to manage all the data.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.660
And then I love the
idea that maybe AI

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:17.360
could do what human
brains can't do alone,

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:18.943
like manage
understanding of the data

00:14:18.943 --> 00:14:20.610
because it's one thing
to organize data.

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:23.370
It's another to say, oh,
this as you point out

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:26.100
in the analogy with code,
that this particular gene

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:29.220
and that particular gene
are potentially interesting,

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:30.780
whereas a human
being would never

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:33.013
make that potential connection.

00:14:33.013 --> 00:14:33.930
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.250
PRISCILLA CHAN: So
the tools that CZI

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:36.450
can bring to the table--

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:38.985
we fund science, like
you're talking about.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:43.560
There's lots of ways
to fund science.

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:46.710
And just to be
clear, what we fund

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:49.920
is a tiny fraction of what
the NIH funds, for instance.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So you guys
have been generous enough

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:57.805
that it definitely holds
wait to NIH's contribution.

00:14:57.805 --> 00:14:58.680
PRISCILLA CHAN: Yeah.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.183
But I think every funder has
its own role in the ecosystem.

00:15:02.183 --> 00:15:03.600
And for us, it's
really, how do we

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.650
incentivize new points of view?

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:07.260
How do we incentivize
collaboration?

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:09.760
How do we incentivize
open science?

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:14.490
And so a lot of our grants
include inviting people

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.040
to look at different fields.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:22.650
Our first neuroscience RFA was
aimed towards incentivizing

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:24.960
people from different
backgrounds-- immunologists,

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:27.360
microbiologists--
to come and look

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:31.200
at how our nervous system works
and how to keep it healthy.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:35.280
Or we ask that our
grantees participate

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.080
in the pre-print
movement to accelerate

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:40.470
the rate of sharing knowledge
and actually others being

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:42.210
able to build upon science.

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:44.970
So that's the
funding that we do.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:49.980
In terms of building, we
build software and hardware,

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:50.910
like you mentioned.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:54.270
We put together
teams that can build

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:59.460
tools that are more durable
and scalable than someone

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.800
in a single lab might
be incentivized to do.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.570
There's a ton of great ideas.

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:07.530
And nowadays, most scientists
can tinker and build

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:09.240
something useful for their lab.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:11.550
But it's really
hard for them to be

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:14.010
able to share that
tool sometimes

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:17.610
beyond their own laptop
or forget the next Lab

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:19.750
over or across the globe.

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:23.340
So we partner with scientists
to see what is useful,

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:24.420
what kinds of tools.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:29.820
In imaging, Napari, it's
a useful image annotation

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:34.170
tool that is born from
an open source community.

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.060
And how can we
contribute to that?

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:40.350
Or a CELLxGENE, which works
on single cell data sets.

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:44.220
And how can we make it build a
useful tool so that scientists

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.650
can share data sets,
analyze their own

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:50.740
and contribute to a larger
corpus of information?

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:55.050
So we have software teams that
are building, collaborating

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:57.120
with scientists to make
sure that we're building

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.810
easy to use, durable,
translatable tools

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:04.510
across the scientific community
in the areas that we work in.

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:08.940
We also have institutes-- this
is where the imaging work comes

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.990
in-- where we are proud owners
of an electron microscope

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:14.349
right now.

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:18.930
It's going to be installed
at our imaging institute.

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:21.390
And that will really
contribute to the way

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:22.859
where we can see
work differently.

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:26.780
But more hardware does
need to be developed.

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:30.560
We're partnering with
the fantastic scientists

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:36.800
in the biohub network to build
a mini-phase plate to increase

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:42.950
to align the electrons through
the electron microscope

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:44.600
to be able to increase
the resolution,

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.580
so we can see in sharper detail.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.510
So there's a lot of innovative
work within the network that's

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:51.950
happening.

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:55.880
And these institutes
have grand challenges

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:57.620
that they're working on.

00:17:57.620 --> 00:17:59.840
Back to your
question about cells,

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:03.890
cells are just the smallest
unit that are alive.

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:07.880
And your body,
all of our bodies,

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:10.430
have many, many, many cells.

00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:14.270
Some estimate of like
37 trillion cells,

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:15.620
different cells in your body.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:17.210
And what are they all doing?

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.880
And what do they look
like when you're healthy?

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:21.610
What do they look
like when you're sick?

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:28.070
And where we're at right now
with our understanding of cells

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:30.760
and what happens
when you get sick

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:35.770
is basically we've gotten pretty
good at, from the Human Genome

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:38.470
Project, looking at
how different mutations

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:40.690
in your genetic
code lead for you

00:18:40.690 --> 00:18:43.360
to be more susceptible
to get sick or directly

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:44.690
cause you to get sick.

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:49.120
So we go from a mutation
in your DNA to, wow,

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:53.440
you now have Huntington's
disease, for instance.

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.530
And there's a lot that
happens in the middle.

00:18:56.530 --> 00:19:00.640
And that's one of the questions
that we're going after at CZI,

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:02.750
is what actually happens.

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:05.800
So an analogy that I like to
use to share with my friends

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:08.860
is, right now, say we
have a recipe for a cake.

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:11.080
We know there's a
typo in the recipe.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.320
And then the cake is awful.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:15.580
That's all we know.

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:18.790
We don't know how the
chef interprets the typo.

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:20.770
We don't know what
happens in the oven.

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:24.170
And we don't actually
know how it's exactly

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:26.060
connected to how the
cake didn't turn out

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:28.610
or how you had expected it.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:32.000
A lot of that is unknown.

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:34.310
But we can actually
systematically try

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:35.390
to break this down.

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:38.720
And one segment of that
journey that we're looking at

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:42.350
is how that mutation
gets translated and acted

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:43.760
upon in your cells.

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:47.870
And all of your cells
have what's called mRNA.

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:53.580
mRNA are the actual instructions
that are taken from the DNA.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:56.450
And our work in
Single-Cell is looking

00:19:56.450 --> 00:20:02.300
at how every cell in your
body is actually interpreting

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.760
your DNA slightly
differently and what

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:07.580
happens when healthy cells
are interpreting the DNA

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:09.680
instructions and
when sick cells are

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.210
interpreting those directions.

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:13.550
And that is a ton of data.

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:15.980
I just told you, there's
37 trillion cells.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:21.210
There's different large
sets of mRNA in each cell.

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:25.550
But the work that we've been
funding is looking at how--

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.950
first of all, gathering
that information.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:31.460
We've been incredibly
lucky to be

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:36.920
part of a very fast-moving
field where we've gone from,

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:41.630
in 2017, funding some
methods work to now

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.960
having really not complete,
but nearly complete

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:49.850
atlases of how the human body
works, how flies work, how mice

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:53.210
work at the single-cell
level and being

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.940
able to then try
to piece together

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.850
how does that all come
together when you're healthy

00:20:58.850 --> 00:20:59.990
and when you're sick.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:05.000
And the neat thing about
the inflection point

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:09.200
where we're at in AI is that
I can't look at this data

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:10.260
and make sense of it.

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:11.450
There's just too much of it.

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:13.880
And biology is complex.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.180
Human bodies are complex.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.210
We need this much information.

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:20.150
But the use of large
language models

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:22.700
can help us actually
look at that data

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:25.820
and gain insights,
look at what trends

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:32.960
are consistent with health and
what trends are unsuspected.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:36.920
And eventually, our
hope, through the use

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:39.050
of these data sets that
we've helped curate

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:41.270
and the application of
large language models,

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:44.780
is to be able to formulate a
virtual cell, a cell that's

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:49.250
completely built off of
the data sets of what

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:52.820
we know about the human body,
but allows us to manipulate,

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:56.240
and learn faster and
try new things to help

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:59.382
move science and
then medicine along.

00:21:59.382 --> 00:22:00.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Do you think we've

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:05.250
cataloged the total number
of different cell types?

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:07.560
Every week, I look
at great journals

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:08.820
like Cell Nature and Science.

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:12.150
And for instance, I saw
recently that, using single cell

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:16.980
sequencing, they've categorized
18 plus different types

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:18.000
of fat cells.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.430
We always think of like a fat
cell versus a muscle cell.

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:22.440
So now, you've got 18 types.

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:25.560
Each one is going to express
many, many different genes

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.360
and mRNAs.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:31.350
And perhaps one of
them is responsible

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:35.010
for what we see in
advanced type 2 diabetes,

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:38.820
or in other forms of obesity,
or where people can't lay down

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.190
fat cells, which turns out
to be just as detrimental

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:42.760
in those extreme cases.

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:44.790
So now, you've got all
these lists of genes.

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:50.187
But I always thought of single
cell sequencing as necessary,

00:22:50.187 --> 00:22:51.270
but not sufficient, right?

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:54.340
You need the information, but
it doesn't resolve the problem.

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:57.990
And I think of it more as
a hypothesis-generating

00:22:57.990 --> 00:22:59.097
experiment.

00:22:59.097 --> 00:23:00.430
OK, so you have all these genes.

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:02.430
And you can say, well,
this gene is particularly

00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:07.300
elevated in the diabetic
cell type of, let's say,

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:09.940
one of these fat cells or
muscle cells for that matter,

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:11.990
whereas it's not
in non-diabetics.

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:15.760
So then of the millions
of different cells,

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:19.580
maybe only five of them
differ dramatically.

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:21.280
So then you generate
a hypothesis.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:22.630
Oh, it's the ones that
differ dramatically

00:23:22.630 --> 00:23:23.422
that are important.

00:23:23.422 --> 00:23:29.110
But maybe one of those genes,
when it's only 50% changed,

00:23:29.110 --> 00:23:32.480
has a huge effect because of
some network biology effect.

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:35.080
And so I guess what I'm
trying to get to here

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:37.660
is how does one
meet that challenge.

00:23:37.660 --> 00:23:40.030
And can AI help
resolve that challenge

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:42.220
by essentially placing
those lists of genes

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:44.630
into 10,000 hypotheses?

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:46.630
Because I'll tell you
that the graduate students

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:49.060
and postdocs in my lab
get a chance to test one

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:50.170
hypothesis at a time.

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:50.570
PRISCILLA CHAN: I know.

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:51.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And that's
really the challenge,

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:52.750
let alone one lab.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:54.705
And so for those
that are listening

00:23:54.705 --> 00:23:56.080
to this-- and
hopefully, it's not

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:59.260
getting outside the scope
of standard understanding

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:01.155
or the understanding
we've generated here.

00:24:01.155 --> 00:24:02.530
But what I'm
basically saying is,

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:04.300
you have to pick at some point.

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:05.710
More data always sounds great.

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:08.200
But then how do you
decide what to test?

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.930
PRISCILLA CHAN: So no, we
don't know all the cell types.

00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:15.070
I think one thing that was
really exciting when we first

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:18.790
launched this work
was cystic fibrosis.

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:21.670
Cystic fibrosis is caused
by mutation in CFTR.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:23.060
That's pretty well known.

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:26.080
It affects a certain channel
that makes it hard for mucus

00:24:26.080 --> 00:24:26.830
to be cleared.

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:28.900
That's the basics
of cystic fibrosis.

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:31.240
When I went to medical
school, it was taught as fact.

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:32.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So their
lungs fill up with fluid.

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:34.527
These are people who
are carrying around

00:24:34.527 --> 00:24:35.830
sacks of fluid filling up.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:36.880
PRISCILLA CHAN: Yep.

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:37.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I've worked
with people like that.

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:39.490
And they have to literally
dump the fluid out.

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:39.820
PRISCILLA CHAN: Exactly.

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:41.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: They can't
run or do intense exercise.

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:42.760
Life is shorter.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:44.093
PRISCILLA CHAN: Life is shorter.

00:24:44.093 --> 00:24:48.040
And when we applied single-cell
methodologies to the lungs,

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:51.550
they discovered an
entirely new cell type

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:56.530
that actually is affected by
a mutation in the CF mutation,

00:24:56.530 --> 00:24:58.570
in cystic fibrosis
mutation, that

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:00.220
actually changes
the paradigm of how

00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:01.660
we think about cystic fibrosis.

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:02.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Amazing.

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:04.437
PRISCILLA CHAN: [? Just ?]
[? unknown. ?] So I don't think

00:25:04.437 --> 00:25:06.323
we know all the cell types.

00:25:06.323 --> 00:25:07.990
I think we'll continue
to discover them.

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:10.870
And we'll continue to discover
new relationships between cell

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:13.990
and disease, which leads me
to the second example I want

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:17.710
to bring up, is
this large data set

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:19.960
that the entire
scientific community has

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:21.160
built around single cell.

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:26.030
It's starting to allow us to
say this mutation, where is it

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:26.530
expressed?

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:28.700
What types of cell
types it's expressed in?

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:31.540
And we actually
have built a tool

00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:36.587
at CZI called CELLxGENE, where
you can put in the mutation

00:25:36.587 --> 00:25:37.670
that you're interested in.

00:25:37.670 --> 00:25:40.390
And it gives you a heat
map of cross cell types

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:45.190
of which cell types are
expressing the gene that you're

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:46.160
interested in.

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:48.490
And so then you can
start looking at, OK,

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:55.400
if I look at gene X and I know
it's related to heart disease--

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:57.100
but if you look at
the heat map, it's

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.140
also spiking in the pancreas.

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:01.180
That allows you to
generate a hypothesis.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:02.250
Why?

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:04.860
And what happens when
this gene is mutated

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:07.380
and the function
of your pancreas?

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:11.910
Really exciting way to look
and ask questions differently.

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:14.610
And you can also
imagine a world where

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:19.890
if you're trying to develop a
therapy, a drug, and the goal

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:21.870
is to treat the
function in the heart,

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:23.820
but you know that
it's also really

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:25.560
active in the pancreas again.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:29.280
So is there going to be
an unexpected side effect

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.040
that you should think
about as you're bringing

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:34.000
this drug to clinical trials?

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:36.990
So it's an incredibly
exciting tool

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:38.670
and one that's only
going to get better

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:41.010
as we get more and
more sophisticated

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:42.162
ways to analyze the data.

00:26:42.162 --> 00:26:43.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I must say, I love

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:45.960
that because if I look at
the advances in neuroscience

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:49.080
over the last 15
years, most of them

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:51.735
didn't necessarily come from
looking at the nervous system.

00:26:51.735 --> 00:26:54.840
They came from the understanding
that the immune system

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:55.590
impacts the brain.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:57.540
Everyone prior to that
talked about the brain

00:26:57.540 --> 00:26:59.260
as an immune-privileged organ.

00:26:59.260 --> 00:27:02.380
What you just said
also bridges the divide

00:27:02.380 --> 00:27:05.470
between single cells,
organs and systems, right?

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:07.602
Because ultimately,
cells make up organs.

00:27:07.602 --> 00:27:08.560
Organs make up systems.

00:27:08.560 --> 00:27:09.890
And they're all
talking to one another.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:12.310
And everyone nowadays is
familiar with gut-brain axis

00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:14.050
or the microbiome
being so important.

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:20.950
But rarely is the discussion
between organs discussed,

00:27:20.950 --> 00:27:21.680
so to speak.

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:23.710
So I think it's wonderful.

00:27:23.710 --> 00:27:26.500
So that tool was
generated by CZI.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:27.758
Or CCI funded that tool?

00:27:27.758 --> 00:27:29.050
MARK ZUCKERBERG: We built that.

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:29.560
PRISCILLA CHAN: We built it.

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:30.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You built it.

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:31.780
So is it built by Meta?

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:32.397
Is this Meta?

00:27:32.397 --> 00:27:34.480
MARK ZUCKERBERG: No, no,
it has its own engineers.

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:35.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:35.917
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

00:27:35.917 --> 00:27:37.960
They're completely
different organizations.

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:39.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Incredible.

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:41.352
And so a graduate
student or postdoc

00:27:41.352 --> 00:27:43.060
who's interested in
a particular mutation

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:45.250
could put this mutation
into this database.

00:27:45.250 --> 00:27:46.900
That graduate student
or postdoc might

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:49.810
be in a laboratory known
for working on heart,

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:51.790
but suddenly find that
they're collaborating

00:27:51.790 --> 00:27:55.450
with other scientists that work
on the pancreas, which also

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:57.665
is wonderful because
it bridges the divide

00:27:57.665 --> 00:27:58.540
between these fields.

00:27:58.540 --> 00:28:00.470
Fields are so
siloed in science--

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:02.140
not just different
buildings, but people

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:04.330
rarely talk, unless things
like this are happening.

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:05.890
PRISCILLA CHAN: I mean, the
graduate student is someone

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:07.765
that we want to empower
because, one, they're

00:28:07.765 --> 00:28:09.430
the future of
science, as you know.

00:28:09.430 --> 00:28:12.430
And within CELLxGENE,
if you put in the gene

00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:14.590
you're interested in and
it shows you the heat map,

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:18.560
we also will pull up the most
relevant papers to that gene.

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:20.733
And so read these things.

00:28:20.733 --> 00:28:22.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's fantastic.

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:24.100
As we all know,
quality nutrition

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:26.500
