00:29:52.462 --> 00:29:54.837
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I just think
going back to your question

00:29:54.837 --> 00:29:57.460
from before are there going
to be more cell types that

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:58.120
get discovered?

00:29:58.120 --> 00:29:59.500
I mean, I assume so, right?

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:01.828
I mean, no catalog of
this stuff is ever--

00:30:01.828 --> 00:30:03.370
it doesn't seem like
we're ever done.

00:30:03.370 --> 00:30:04.810
we keep on finding more.

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:09.180
But I think that that
gets to one of the things

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:12.780
that I think are the
strengths of modern LLMs,

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:16.320
is the ability to imagine
different states that things

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:17.100
can be in.

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:20.610
So from all the work that
we've done and funded

00:30:20.610 --> 00:30:24.090
on the Human Cell Atlas, there
is a large corpus of data

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:28.930
that you can now train a
kind of large-scale model on.

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:32.255
And one of the things
that we're doing at CZI,

00:30:32.255 --> 00:30:33.630
which I think is
pretty exciting,

00:30:33.630 --> 00:30:38.460
is building what we think is one
of the largest non-profit life

00:30:38.460 --> 00:30:41.100
sciences AI clusters.

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:44.190
It's on the order of 1,000 GPUs.

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:47.280
And it's larger than what
most people have access

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:52.830
to in academia that you can do
serious engineering work on.

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:55.740
And by basically
training a model

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:57.360
with all of the
Human Cell Atlas Data

00:30:57.360 --> 00:31:00.690
and a bunch of other
inputs as well,

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:04.200
we think you'll be able
to basically imagine

00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:06.270
all of the different
types of cells and all

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:08.920
the different states that they
can be in, and when they're

00:31:08.920 --> 00:31:10.570
healthy and diseased,
and how they'll

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:14.620
interact with different--

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:16.390
interact with each
other, interact

00:31:16.390 --> 00:31:18.010
with different potential drugs.

00:31:18.010 --> 00:31:20.320
But I think the state
of LLMs, I think

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:22.420
this is where it's
helpful to understand--

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:24.160
have a good understanding
and be grounded

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:25.870
in the modern state of AI.

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:28.180
I mean, these things
are not foolproof.

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:30.400
I mean, one of the
flaws of modern LLMs

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:31.960
is they hallucinate.

00:31:31.960 --> 00:31:33.760
So the question is,
how do you make it

00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:37.420
so that that can be an advantage
rather than a disadvantage?

00:31:37.420 --> 00:31:39.820
And I think the way that it
ends up being an advantage

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:42.400
is when they help you
imagine a bunch of states

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:45.098
that someone could be in, but
then you, as the scientist

00:31:45.098 --> 00:31:47.140
or engineer, go and validate
that those are true,

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:49.360
whether they're solutions
to how a protein can

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:51.940
be folded or possible
states that a cell could

00:31:51.940 --> 00:31:53.920
be in when it's interacting
with other things.

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:56.830
But we're not yet
at the state with AI

00:31:56.830 --> 00:31:59.410
that you can just take the
outputs of these things

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:02.710
as gospel and run from there.

00:32:02.710 --> 00:32:05.350
But they are very good,
I think as you said,

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:09.520
hypothesis generators or
possible solution generators

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:10.930
that then you can go validate.

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:14.140
So I think that that's
a very powerful thing

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:15.460
that we can basically--

00:32:15.460 --> 00:32:17.680
building on the first
five years of science work

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:19.540
around the Human Cell Atlas
and all the data that's

00:32:19.540 --> 00:32:21.640
been built out-- carry
that forward into something

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:25.822
that I think is going to be a
very novel tool going forward.

00:32:25.822 --> 00:32:27.280
And that's the type
of thing that I

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:29.290
think we're set up to do well.

00:32:29.290 --> 00:32:34.990
I mean, you had this exchange a
little while back about funding

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:40.210
levels and how CZI is just a
drop in the bucket compared

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:42.730
to NIH.

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:45.370
The thing that I think we
can do that's different

00:32:45.370 --> 00:32:50.500
is funding some of these
longer term, bigger projects.

00:32:50.500 --> 00:32:53.200
It is hard to galvanize
the and pull together

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:54.650
the energy to do that.

00:32:54.650 --> 00:32:58.660
And it's a lot of what most
science funding is, relatively

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:00.280
small projects
that are exploring

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:02.710
things over relatively
short time horizons.

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:04.480
And one of the things
that we try to do

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:09.040
is build these tools over
5, 10, 15-year periods.

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:10.800
They're often
projects that require

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:12.550
hundreds of millions
of dollars of funding

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:15.520
and world-class engineering
teams and infrastructure to do.

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:19.300
And that, I think, is a pretty
cool contribution to the field

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:21.820
that I think is--

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:23.832
there aren't as
many other folks who

00:33:23.832 --> 00:33:25.040
are doing that kind of thing.

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:25.930
But that's one of
the reasons why

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:28.330
I'm personally excited
about the virtual cell stuff

00:33:28.330 --> 00:33:31.228
because it just this perfect
intersection of all the

00:33:31.228 --> 00:33:32.770
stuff that we've
done in single cell,

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:36.250
the previous collaborations
that we've done with the field

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:39.940
and bringing together
the industry and AI

00:33:39.940 --> 00:33:41.140
expertise around this.

00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:42.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, I completely

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:46.510
agree that the model of science
that you're putting together

00:33:46.510 --> 00:33:49.240
with CZI isn't just
unique from NIH,

00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:50.710
but it's extremely
important that

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:54.398
the independent
investigator model is what's

00:33:54.398 --> 00:33:56.440
driven the progression of
Science in this country

00:33:56.440 --> 00:34:00.220
and, to some extent, in Northern
Europe for the last 100 years.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:04.120
And it's wonderful,
on the one hand,

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:08.290
because it allows for that
image we have of a scientist

00:34:08.290 --> 00:34:10.480
tinkering away or the people
in their lab, and then

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:12.429
the eurekas.

00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:16.690
And that hopefully translates
to better human health.

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:21.159
But I think, in my opinion,
we've moved past that model

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:23.650
as the most effective model
or the only model that

00:34:23.650 --> 00:34:24.554
should be explored.

00:34:24.554 --> 00:34:26.679
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah, I
just think it's a balance.

00:34:26.679 --> 00:34:27.040
You want that.

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:28.210
But you want to
empower those people.

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:30.010
I think that that's these
tools empower those folks.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:30.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sure.

00:34:30.100 --> 00:34:32.260
And there are mechanisms
to do that, like NIH.

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:34.570
But it's hard to do
collaborative science.

00:34:34.570 --> 00:34:37.810
It's interesting that we're
sitting here not far--

00:34:37.810 --> 00:34:40.059
because I grew up right
near here as well.

00:34:40.059 --> 00:34:43.810
I'm not far from the garage
model of tech, right?

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:48.550
The Hewlett-Packard model,
not far from here at all.

00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:51.940
And the idea was the tinkerer
in the garage, the inventor.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:54.100
And then people often
forget that to implement

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:55.600
all the technologies
they discovered

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:58.840
took enormous factories
and warehouses.

00:34:58.840 --> 00:35:01.580
So there's a similarity there
to Facebook, Meta, et cetera.

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:03.310
But I think, in
science, we imagine

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:05.380
that the scientists
alone in their laboratory

00:35:05.380 --> 00:35:06.520
and those eureka moments.

00:35:06.520 --> 00:35:10.210
But I think, nowadays, the
big questions really require

00:35:10.210 --> 00:35:13.508
extensive collaboration and
certainly tool development.

00:35:13.508 --> 00:35:15.550
And one of the tools that
you keep coming back to

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:17.500
is these LLMs, these
large language models.

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:19.060
And maybe you could
just elaborate,

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:20.800
for those that aren't familiar.

00:35:20.800 --> 00:35:24.700
What is a large language model?

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:27.850
For the uninformed, what is it?

00:35:27.850 --> 00:35:33.850
And what does it allow us to
do that different, other types

00:35:33.850 --> 00:35:35.440
of AI don't allow?

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:36.970
Or more importantly,
perhaps what

00:35:36.970 --> 00:35:40.510
does it allow us to do that a
bunch of really smart people,

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:42.580
highly informed in a
given area of science,

00:35:42.580 --> 00:35:43.720
staring at the data--

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:46.613
what can it do
that they can't do?

00:35:46.613 --> 00:35:47.530
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Sure.

00:35:47.530 --> 00:35:51.160
So I think a lot of the
progression of machine learning

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:56.080
has been about building systems,
neural networks or otherwise,

00:35:56.080 --> 00:36:00.280
that can basically make sense
and find patterns in larger

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:01.750
and larger amounts of data.

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:04.510
And there was a breakthrough
a number of years

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:08.680
back that some folks
at Google actually made

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:11.470
called this transformer
model architecture.

00:36:11.470 --> 00:36:13.810
And it was this
huge breakthrough

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:17.380
because before then there
was somewhat of a cap

00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:20.200
where if you fed more
data into a Neural Network

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:23.200
past some point,
it didn't really

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:26.140
glean more insights from
it, whereas transformers

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:28.390
just-- we haven't seen
the end of how big that

00:36:28.390 --> 00:36:29.170
can scale to yet.

00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:30.712
I mean, I think that
there's a chance

00:36:30.712 --> 00:36:32.950
that we run into some ceiling.

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:34.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
it never asymptotes?

00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:35.650
MARK ZUCKERBERG: We
haven't observed it yet.

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:37.692
But we just haven't built
big enough systems yet.

00:36:37.692 --> 00:36:39.618
So I would guess that--

00:36:39.618 --> 00:36:40.160
I don't know.

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:41.535
I think that this
is actually one

00:36:41.535 --> 00:36:43.780
of the big questions
in the AI field today,

00:36:43.780 --> 00:36:47.300
is basically, are transformers
and are the current model

00:36:47.300 --> 00:36:48.470
architectures sufficient?

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:50.210
If you just build larger
and larger clusters,

00:36:50.210 --> 00:36:51.500
do you eventually
get something that's

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:53.600
like human intelligence
or super intelligence?

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:57.260
Or is there some kind
of fundamental limit

00:36:57.260 --> 00:36:59.940
to this architecture that
we just haven't reached yet?

00:36:59.940 --> 00:37:03.723
And once we get a little bit
further in building them out,

00:37:03.723 --> 00:37:04.640
then we'll reach that.

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:06.140
And then we'll need
a few more leaps

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:10.280
before we get to the
level of AI that I

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:12.800
think will unlock
a ton of really

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:14.180
futuristic and amazing things.

00:37:14.180 --> 00:37:15.890
But there's no doubt
that even just being

00:37:15.890 --> 00:37:17.870
able to process
the amount of data

00:37:17.870 --> 00:37:21.590
that we can now with
this model architecture

00:37:21.590 --> 00:37:23.583
has unlocked a lot
of new use cases.

00:37:23.583 --> 00:37:26.000
And the reason why they're
called large language models is

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:30.470
because one of the first uses
of them is people basically

00:37:30.470 --> 00:37:34.580
feed in all of the language
from, basically, the world

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:35.270
wide web.

00:37:35.270 --> 00:37:38.185
And you can think about them as
basically prediction machines.

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:42.710
You put in a prompt.

00:37:42.710 --> 00:37:46.010
And it can basically
predict a version

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:47.130
of what should come next.

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:50.750
So you type in a headline
for a news story.

00:37:50.750 --> 00:37:54.460
And it can predict what it
thinks the story should be.

00:37:54.460 --> 00:37:56.410
Or you could train
it so that it could

00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:58.870
be a chat, bot
where, OK, if you're

00:37:58.870 --> 00:38:02.385
prompted with this question,
you, can get this response.

00:38:02.385 --> 00:38:03.760
But one of the
interesting things

00:38:03.760 --> 00:38:07.000
is it turns out that there's
actually nothing specific

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:08.510
to using human language in it.

00:38:08.510 --> 00:38:11.320
So if instead of feeding
it human language, if you

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:14.770
use that model architecture
for a network and instead

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:18.640
you feed it all of the
Human Cell Atlas Data,

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:21.670
then if you prompt it
with a state of a cell,

00:38:21.670 --> 00:38:24.790
it can spit out
different versions

00:38:24.790 --> 00:38:28.550
of how that cell can
interact or different states

00:38:28.550 --> 00:38:30.550
that the cell could be
in next when it interacts

00:38:30.550 --> 00:38:31.400
with different things.

00:38:31.400 --> 00:38:32.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Does it have
to take a genetics class?

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:34.900
So for instance, if you give
it a bunch of genetics data,

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:36.730
do you have to say, hey,
by the way, and then

00:38:36.730 --> 00:38:39.313
you give it a genetics class so
it understands that you've got

00:38:39.313 --> 00:38:41.315
DNA, RNA, mRNA, and proteins?

00:38:41.315 --> 00:38:43.930
MARK ZUCKERBERG: No, I think
that the basic nature of all

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:46.420
these machine learning
techniques is they're

00:38:46.420 --> 00:38:48.740
basically pattern
recognition systems.

00:38:48.740 --> 00:38:53.960
So there are these very
deep statistical machines

00:38:53.960 --> 00:38:57.860
that are very efficient
at finding patterns.

00:38:57.860 --> 00:38:59.650
So it's not actually--

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:02.210
you don't need to teach
a language model that's

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:07.370
trying to speak a language
a lot of specific things

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:08.613
about that language either.

00:39:08.613 --> 00:39:10.280
You just feed it in
a bunch of examples.

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:14.930
And then let's say you teach
it about something in English,

00:39:14.930 --> 00:39:17.060
but then you also give
it a bunch of examples

00:39:17.060 --> 00:39:19.100
of people speaking Italian.

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:21.530
It'll actually be able to
explain the thing that it

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:23.780
learned in English in Italian.

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:27.590
So the crossover and just
the pattern recognition

00:39:27.590 --> 00:39:30.860
is the thing that is pretty
profound and powerful

00:39:30.860 --> 00:39:31.550
about this.

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:34.500
But it really does apply to
a lot of different things.

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:37.580
Another example in the
scientific community

00:39:37.580 --> 00:39:40.820
has been the work
that AlphaFold,

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:45.320
basically the folks at DeepMind,
have done on protein folding.

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:49.590
It's just basically a lot of
the same model architecture.

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:51.470
But instead of
language, there they

00:39:51.470 --> 00:39:54.653
fold they fed in all
of these protein data.

00:39:54.653 --> 00:39:55.820
And you can give it a state.

00:39:55.820 --> 00:39:59.570
And it can spit out solutions to
how those proteins get folded.

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:01.020
So it's very powerful.

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:05.810
I don't think we know
yet, as an industry, what

00:40:05.810 --> 00:40:07.907
the natural limits of it are.

00:40:07.907 --> 00:40:09.740
I think that that's one
of the things that's

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:11.600
pretty exciting about
the current state.

00:40:11.600 --> 00:40:15.320
But it's certainly allows
you to solve problems

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:19.190
that just weren't solved with
the generation of machine

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:21.302
learning that came before it.

00:40:21.302 --> 00:40:22.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It sounds like CZI

00:40:22.760 --> 00:40:27.260
is moving a lot of work that was
just done in vitro, in dishes,

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:30.530
and in vivo, in
living organisms,

00:40:30.530 --> 00:40:34.040
model organisms are humans,
to in silico, as we say.

00:40:34.040 --> 00:40:38.600
So do you foresee a future where
a lot of biomedical research,

00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:44.420
certainly the work of CZI
included, is done by machines?

00:40:44.420 --> 00:40:46.970
I mean, obviously,
it's much lower cost.

00:40:46.970 --> 00:40:49.160
And you can run millions
of experiments, which,

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:50.993
of course, is not to
say that humans are not

00:40:50.993 --> 00:40:51.920
going to be involved.

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:56.690
But I love the idea that we
can run experiments in silico

00:40:56.690 --> 00:40:58.250
en masse.

00:40:58.250 --> 00:41:01.370
PRISCILLA CHAN: I think
in silico experiments are

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:05.240
going to be incredibly helpful
to test things quickly,

00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:11.180
cheaply and just unleash
a lot of creativity.

00:41:11.180 --> 00:41:13.760
I do think you need to be
very careful about making

00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:18.650
sure it still translates
and matches the humans.

00:41:18.650 --> 00:41:22.040
One thing that's
funny in basic science

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:27.080
is we've basically cured
every single disease in mice.

00:41:27.080 --> 00:41:30.470
We know what's going on when
they have a number of diseases

00:41:30.470 --> 00:41:32.750
because they're used
as a model organism.

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:34.490
But they are not humans.

00:41:34.490 --> 00:41:36.800
And a lot of times,
that research

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:40.730
is relevant, but not
directly one-to-one

00:41:40.730 --> 00:41:42.240
translatable to humans.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:45.050
So you just have to be really
careful about making sure

00:41:45.050 --> 00:41:48.593
that it actually
works for humans.

00:41:48.593 --> 00:41:50.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sounds
like what CZI is doing

00:41:50.510 --> 00:41:53.870
is actually creating
a new field.

00:41:53.870 --> 00:41:55.760
As I'm hearing all of
this, I'm thinking, OK,

00:41:55.760 --> 00:42:00.350
this transcends immunology
department, cardiothoracic

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:02.270
surgery, I mean neuroscience.

00:42:02.270 --> 00:42:06.113
I mean, the idea of a new field,
where you certainly embrace

00:42:06.113 --> 00:42:08.030
the realities of
universities and laboratories

00:42:08.030 --> 00:42:09.740
because that's where most of
the work that you're funding

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:10.240
is done.

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:11.045
Is that right?

00:42:11.045 --> 00:42:11.240
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Mm-hmm.

00:42:11.240 --> 00:42:12.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So maybe we need

00:42:12.860 --> 00:42:16.530
to think about what it means
to do science differently.

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:19.460
And I think that's one of the
things that's most exciting.

00:42:19.460 --> 00:42:21.643
Along those lines, it seems
that bringing together

00:42:21.643 --> 00:42:23.060
a lot of different
types of people

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:27.905
at different major
institutions is going

00:42:27.905 --> 00:42:29.030
to be especially important.

00:42:29.030 --> 00:42:34.090
So I know that the initial
CZI Biohub, gratefully,

00:42:34.090 --> 00:42:35.740
included Stanford.

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:37.600
We'll put that
first in the list,

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:39.850
but also UCSF, forgive me.

00:42:39.850 --> 00:42:42.910
I have many friends at
UCSF and also Berkeley.

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:47.200
But there are now some
additional institutions

00:42:47.200 --> 00:42:47.840
involved.

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:49.298
So maybe you could
talk about that,

00:42:49.298 --> 00:42:52.840
and what motivated the decision
to branch outside the Bay Area

00:42:52.840 --> 00:42:56.500
and why you selected those
particular additional

00:42:56.500 --> 00:42:57.955
institutions to be included.

00:42:57.955 --> 00:43:00.220
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Well,
I'll just say it.

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:02.693
A big part of why we wanted
to create additional biohubs

00:43:02.693 --> 00:43:04.360
is we were just so
impressed by the work

00:43:04.360 --> 00:43:07.330
that the folks who were
running the first biohub did.

00:43:07.330 --> 00:43:08.770
PRISCILLA CHAN: Yeah.

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:11.860
And you should walk
through the work

00:43:11.860 --> 00:43:14.350
of the Chicago Biohub
and the New York Biohub

00:43:14.350 --> 00:43:15.530
that we just announced.

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:18.640
But I think it's actually an
interesting set of examples

00:43:18.640 --> 00:43:22.000
that balance the
limits of what you want

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:25.210
to do with physical
material engineering

00:43:25.210 --> 00:43:28.760
and where things are
purely biological

00:43:28.760 --> 00:43:31.407
because the Chicago team
is really building more

00:43:31.407 --> 00:43:33.990
sensors to be able to understand
what's going on in your body.

00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:37.370
But that's more of a physical
kind of engineering challenge,

00:43:37.370 --> 00:43:40.010
whereas the New York
team-- we basically

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:43.010
talk about this as like a
cellular endoscope of being

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:45.800
able to have an immune
cell or something that

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:50.283
can go and understand,
what's the thing that's

00:43:50.283 --> 00:43:51.200
going on in your body?

00:43:51.200 --> 00:43:52.950
But it's not a physical
piece of hardware.

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:58.070
It's a cell that you can
basically have just go report

00:43:58.070 --> 00:44:00.783
out on different things that
are happening inside the body.

00:44:00.783 --> 00:44:03.241
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, so making
the cell the the microscope.

00:44:03.241 --> 00:44:04.085
PRISCILLA CHAN: Totally.

00:44:04.085 --> 00:44:04.910
MARK ZUCKERBERG: And
then eventually actually

00:44:04.910 --> 00:44:05.780
being able to act on it.

00:44:05.780 --> 00:44:08.160
But I mean, you should go
into more detail on all this.

00:44:08.160 --> 00:44:09.618
PRISCILLA CHAN: So
a core principle

00:44:09.618 --> 00:44:13.910
of how we think about biohubs
is that it has to be--

00:44:13.910 --> 00:44:15.680
when we invited
proposals, it has

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:17.720
to be at least
three institutions,

00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:22.130
so really breaking down the
barrier of a single university,

00:44:22.130 --> 00:44:25.520
oftentimes asking for the
people designing the research

00:44:25.520 --> 00:44:30.430
aim to come from all different
backgrounds and to explain why

00:44:30.430 --> 00:44:32.920
that the problem that
they want to solve

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:38.050
requires interdisciplinary,
inter-university, institution

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:40.690
collaboration to
actually make happen.

00:44:40.690 --> 00:44:42.670
We just put that
request for proposal

00:44:42.670 --> 00:44:45.370
out there with our
San Francisco Biohub

00:44:45.370 --> 00:44:47.170
as an example,
where they've done

00:44:47.170 --> 00:44:52.330
incredible work in single cell
biology and infectious disease.

00:44:52.330 --> 00:44:54.400
And we got--

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:56.800
I want to say--
like 57 proposals

00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:00.400
from over 150 institutions.

00:45:00.400 --> 00:45:02.200
A lot of ideas came together.

00:45:02.200 --> 00:45:05.440
And we were so, so
excited that we've

00:45:05.440 --> 00:45:08.600
been able to launch
Chicago and New York.

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:11.740
Chicago is a collaboration
between UIUC,

00:45:11.740 --> 00:45:14.320
University of Illinois
Urbana-Champaign,

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:18.730
and University of
Chicago and Northwestern.

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:21.220
Obviously, these universities
are multifaceted.

00:45:21.220 --> 00:45:25.270
But if I were to describe
them by their stereotypical

00:45:25.270 --> 00:45:30.250
strength, Northwestern has
an incredible medical system

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:31.660
and hospital system.

00:45:31.660 --> 00:45:35.710
University of Chicago
brings to the table

00:45:35.710 --> 00:45:37.960
incredible basic
science strengths.

00:45:37.960 --> 00:45:41.680
University of Illinois is
a computing powerhouse.

00:45:41.680 --> 00:45:44.027
And so they came
together and proposed

00:45:44.027 --> 00:45:45.610
that they were going
to start thinking

00:45:45.610 --> 00:45:50.380
about cells in tissue,
so one of the layers

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:52.160
that you just alluded to.

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:57.130
So how do the cells that we know
behave and act differently when

00:45:57.130 --> 00:45:58.780
they come together as a tissue?

00:45:58.780 --> 00:46:01.270
And one of the first tissues
that they're starting with

00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:02.180
is skin.

00:46:02.180 --> 00:46:06.040
So they've already been
able to, as a collaboration

00:46:06.040 --> 00:46:11.290
under the leadership, of
Shana Kelly design engineered

00:46:11.290 --> 00:46:12.730
skin tissue.

00:46:12.730 --> 00:46:17.020
The architecture looks the
same as what's in you and I.

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:21.580
And what they've done is
built these super, super thin

00:46:21.580 --> 00:46:22.120
sensors.

00:46:22.120 --> 00:46:25.600
And they embed these sensors
throughout the layers

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:27.100
of this engineered tissue.

00:46:27.100 --> 00:46:29.120
And they read out the data.

00:46:29.120 --> 00:46:33.110
They want to see what
these cells are secreting,

00:46:33.110 --> 00:46:34.800
how these cells
talk to each other

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:37.550
and what happens when
these cells get inflamed.

00:46:37.550 --> 00:46:39.980
Inflammation is an
incredibly important process

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:43.350
that drives 50% of all deaths.

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:46.400
And so this is another
disease-agnostic approach.

00:46:46.400 --> 00:46:48.140
We want to understand
inflammation.

00:46:48.140 --> 00:46:50.330
And they're going to
get a ton of information

00:46:50.330 --> 00:46:55.400
out from these sensors that tell
you what happens when something

00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:58.280
goes awry because
right now we can say,

00:46:58.280 --> 00:47:00.080
when you have an
allergic reaction,

00:47:00.080 --> 00:47:01.820
your skin gets red and puffy.

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:04.400
But what is the
earliest signal of that?

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:08.150
And these sensors can
look at the behaviors

00:47:08.150 --> 00:47:09.560
of these cells over time.

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:11.660
And then you can apply
a large language model

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:14.780
to look at the earliest
statistically significant

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:19.710
changes that can allow you to
intervene as early as possible.

00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:21.800
So that's what Chicago's doing.

00:47:21.800 --> 00:47:24.800
They're starting
in the skin cells.

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:28.030
They're also looking at the
neuromuscular junction, which

00:47:28.030 --> 00:47:32.050
is the connection between where
a neuron attaches to a muscle

00:47:32.050 --> 00:47:33.640
and tells the muscle
how to behave--

00:47:33.640 --> 00:47:38.680
super important in things
like ALS, but also in aging.

00:47:38.680 --> 00:47:41.290
The slowed transmission
of information

00:47:41.290 --> 00:47:42.820
across that
neuromuscular junction

00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:44.710
is what causes old
people to fall.

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:47.900
Their brain cannot trigger their
muscles to react fast enough.

00:47:47.900 --> 00:47:50.200
And so we want to
be able to embed

00:47:50.200 --> 00:47:55.120
these sensors to understand how
these different, interconnected

00:47:55.120 --> 00:47:57.610
systems within our
bodies work together.

00:47:57.610 --> 00:48:03.820
In New York, they're doing a
related, but equally exciting

00:48:03.820 --> 00:48:08.320
project where they're
engineering individual cells

00:48:08.320 --> 00:48:14.990
to be able to go in and identify
changes in a human body.

00:48:14.990 --> 00:48:18.130
So what they'll do is--

00:48:18.130 --> 00:48:19.270
they're calling it--

00:48:19.270 --> 00:48:20.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's wild.

00:48:20.060 --> 00:48:20.893
I mean, I love that.

00:48:20.893 --> 00:48:22.077
I mean, this is--

00:48:22.077 --> 00:48:23.410
I don't want to go on a tangent.

00:48:23.410 --> 00:48:25.900
But for those that want to
look it up adaptive optics,

00:48:25.900 --> 00:48:28.812
there's a lot of
distortion and interference

00:48:28.812 --> 00:48:30.520
when you try and look
at something really

00:48:30.520 --> 00:48:31.720
small or really far away.

00:48:31.720 --> 00:48:34.540
And really smart
physicists figured out,

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:37.510
well, use the interference
as part of the microscope.

00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:40.340
Make those actually
lenses of the microscope.

00:48:40.340 --> 00:48:41.140
MARK ZUCKERBERG: We
should talk about imaging

00:48:41.140 --> 00:48:43.420
separately after you talk
about the New York Biohub.

00:48:43.420 --> 00:48:45.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's extremely
clever, along those lines.

00:48:45.340 --> 00:48:46.150
It's not intuitive.

00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:49.060
But then when you hear it, it's
like it makes so much sense.

00:48:49.060 --> 00:48:50.530
It's not immediately intuitive.

00:48:50.530 --> 00:48:53.260
Make the cells that already
can navigate to tissues

00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:55.298
or embed themselves in
tissues be the microscope

00:48:55.298 --> 00:48:56.090
within that tissue.

00:48:56.090 --> 00:48:57.085
I love it.

00:48:57.085 --> 00:48:58.380
PRISCILLA CHAN: Totally.

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:00.520
The way that I explain
this to my friends

00:49:00.520 --> 00:49:03.760
and my family is this
is Fantastic Voyage,

00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:06.130
but real life.

00:49:06.130 --> 00:49:07.810
We are going into
the human body.

00:49:07.810 --> 00:49:11.020
And we're using the immune
cells, which are privileged

00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:13.300
and already working to
keep your body healthy,

00:49:13.300 --> 00:49:17.000
and being able to target them
to examine certain things.

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:21.190
So you can engineer an immune
cell to go in your body

00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:24.470
and look inside your
coronary arteries and say,

00:49:24.470 --> 00:49:26.620
are these arteries healthy?

00:49:26.620 --> 00:49:27.760
Or are there plaques?

00:49:27.760 --> 00:49:30.400
Because plaques
lead to blockage,

00:49:30.400 --> 00:49:32.350
which lead to heart attacks.

00:49:32.350 --> 00:49:35.350
And the cell can then
record that information

00:49:35.350 --> 00:49:36.480
and report it back out.

00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:38.230
That's the first half
of what the New York

00:49:38.230 --> 00:49:39.430
Biohub is going to do.

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:39.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fantastic.

00:49:39.970 --> 00:49:41.720
PRISCILLA CHAN: The
second half is can you

00:49:41.720 --> 00:49:44.270
then engineer the cells to
go do something about it.

00:49:44.270 --> 00:49:46.270
Can I then tell
a different cell,

00:49:46.270 --> 00:49:49.150
immune cell that is able
to transport in your body

00:49:49.150 --> 00:49:52.490
to go in and clean that
up in a targeted way?

00:49:52.490 --> 00:49:56.080
And so it's incredibly exciting.

00:49:56.080 --> 00:49:58.060
They're going to
study things that

00:49:58.060 --> 00:50:01.180
are immune privilege, that
your immune system normally

00:50:01.180 --> 00:50:03.250
doesn't have access to--

00:50:03.250 --> 00:50:06.370
things like ovarian
and pancreatic cancer.

00:50:06.370 --> 00:50:09.700
They'll also look at a number
of neurodegenerative diseases,

00:50:09.700 --> 00:50:14.440
since the immune system doesn't
presently have a ton of access

00:50:14.440 --> 00:50:17.020
into the nervous system.

00:50:17.020 --> 00:50:22.000
But it's both mind blowing
and it feels like sci-fi.

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:23.740
But science is
actually in a place

00:50:23.740 --> 00:50:26.920
where if you really push
a group of incredibly

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:29.620
qualified scientists
say, could you do this

00:50:29.620 --> 00:50:33.040
if given the chance, the
answer is like probably.

00:50:33.040 --> 00:50:36.820
Give us enough time, the
bright team and resources.

00:50:36.820 --> 00:50:37.660
It's doable.

00:50:37.660 --> 00:50:38.577
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

00:50:38.577 --> 00:50:40.480
I mean, it's a 10
to 15-year project.

00:50:40.480 --> 00:50:43.258
But it's awesome,
engineered cells, yeah.

00:50:43.258 --> 00:50:44.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
love the optimism.

00:50:44.800 --> 00:50:47.260
And the moment you said make
the cell the microscope,

00:50:47.260 --> 00:50:50.110
so to speak, I was
like yes, yes and yes.

00:50:50.110 --> 00:50:52.270
It just makes so much sense.

00:50:52.270 --> 00:50:56.980
What motivated the decision
to do the work of CZI

00:50:56.980 --> 00:51:00.190
in the context of existing
universities as opposed to--

00:51:00.190 --> 00:51:02.590
there's still some real
estate up in Redwood City

00:51:02.590 --> 00:51:04.930
where there's a bunch of
space to put biotech companies

00:51:04.930 --> 00:51:08.050
and just hiring people
from all backgrounds

00:51:08.050 --> 00:51:11.650
and saying, hey, have at it and
doing this stuff from scratch?

00:51:11.650 --> 00:51:14.680
I mean, it's a very
interesting decision

00:51:14.680 --> 00:51:16.360
to do this in the
context of an existing

00:51:16.360 --> 00:51:18.730
framework of graduate students
that need to do their thesis

00:51:18.730 --> 00:51:20.563
and get a first author
paper because there's

00:51:20.563 --> 00:51:22.300
a whole set of structures
within academia

00:51:22.300 --> 00:51:24.610
that I think both
facilitate, but also limit

00:51:24.610 --> 00:51:26.230
the progression of science.

00:51:26.230 --> 00:51:28.270
That independent
investigator model

00:51:28.270 --> 00:51:30.460
that we talked about
a little bit earlier,

00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:32.842
it's so core to the way
science has been done.

00:51:32.842 --> 00:51:34.550
This is very different
and frankly sounds

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:37.190
far more efficient, if I'm
to be completely honest.

00:51:37.190 --> 00:51:41.360
And we'll see if I renew my
NIH funding after saying that.

00:51:41.360 --> 00:51:43.700
But I think we all
want the same thing.

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:47.850
As scientists and
as humans, we want

00:51:47.850 --> 00:51:49.100
to understand the way we work.

00:51:49.100 --> 00:51:53.305
And we want healthy people
to persist to be healthy.

00:51:53.305 --> 00:51:54.930
And we want sick
people to get healthy.

00:51:54.930 --> 00:51:56.680
I mean, that's really
ultimately the goal.

00:51:56.680 --> 00:51:57.840
It's not super complicated.

00:51:57.840 --> 00:51:58.670
It's just hard to do.

00:51:58.670 --> 00:52:00.420
PRISCILLA CHAN: So the
teams at the biohub

00:52:00.420 --> 00:52:03.250
are actually independent
of the universities.

00:52:03.250 --> 00:52:04.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

00:52:04.250 --> 00:52:06.083
PRISCILLA CHAN: So each
biohub will probably

00:52:06.083 --> 00:52:11.060
have in total maybe 50 people
working on deep efforts.

00:52:11.060 --> 00:52:14.840
However, it's an acknowledgment
that not all of the best

00:52:14.840 --> 00:52:17.120
scientists who can
contribute to this area

00:52:17.120 --> 00:52:21.500
are actually going to, one,
want to leave a university

00:52:21.500 --> 00:52:25.520
or want to take on the
full-time scope of this project.

00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:29.480
So it's the ability to
partner with universities

00:52:29.480 --> 00:52:33.830
and to have the faculty
at all the universities

00:52:33.830 --> 00:52:37.250
be able to contribute
to the overall project,

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:39.220
is how the biohub is structured.

00:52:39.220 --> 00:52:40.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

00:52:40.220 --> 00:52:42.720
MARK ZUCKERBERG: But a lot of
the way that we're approaching

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:45.920
CZI is this long-term,
iterative project

00:52:45.920 --> 00:52:48.200
to figure out-- try a
bunch of different things,

00:52:48.200 --> 00:52:51.680
figure out which things produce
the most interesting results,

00:52:51.680 --> 00:52:55.610
and then double down on those
in the next five-year push.

00:52:55.610 --> 00:52:57.800
So we just went
through this period

00:52:57.800 --> 00:53:00.200
where we wrapped
up the first five

00:53:00.200 --> 00:53:01.695
years of the science program.

00:53:01.695 --> 00:53:03.320
And we tried a lot
of different models,

00:53:03.320 --> 00:53:04.570
all kinds of different things.

00:53:04.570 --> 00:53:07.310
And it's not that
the biohub model--

00:53:07.310 --> 00:53:10.130
we don't think it's
the best or only model.

00:53:10.130 --> 00:53:14.090
But we found that it was
a really interesting way

00:53:14.090 --> 00:53:16.370
to unlock a bunch
of collaboration

00:53:16.370 --> 00:53:18.950
and bring some
technical resources that

00:53:18.950 --> 00:53:20.840
allow for this longer
term development.

00:53:20.840 --> 00:53:25.370
And it's not something that
is widely being pursued

00:53:25.370 --> 00:53:26.600
across the rest of the field.

00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:29.090
So we figured, OK, this
is an interesting thing

00:53:29.090 --> 00:53:30.500
that we can help push on.

00:53:30.500 --> 00:53:34.620
But I mean, yeah, we do
believe in the collaboration.

00:53:34.620 --> 00:53:37.910
But I also think that
we come at this with--

00:53:37.910 --> 00:53:40.340
we don't think that the way
that we're pursuing this

00:53:40.340 --> 00:53:42.345
is the only way to
do this or the way

00:53:42.345 --> 00:53:43.470
that everyone should do it.

00:53:43.470 --> 00:53:49.010
We're pretty aware of what
is the rest of the ecosystem

00:53:49.010 --> 00:53:51.110
and how we can play
a unique role in it.

00:53:51.110 --> 00:53:52.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It
feels very synergistic

00:53:52.250 --> 00:53:53.810
with the way science
is already done

00:53:53.810 --> 00:53:56.780
and also fills an incredibly
important niche that,

00:53:56.780 --> 00:53:59.420
frankly, wasn't filled before.

00:53:59.420 --> 00:54:01.350
Along the lines of
implementation--

00:54:01.350 --> 00:54:05.930
so let's say your large language
models combined with imaging

00:54:05.930 --> 00:54:10.790
tools reveal that a
particular set of genes acting

00:54:10.790 --> 00:54:12.110
in a cluster--

00:54:12.110 --> 00:54:15.320
I don't know-- set
up an organ crash.

00:54:15.320 --> 00:54:18.170
Let's say the pancreas
crashes at a particular stage

00:54:18.170 --> 00:54:19.980
of pancreatic cancer.

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:22.850
I mean, it's still one of the
most deadliest of the cancers.

00:54:22.850 --> 00:54:26.390
And there are others that you
certainly wouldn't want to get.

00:54:26.390 --> 00:54:29.900
But that's among the ones you
wouldn't want to get the most.

00:54:29.900 --> 00:54:30.883
So you discover that.

00:54:30.883 --> 00:54:32.300
And then and the
idea is that, OK,

00:54:32.300 --> 00:54:34.880
then AI reveals
some potential drug

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:38.460
targets that then bear
out in vitro, in a dish

00:54:38.460 --> 00:54:40.700
and in a mouse model.

00:54:40.700 --> 00:54:43.970
How is the actual implementation
to drug discovery?

00:54:43.970 --> 00:54:47.150
Or maybe this target is
druggable, maybe it's not.

00:54:47.150 --> 00:54:49.760
Maybe it requires
some other approach--

00:54:49.760 --> 00:54:52.730
laser ablation
approach or something.

00:54:52.730 --> 00:54:53.690
We don't know.

00:54:53.690 --> 00:54:55.790
But ultimately,
is CZI going to be

00:54:55.790 --> 00:54:58.070
involved in the implementation
of new therapeutics?

00:54:58.070 --> 00:54:59.308
Is that the idea?

00:54:59.308 --> 00:55:00.350
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Less so.

00:55:00.350 --> 00:55:02.130
PRISCILLA CHAN: Less so.

00:55:02.130 --> 00:55:04.640
This is where it's important
to work in an ecosystem

00:55:04.640 --> 00:55:06.650
and to know your
own limitations.

00:55:06.650 --> 00:55:10.070
There are groups, and
startups and companies

00:55:10.070 --> 00:55:14.330
that take that and bring it to
translation very effectively.

00:55:14.330 --> 00:55:16.850
I would say the
place where we have

00:55:16.850 --> 00:55:19.880
a small window into
that world is actually

00:55:19.880 --> 00:55:22.490
our work with rare
disease groups.

00:55:22.490 --> 00:55:25.940
We have, through our
Rare As One portfolio,

00:55:25.940 --> 00:55:30.470
funded patient advocates
to create rare disease

00:55:30.470 --> 00:55:37.180
organizations where patients
come together and actually pool

00:55:37.180 --> 00:55:39.130
their collective experience.

00:55:39.130 --> 00:55:41.470
They build
bioregistries, registries

00:55:41.470 --> 00:55:42.850
of their natural history.

00:55:42.850 --> 00:55:46.360
And they both partner
with researchers

00:55:46.360 --> 00:55:48.280
to do the research
about their disease

00:55:48.280 --> 00:55:53.440
and with drug developers to
incentivize drug developers

00:55:53.440 --> 00:55:56.840
to focus on what they may
need for their disease.

00:55:56.840 --> 00:55:59.350
And one thing that's
important to point out

00:55:59.350 --> 00:56:01.030
is that rare
diseases aren't rare.

00:56:01.030 --> 00:56:03.820
There are over
7,000 rare diseases

00:56:03.820 --> 00:56:08.420
and collectively impact
many, many individuals.

00:56:08.420 --> 00:56:14.240
And I think the thing
that's, from a basic science

00:56:14.240 --> 00:56:16.850
perspective, the incredibly
fascinating thing

00:56:16.850 --> 00:56:20.030
about rare diseases is that
they're actually windows to how

00:56:20.030 --> 00:56:22.650
the body normally should work.

00:56:22.650 --> 00:56:28.340
And so there are often
mutations that when

00:56:28.340 --> 00:56:32.000
genes that when they're mutated
cause very specific diseases,

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:36.200
but that tell you how the
normal biology works as well.

00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:37.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

00:56:37.250 --> 00:56:42.710
So you discussed basically the
major goals and initiatives

00:56:42.710 --> 00:56:46.460
of the CZI for the next,
say, 5 to 10 years.

00:56:46.460 --> 00:56:48.740
And then beyond
that, the targets

00:56:48.740 --> 00:56:51.170
will be explored by
biotech companies.

00:56:51.170 --> 00:56:54.480
They'll grab those targets, and
test them and implement them.

00:56:54.480 --> 00:56:56.105
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
There's also, I think,

00:56:56.105 --> 00:56:58.550
been a couple of teams from
the initial biohub that

00:56:58.550 --> 00:57:01.140
were interested in spinning
out ideas into startups.

00:57:01.140 --> 00:57:04.070
So even though it's
not a thing that we're

00:57:04.070 --> 00:57:07.880
going to pursue because
we're a philanthropy,

00:57:07.880 --> 00:57:10.400
we want to enable
the work that gets

00:57:10.400 --> 00:57:13.490
done to be able to get turned
into companies and things

00:57:13.490 --> 00:57:16.070
that other people
go take and run

00:57:16.070 --> 00:57:20.002
towards building
ultimately therapeutics.

00:57:20.002 --> 00:57:20.960
So that's another zone.

00:57:20.960 --> 00:57:22.970
But that's not a thing
that we're going to do.

00:57:22.970 --> 00:57:24.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

00:57:24.380 --> 00:57:26.630
I gather you're both optimists.

00:57:26.630 --> 00:57:27.260
Yeah?

00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:30.210
Is that part of what
brought you together?

00:57:30.210 --> 00:57:32.390
Forgive me for switching
to a personal question.

00:57:32.390 --> 00:57:35.000
But I love the
optimism that seems

00:57:35.000 --> 00:57:36.523
to sit at the root of the CZI.

00:57:36.523 --> 00:57:37.940
PRISCILLA CHAN: I
will say that we

00:57:37.940 --> 00:57:40.280
are incredibly hopeful people.

00:57:40.280 --> 00:57:44.450
But it manifests in different
ways between the two of us.

00:57:44.450 --> 00:57:46.137
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

00:57:46.137 --> 00:57:47.720
PRISCILLA CHAN: How
would you describe

00:57:47.720 --> 00:57:49.670
your optimism versus mine?

00:57:49.670 --> 00:57:52.445
It's not a loaded question.

00:57:52.445 --> 00:57:53.695
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I don't know.

00:57:58.057 --> 00:57:58.557
Huh.

00:58:02.740 --> 00:58:05.470
I mean, I think I'm more
probably technologically

00:58:05.470 --> 00:58:08.000
optimistic about
what can be built.

00:58:08.000 --> 00:58:15.430
And I think you, because of
your focus as an actual doctor,

00:58:15.430 --> 00:58:18.790
have more of a
sense of how that's

00:58:18.790 --> 00:58:22.660
going to affect actual
people in their lives,

00:58:22.660 --> 00:58:24.580
whereas, for me, it's like--

00:58:24.580 --> 00:58:28.960
I mean, a lot of my
work is we touch a lot

00:58:28.960 --> 00:58:30.880
of people around the world.

00:58:30.880 --> 00:58:32.480
And the scale is immense.

00:58:32.480 --> 00:58:34.570
And I think, for
you, it's like being

00:58:34.570 --> 00:58:38.630
able to improve the
lives of individuals,

00:58:38.630 --> 00:58:41.560
whether it's students at any of
the schools that you've started

00:58:41.560 --> 00:58:44.143
or any of the stuff that we've
supported through the education

00:58:44.143 --> 00:58:47.830
work, which isn't the
goal here, or just

00:58:47.830 --> 00:58:50.440
being able to improve people's
lives in that way I think

00:58:50.440 --> 00:58:53.710
is the thing that I've seen
be super passionate about.

00:58:53.710 --> 00:58:54.473
I don't know.

00:58:54.473 --> 00:58:56.140
Do you agree with
that characterization?

00:58:56.140 --> 00:58:57.370
I'm trying I'm trying to--

00:58:57.370 --> 00:58:59.037
PRISCILLA CHAN: Yeah,
I agree with that.

00:58:59.037 --> 00:59:00.790
I think that's very fair.

00:59:00.790 --> 00:59:02.840
And I'm sort of
giggling to myself

00:59:02.840 --> 00:59:06.740
because in day-to-day
life, as life partners,

00:59:06.740 --> 00:59:09.350
our relative optimism
comes through

00:59:09.350 --> 00:59:14.120
as Mark just is overly
optimistic about his time

00:59:14.120 --> 00:59:17.192
management and will get
engrossed in interesting ideas.

00:59:17.192 --> 00:59:18.275
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I'm late.

00:59:18.275 --> 00:59:19.708
PRISCILLA CHAN: And he's late.

00:59:19.708 --> 00:59:21.875
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Physicians
are very punctual, yeah.

00:59:21.875 --> 00:59:23.458
PRISCILLA CHAN: And
because he's late,

00:59:23.458 --> 00:59:26.360
I have to channel Mark
is an optimist whenever

00:59:26.360 --> 00:59:27.200
I'm waiting for him.

00:59:27.200 --> 00:59:29.033
MARK ZUCKERBERG: That's
such a nice way of--

00:59:29.033 --> 00:59:30.320
OK, I'll start using that.

00:59:30.320 --> 00:59:31.250
PRISCILLA CHAN:
That's what I think

00:59:31.250 --> 00:59:33.542
when I'm in the driveway with
the kids waiting for you.

00:59:33.542 --> 00:59:36.000
I'm like, Mark is an optimist.

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:39.920
And so his optimism
translates to some tardiness,

00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:45.607
whereas I'm a how is this
going to happen like.

00:59:45.607 --> 00:59:46.940
I'm going to open a spreadsheet.

00:59:46.940 --> 00:59:49.130
I'm going to start
putting together a plan

00:59:49.130 --> 00:59:51.410
and pulling together
all the pieces,

00:59:51.410 --> 00:59:55.503
calling people to bring
something to life.

00:59:55.503 --> 00:59:57.920
MARK ZUCKERBERG: But it is one
of my favorite quotes, that

00:59:57.920 --> 01:00:01.340
is optimists tend
to be successful

01:00:01.340 --> 01:00:03.940
and pessimists tend to be right.

01:00:03.940 --> 01:00:06.550
And yeah, I mean, I
think it's true in a lot

01:00:06.550 --> 01:00:09.308
of different aspects of life.

01:00:09.308 --> 01:00:10.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Who said that?

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:11.860
Did you say that,
Mark Zuckerberg?

01:00:11.860 --> 01:00:13.030
MARK ZUCKERBERG: No, I did not.

01:00:13.030 --> 01:00:13.650
PRISCILLA CHAN: Absolutely not.

01:00:13.650 --> 01:00:14.890
MARK ZUCKERBERG: No, no, no.

01:00:14.890 --> 01:00:15.430
I like it.

01:00:15.430 --> 01:00:17.050
I did not invent it.

01:00:17.050 --> 01:00:17.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
We'll give it to you.

01:00:17.840 --> 01:00:18.400
We'll put it out there.

01:00:18.400 --> 01:00:19.660
MARK ZUCKERBERG: No, no, no.

01:00:19.660 --> 01:00:21.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Just
kidding, just kidding.

01:00:21.010 --> 01:00:22.690
MARK ZUCKERBERG: But I do
think that there's really

01:00:22.690 --> 01:00:23.648
something to it, right?

01:00:23.648 --> 01:00:26.110
I mean, if you're
discussing any idea,

01:00:26.110 --> 01:00:28.395
there's all these reasons
why it might not work.

01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:34.060
And those reasons
are probably true.

01:00:34.060 --> 01:00:37.500
The people who are stating them
probably have some validity

01:00:37.500 --> 01:00:38.000
to it.

01:00:38.000 --> 01:00:40.630
But the question is, is that
the most productive way to view

01:00:40.630 --> 01:00:42.640
the world?

01:00:42.640 --> 01:00:44.560
Across the board,
I think the people

01:00:44.560 --> 01:00:46.210
who tend to be the
most productive

01:00:46.210 --> 01:00:47.920
and get the most done--

01:00:47.920 --> 01:00:49.355
you kind of need
to be optimistic

01:00:49.355 --> 01:00:51.730
because if you don't believe
that something can get done,

01:00:51.730 --> 01:00:53.420
then why would
you go work on it?

01:00:53.420 --> 01:00:54.795
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
The reason I ask

01:00:54.795 --> 01:00:58.330
the question is that these days
we hear a lot about the future

01:00:58.330 --> 01:01:00.950
is looking so dark in
these various ways.

01:01:00.950 --> 01:01:02.930
And you have children.

01:01:02.930 --> 01:01:04.160
So you have families.

01:01:04.160 --> 01:01:06.770
And you are a family, excuse me.

01:01:06.770 --> 01:01:09.380
And you also have
families independently

01:01:09.380 --> 01:01:10.490
that are now merged.

01:01:10.490 --> 01:01:14.270
But I love the
optimism behind the CZI

01:01:14.270 --> 01:01:18.620
because, behind
all this, there's

01:01:18.620 --> 01:01:21.020
a set of big
statements on the wall.

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:23.870
One, the future can be
better than the present,

01:01:23.870 --> 01:01:26.990
in terms of treating disease,
maybe even, you said,

01:01:26.990 --> 01:01:29.930
eliminating diseases,
all diseases.

01:01:29.930 --> 01:01:31.940
I love that optimism.

01:01:31.940 --> 01:01:35.210
And there's a tractable
path to do it.

01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:38.480
We're going to put literally
money, and time, and energy,

01:01:38.480 --> 01:01:42.150
and people, and technology
and AI behind that.

01:01:42.150 --> 01:01:47.510
And so I have to ask,
was having children

01:01:47.510 --> 01:01:51.598
a significant modifier in terms
of your view of the future?

01:01:51.598 --> 01:01:53.390
Like wow, you hear all
this doom and gloom.

01:01:53.390 --> 01:01:55.250
What's the future going
to be like for them?

01:01:55.250 --> 01:01:58.400
Did you sit back and
think, what would it

01:01:58.400 --> 01:02:01.097
look like if there was a
future with no diseases?

01:02:01.097 --> 01:02:02.930
Is that the future, we
want our children in?

01:02:02.930 --> 01:02:04.490
I mean, I'm voting a big yes.

01:02:04.490 --> 01:02:06.630
So we're not we're not
going to debate that at all.

01:02:06.630 --> 01:02:09.320
But was having
children an inspiration

01:02:09.320 --> 01:02:11.403
for the CZI in some way?

01:02:11.403 --> 01:02:12.320
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:12.820
So

01:02:12.820 --> 01:02:15.600
PRISCILLA CHAN: I think
my answer to that--

01:02:15.600 --> 01:02:18.530
I would dial backwards for me.

01:02:18.530 --> 01:02:22.010
And I'll just tell a very
brief story about my family.

01:02:22.010 --> 01:02:25.700
I'm the daughter of
Chinese-Vietnamese refugees.

01:02:25.700 --> 01:02:29.270
My parents and grandparents
were boat people,

01:02:29.270 --> 01:02:31.370
if you remember
people left Vietnam

01:02:31.370 --> 01:02:34.910
during the war in these small
boats into the South China Sea.

01:02:34.910 --> 01:02:40.550
And there were stories about
how these boats would sink

01:02:40.550 --> 01:02:41.880
with whole families on them.

01:02:41.880 --> 01:02:43.850
And so my
grandparents, both sets

01:02:43.850 --> 01:02:45.500
of grandparents who
knew each other,

01:02:45.500 --> 01:02:48.980
decided that there was a
better future out there.

01:02:48.980 --> 01:02:51.480
And they were willing
to take risks for it.

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:54.890
But they were afraid of
losing all of their kids.

01:02:54.890 --> 01:02:56.450
My dad is one of six.

01:02:56.450 --> 01:02:57.900
My mom is one of 10.

01:02:57.900 --> 01:03:02.630
And so they decided
that there was something

01:03:02.630 --> 01:03:04.430
out there in this bleak time.

01:03:04.430 --> 01:03:08.090
And they paired up their
kids, one from each family,

01:03:08.090 --> 01:03:10.790
and sent them out on
these little boats

01:03:10.790 --> 01:03:15.930
before the internet, before
cell phones, and just said,

01:03:15.930 --> 01:03:17.515
we'll see you on the other side.

01:03:17.515 --> 01:03:18.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Wow.

01:03:18.390 --> 01:03:20.310
PRISCILLA CHAN:
And the kids were

01:03:20.310 --> 01:03:25.680
between the ages of like
10 to 25, so young kids.

01:03:25.680 --> 01:03:30.300
My mom was a teenager, early
teen when this happened.

01:03:30.300 --> 01:03:32.250
And everyone made it.

01:03:32.250 --> 01:03:34.600
And I get to sit
here and talk to you.

01:03:34.600 --> 01:03:39.190
So how could I not believe
that better is possible?

01:03:39.190 --> 01:03:42.630
And like I hope that that's
in my epigenetics somewhere

01:03:42.630 --> 01:03:44.145
and that I carry on.

01:03:44.145 --> 01:03:46.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That
is a spectacular story.

01:03:46.020 --> 01:03:46.770
PRISCILLA CHAN: Isn't that wild?

01:03:46.770 --> 01:03:47.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It is spectacular.

01:03:47.700 --> 01:03:49.530
PRISCILLA CHAN: How can I
be a pessimist with that?

01:03:49.530 --> 01:03:50.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

01:03:50.430 --> 01:03:52.410
And I so appreciate that
you became a physician

01:03:52.410 --> 01:03:55.290
because you're now
bringing that optimism,

01:03:55.290 --> 01:03:57.768
and that epigenetic
understanding,

01:03:57.768 --> 01:04:00.060
and cognitive understanding
and emotional understanding

01:04:00.060 --> 01:04:01.620
to the field of medicine.

01:04:01.620 --> 01:04:05.005
So I'm grateful to the people
that made that decision.

01:04:05.005 --> 01:04:05.880
PRISCILLA CHAN: Yeah.

01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:10.200
I've always known that story.

01:04:10.200 --> 01:04:13.110
But you don't understand
how wild that feels

01:04:13.110 --> 01:04:14.610
until you have your own child.

01:04:14.610 --> 01:04:16.440
And you're like,
well, I can't even--

01:04:16.440 --> 01:04:21.090
I refuse to let her use glass
bottles only or something

01:04:21.090 --> 01:04:21.660
like that.

01:04:21.660 --> 01:04:26.220
And you're like, oh my God,
the risk and the willingness

01:04:26.220 --> 01:04:30.240
of my grandparents to believe
in something bigger and better

01:04:30.240 --> 01:04:32.350
is just astounding.

01:04:32.350 --> 01:04:36.127
And our own children give
it a sense of urgency.

01:04:36.127 --> 01:04:37.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Again,
a spectacular story.

01:04:37.960 --> 01:04:40.827
And you're sending knowledge
out into the fields of science

01:04:40.827 --> 01:04:42.910
and bringing knowledge
into the fields of science.

01:04:42.910 --> 01:04:44.050
And I love this.

01:04:44.050 --> 01:04:45.820
We'll see you on the other side.

01:04:45.820 --> 01:04:50.050
I'm confident that it
will all come back.

01:04:50.050 --> 01:04:53.593
Well, thank you
so much for that.

01:04:53.593 --> 01:04:55.510
Mark, you have the
opportunity to talk about--

01:04:55.510 --> 01:04:57.670
did having kids
change your worldview?

01:04:57.670 --> 01:04:59.990
MARK ZUCKERBERG: It's really
tough to beat that story.

01:04:59.990 --> 01:05:01.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It is
tough to beat that story.

01:05:01.570 --> 01:05:03.080
And they are also your children.

01:05:03.080 --> 01:05:08.535
So in this case, you get two for
the price of one, so to speak.

01:05:08.535 --> 01:05:10.660
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Having
children definitely changes

01:05:10.660 --> 01:05:11.840
your time horizon.

01:05:11.840 --> 01:05:14.080
So I think that
that's one thing.

01:05:14.080 --> 01:05:17.003
There are all these things that
I think we had talked about,

01:05:17.003 --> 01:05:19.420
for as long as we've known
each other, that you eventually

01:05:19.420 --> 01:05:20.070
want to go do.

01:05:20.070 --> 01:05:21.820
But then it's like,
oh, we're having kids.

01:05:21.820 --> 01:05:23.740
We need to get on this, right?

01:05:23.740 --> 01:05:24.880
So I think that there's--

01:05:24.880 --> 01:05:25.600
PRISCILLA CHAN:
That was actually

01:05:25.600 --> 01:05:28.510
one of the checklists, the baby
checklist before the first.

01:05:28.510 --> 01:05:30.510
MARK ZUCKERBERG: It was
like, the baby's coming.

01:05:30.510 --> 01:05:32.153
We have to start CZI.

01:05:32.153 --> 01:05:33.070
PRISCILLA CHAN: Truly.

01:05:33.070 --> 01:05:35.380
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I'm like
sitting in the hospital

01:05:35.380 --> 01:05:39.280
delivery room finishing
editing the letter that we

01:05:39.280 --> 01:05:41.313
were going to publish
to announce the work.

01:05:41.313 --> 01:05:43.730
PRISCILLA CHAN: Some people
think that is an exaggeration.

01:05:43.730 --> 01:05:44.530
It was not.

01:05:44.530 --> 01:05:46.895
We really were editing
the final draft.

01:05:46.895 --> 01:05:48.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Birthed CZI before you

01:05:48.520 --> 01:05:50.890
birthed the human child.

01:05:50.890 --> 01:05:53.260
Well, it's an
incredible Initiative.

01:05:53.260 --> 01:05:56.860
I've been following it
since its inception.

01:05:56.860 --> 01:06:00.220
And it's already been
tremendously successful.

01:06:00.220 --> 01:06:02.230
And everyone in the
field of science--

01:06:02.230 --> 01:06:03.550
and I have a lot of
communication with those

01:06:03.550 --> 01:06:04.090
folks--

01:06:04.090 --> 01:06:04.960
feels the same way.

01:06:04.960 --> 01:06:07.630
And the future is even
brighter for it, it's clear.

01:06:07.630 --> 01:06:10.210
And thank you for expanding
to the Midwest and New York.

01:06:10.210 --> 01:06:14.680
And we're all very excited to
see where all of this goes.

01:06:14.680 --> 01:06:16.600
I share in your optimism.

01:06:16.600 --> 01:06:18.260
And thank you for
your time today.

01:06:18.260 --> 01:06:19.330
PRISCILLA CHAN: Yeah, thank you.

01:06:19.330 --> 01:06:20.455
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Thank you.

01:06:20.455 --> 01:06:21.163
A lot more to do.

01:06:21.163 --> 01:06:23.122
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd like
to take a quick break

01:06:23.122 --> 01:06:24.820
and thank our sponsor,
InsideTracker.

01:06:24.820 --> 01:06:27.460
InsideTracker is a
personalized nutrition platform

01:06:27.460 --> 01:06:30.010
that analyzes data
from your blood and DNA

01:06:30.010 --> 01:06:31.780
to help you better
