is that you get information

01:06:47.380 --> 01:06:49.900
back about metabolic
factors, lipids, and hormones

01:06:49.900 --> 01:06:50.443
and so forth.

01:06:50.443 --> 01:06:52.610
But you don't know what to
do with that information.

01:06:52.610 --> 01:06:54.890
With InsideTracker,
they make it very easy

01:06:54.890 --> 01:06:57.050
because they have a
personalized platform that

01:06:57.050 --> 01:06:59.420
allows you to see the
levels of all those things--

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:01.760
metabolic factors, lipids,
hormones, et cetera.

01:07:01.760 --> 01:07:03.830
But it gives you
specific directives

01:07:03.830 --> 01:07:05.990
that you can follow that
relate to nutrition,

01:07:05.990 --> 01:07:07.770
behavioral modification,
supplements,

01:07:07.770 --> 01:07:09.200
et cetera that
can help you bring

01:07:09.200 --> 01:07:12.030
those numbers into the ranges
that are optimal for you.

01:07:12.030 --> 01:07:13.490
If you'd like to
try InsideTracker,

01:07:13.490 --> 01:07:16.580
you can go to
insidetracker.com/huberman

01:07:16.580 --> 01:07:19.470
to get 20% off any of
InsideTracker's plans.

01:07:19.470 --> 01:07:22.640
Again, that's
insidetracker.com/huberman.

01:07:22.640 --> 01:07:26.000
And now for my discussion
with Mark Zuckerberg.

01:07:26.000 --> 01:07:28.460
Slight shift of topic here--

01:07:28.460 --> 01:07:30.950
you're extremely
well-known for your role

01:07:30.950 --> 01:07:32.360
in technology development.

01:07:32.360 --> 01:07:35.570
But by virtue of your
personal interests

01:07:35.570 --> 01:07:39.590
and also where Meta
technology interfaces

01:07:39.590 --> 01:07:41.510
with mental health
and physical health,

01:07:41.510 --> 01:07:45.090
you're starting to become
synonymous with health,

01:07:45.090 --> 01:07:47.030
whether you realize it or not.

01:07:47.030 --> 01:07:50.210
Part of that is because
there's posts, footage

01:07:50.210 --> 01:07:51.830
of you rolling jiu jitsu.

01:07:51.830 --> 01:07:54.680
You won a jiu jitsu
competition recently.

01:07:54.680 --> 01:07:59.010
You're doing other forms of
martial arts, water sports,

01:07:59.010 --> 01:08:02.340
including surfing,
and on and on.

01:08:02.340 --> 01:08:05.090
So you're doing it yourself.

01:08:05.090 --> 01:08:08.270
But maybe we could just
start off with technology

01:08:08.270 --> 01:08:12.830
and get this issue out
of the way first, which

01:08:12.830 --> 01:08:16.620
is that I think many people
assume that technology,

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:19.430
especially technology that
involves a screen, excuse

01:08:19.430 --> 01:08:23.250
me, of any kind is going to
be detrimental to our health.

01:08:23.250 --> 01:08:26.790
But that doesn't necessarily
have to be the case.

01:08:26.790 --> 01:08:31.189
So could you explain
how you see technology

01:08:31.189 --> 01:08:35.000
meshing with, inhibiting,
or maybe even promoting

01:08:35.000 --> 01:08:36.873
physical and mental health?

01:08:36.873 --> 01:08:37.790
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Sure.

01:08:37.790 --> 01:08:43.430
I mean, I think this is
a really important topic.

01:08:43.430 --> 01:08:47.840
The research that we've
done suggests that it's not

01:08:47.840 --> 01:08:49.229
all good or all bad.

01:08:49.229 --> 01:08:51.470
I think how you're
using the technology has

01:08:51.470 --> 01:08:54.560
a big impact on
whether it is basically

01:08:54.560 --> 01:08:56.189
a positive experience for you.

01:08:56.189 --> 01:08:59.510
And even within technology,
even within social media,

01:08:59.510 --> 01:09:02.510
there's not one type of
thing that people do.

01:09:02.510 --> 01:09:08.000
I think, at its best, you're
forming meaningful connections

01:09:08.000 --> 01:09:09.950
with other people.

01:09:09.950 --> 01:09:12.439
And there's a lot of research
that basically suggests

01:09:12.439 --> 01:09:15.529
that it's the
relationships that we have

01:09:15.529 --> 01:09:21.210
and the friendships that bring
the most happiness in our lives

01:09:21.210 --> 01:09:23.510
and, at some level,
end up even correlating

01:09:23.510 --> 01:09:25.220
with living a longer
and healthier life

01:09:25.220 --> 01:09:28.354
because that grounding
that you have in community

01:09:28.354 --> 01:09:29.729
ends up being
important for that.

01:09:29.729 --> 01:09:32.729
So I think that aspect
of social media,

01:09:32.729 --> 01:09:37.303
which is the ability to connect
with people, to understand

01:09:37.303 --> 01:09:38.720
what's going on
in people's lives,

01:09:38.720 --> 01:09:40.970
have empathy for them,
communicate what's

01:09:40.970 --> 01:09:43.850
going on with your life,
express that, that's

01:09:43.850 --> 01:09:44.930
generally positive.

01:09:44.930 --> 01:09:47.390
There are ways that
it can be negative,

01:09:47.390 --> 01:09:50.507
in terms of bad interactions,
things like bullying,

01:09:50.507 --> 01:09:52.840
which we can talk about because
there's a lot that we've

01:09:52.840 --> 01:09:55.720
done to basically make sure that
people can be safe from that

01:09:55.720 --> 01:09:58.540
and give people tools and
give kids the ability to have

01:09:58.540 --> 01:09:59.800
the right parental controls.

01:09:59.800 --> 01:10:01.180
Their parents can oversee that.

01:10:01.180 --> 01:10:04.270
But that's the interacting
with people side.

01:10:04.270 --> 01:10:06.950
There's another
side of all of this,

01:10:06.950 --> 01:10:11.740
which I think of as just
passive consumption, which,

01:10:11.740 --> 01:10:15.160
at its best, is entertainment.

01:10:15.160 --> 01:10:17.800
And entertainment is an
important human thing, too.

01:10:17.800 --> 01:10:22.300
But I don't think that that
has quite the same association

01:10:22.300 --> 01:10:27.280
with the long-term well-being
and health benefits

01:10:27.280 --> 01:10:31.330
as being able to help people
connect with other people does.

01:10:31.330 --> 01:10:39.270
And I think, at its worst, some
of the stuff we see online--

01:10:39.270 --> 01:10:41.100
I think, these days,
a lot of the news

01:10:41.100 --> 01:10:44.340
is just so relentlessly
negative that it's just

01:10:44.340 --> 01:10:46.950
hard to come away
from an experience

01:10:46.950 --> 01:10:50.610
where looking at the
news for half an hour

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:52.870
and feel better about the world.

01:10:52.870 --> 01:10:57.340
So I think that
there's a mix on this.

01:10:57.340 --> 01:10:59.020
I think the more
that social media

01:10:59.020 --> 01:11:03.310
is about connecting
with people and the more

01:11:03.310 --> 01:11:07.720
that when you're consuming
and using the media

01:11:07.720 --> 01:11:11.380
part of social media to
learn about things that

01:11:11.380 --> 01:11:15.520
enrich you and can provide
inspiration or education as

01:11:15.520 --> 01:11:18.700
opposed to things that
just leave you with a more

01:11:18.700 --> 01:11:23.380
toxic feeling, that's the
balance that we try to get

01:11:23.380 --> 01:11:24.620
right across our products.

01:11:24.620 --> 01:11:27.010
And I think we're pretty
aligned with the community

01:11:27.010 --> 01:11:28.930
because, at the end of
the day, I mean, people

01:11:28.930 --> 01:11:32.380
don't want to use a product
and come away feeling bad.

01:11:32.380 --> 01:11:36.032
There's a lot that
people talk about--

01:11:36.032 --> 01:11:37.740
evaluate a lot of
these products in terms

01:11:37.740 --> 01:11:39.540
of information and utility.

01:11:39.540 --> 01:11:41.370
But I think it's
as important, when

01:11:41.370 --> 01:11:43.320
you're designing a
product, to think

01:11:43.320 --> 01:11:45.690
about what kind of
feeling you're creating

01:11:45.690 --> 01:11:48.300
with the people who
use it, whether that's

01:11:48.300 --> 01:11:50.580
an aesthetic sense when
you're designing hardware,

01:11:50.580 --> 01:11:54.180
or just what do you
make people feel.

01:11:54.180 --> 01:11:58.740
And generally, people don't
want to feel bad, right?

01:11:58.740 --> 01:12:00.820
That doesn't mean that
we want to shelter people

01:12:00.820 --> 01:12:02.820
from bad things that are
happening in the world.

01:12:02.820 --> 01:12:05.790
But I don't really think that--

01:12:05.790 --> 01:12:09.150
it's not what people
want for us to just

01:12:09.150 --> 01:12:14.917
be just showing all this super
negative stuff all day long.

01:12:14.917 --> 01:12:17.250
So we work hard on all these
different problems-- making

01:12:17.250 --> 01:12:20.140
sure that we're helping connect
people as best as possible,

01:12:20.140 --> 01:12:23.288
helping make sure that
we give people good tools

01:12:23.288 --> 01:12:25.080
to block people who
might be bullying them,

01:12:25.080 --> 01:12:28.020
or harass them, or
especially for younger folks,

01:12:28.020 --> 01:12:30.450
anyone under the age of 16
defaults into an experience

01:12:30.450 --> 01:12:32.040
where their
experience is private.

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:34.260
We have all these
parental tools.

01:12:34.260 --> 01:12:38.920
So that way, parents can
understand what their children

01:12:38.920 --> 01:12:41.500
are up to in a good balance.

01:12:41.500 --> 01:12:43.600
And then on the
other side, we try

01:12:43.600 --> 01:12:45.700
to give people tools
to understand how

01:12:45.700 --> 01:12:47.440
they're spending their time.

01:12:47.440 --> 01:12:51.040
We try to give people tools
so that if you're a teen

01:12:51.040 --> 01:12:55.022
and you're stuck in some
loop of just looking

01:12:55.022 --> 01:12:57.230
at one type of content,
we'll nudge you and say, hey,

01:12:57.230 --> 01:12:59.620
you've been looking at content
of this type for a while.

01:12:59.620 --> 01:13:00.662
How about something else?

01:13:00.662 --> 01:13:02.450
And here's a bunch
of other examples.

01:13:02.450 --> 01:13:03.670
So I think that there
are things that you

01:13:03.670 --> 01:13:05.590
can do to push this in
a positive direction.

01:13:05.590 --> 01:13:07.780
But I think it just
starts with having

01:13:07.780 --> 01:13:11.650
a more nuanced view of this
isn't all good or all bad.

01:13:11.650 --> 01:13:14.020
And the more that you
can make it a positive

01:13:14.020 --> 01:13:16.120
thing, the better this
will be for all the people

01:13:16.120 --> 01:13:17.530
who use our products.

01:13:17.530 --> 01:13:19.447
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That
makes really good sense.

01:13:19.447 --> 01:13:21.470
In terms of the negative
experience, I agree.

01:13:21.470 --> 01:13:23.560
I don't think anyone wants
a negative experience

01:13:23.560 --> 01:13:24.310
in the moment.

01:13:24.310 --> 01:13:26.960
I think where some people
get concerned perhaps--

01:13:26.960 --> 01:13:28.960
and I think about my own
interactions with, say,

01:13:28.960 --> 01:13:32.470
Instagram, which I use all the
time for getting information

01:13:32.470 --> 01:13:34.270
out, but also
consuming information.

01:13:34.270 --> 01:13:35.270
And I happen to love it.

01:13:35.270 --> 01:13:36.820
It's where I
essentially launched

01:13:36.820 --> 01:13:41.660
the non-podcast segment of
my podcast and continue to.

01:13:41.660 --> 01:13:44.030
I can think of experiences
that are a little bit

01:13:44.030 --> 01:13:46.670
like highly
processed food, where

01:13:46.670 --> 01:13:48.500
it tastes good at the time.

01:13:48.500 --> 01:13:50.870
It's highly engrossing.

01:13:50.870 --> 01:13:53.180
But it it's not
necessarily nutritious.

01:13:53.180 --> 01:13:55.950
And you don't feel
very good afterwards.

01:13:55.950 --> 01:13:59.420
So for me, that would
be the little collage

01:13:59.420 --> 01:14:01.917
of default options to
click on in Instagram.

01:14:01.917 --> 01:14:03.500
Occasionally, I
notice-- and this just

01:14:03.500 --> 01:14:07.340
reflects my failure, not
Instagram's, that there

01:14:07.340 --> 01:14:10.070
are a lot of street
fight things,

01:14:10.070 --> 01:14:12.170
like people beating
people up on the street.

01:14:12.170 --> 01:14:15.680
And I have to say, these have a
very strong gravitational pull.

01:14:15.680 --> 01:14:18.090
I'm not somebody that enjoys
seeing violence, per se.

01:14:18.090 --> 01:14:19.692
But you know I find myself--

01:14:19.692 --> 01:14:21.650
I'll click on one of
these, like what happened?

01:14:21.650 --> 01:14:23.630
And I'll see someone get hit.

01:14:23.630 --> 01:14:26.130
And there's a little melee
on the street or something.

01:14:26.130 --> 01:14:28.340
And those seem to be
offered to me a lot lately.

01:14:28.340 --> 01:14:30.290
And again, this is
my fault. It reflects

01:14:30.290 --> 01:14:32.310
my prior searching experience.

01:14:32.310 --> 01:14:37.630
But I noticed that it has a bit
of a gravitational pull, where

01:14:37.630 --> 01:14:39.310
I didn't learn anything.

01:14:39.310 --> 01:14:43.240
It's not teaching me any
useful street self-defense

01:14:43.240 --> 01:14:45.430
skills of any kind.

01:14:45.430 --> 01:14:49.060
And at the same time,
I also really enjoy

01:14:49.060 --> 01:14:50.845
some of the cute animal stuff.

01:14:50.845 --> 01:14:52.220
And so I get a
lot of those also.

01:14:52.220 --> 01:14:54.580
So there's this
polarized collage

01:14:54.580 --> 01:14:56.830
that's offered to me that
reflects my prior search

01:14:56.830 --> 01:14:58.240
behavior.

01:14:58.240 --> 01:15:01.750
You could argue that the
cute animal stuff is just

01:15:01.750 --> 01:15:02.620
entertainment.

01:15:02.620 --> 01:15:04.990
But actually, it fills
me with a feeling,

01:15:04.990 --> 01:15:06.790
in some cases, that
truly delights me.

01:15:06.790 --> 01:15:07.665
I delight in animals.

01:15:07.665 --> 01:15:09.373
And we're not just
talking about kittens.

01:15:09.373 --> 01:15:11.080
I mean, animals I've
never seen before,

01:15:11.080 --> 01:15:12.700
interactions between
animals I've never seen

01:15:12.700 --> 01:15:13.990
before that truly delight me.

01:15:13.990 --> 01:15:15.430
They energize me
in a positive way

01:15:15.430 --> 01:15:19.010
that when I leave Instagram,
I do think I'm better off.

01:15:19.010 --> 01:15:21.410
So I'm grateful for the
algorithm in that sense.

01:15:21.410 --> 01:15:26.290
But I guess, the direct question
is, is the algorithm just

01:15:26.290 --> 01:15:29.080
reflective of what one
has been looking at a lot

01:15:29.080 --> 01:15:31.330
prior to that moment
where they log on?

01:15:31.330 --> 01:15:35.240
Or is it also trying to do
exactly what you described,

01:15:35.240 --> 01:15:38.540
which is trying to give people
a good-feeling experience that

01:15:38.540 --> 01:15:40.443
leads to more good feelings?

01:15:40.443 --> 01:15:41.360
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:15:41.360 --> 01:15:44.270
I mean, I think we try to
do this in a long-term way.

01:15:44.270 --> 01:15:46.550
I think one simple
example of this

01:15:46.550 --> 01:15:49.760
is we had this issue
a number of years back

01:15:49.760 --> 01:15:52.760
about clickbait
news, so articles

01:15:52.760 --> 01:15:57.913
that would have basically
a headline that grabbed

01:15:57.913 --> 01:15:59.330
your attention,
that made you feel

01:15:59.330 --> 01:16:00.450
like, oh, I need
to click on this.

01:16:00.450 --> 01:16:01.492
And then you click on it.

01:16:01.492 --> 01:16:03.890
And then the article is
actually about something that's

01:16:03.890 --> 01:16:05.850
somewhat tangential to it.

01:16:05.850 --> 01:16:07.790
But people clicked on it.

01:16:07.790 --> 01:16:11.028
So the naive version of this
stuff, the 10-year-old version

01:16:11.028 --> 01:16:13.070
was like, oh, people seem
to be clicking on this.

01:16:13.070 --> 01:16:14.030
Maybe that's good.

01:16:14.030 --> 01:16:16.940
But it's actually a pretty
straightforward exercise

01:16:16.940 --> 01:16:19.730
to instrument the system to
realize that, hey, people

01:16:19.730 --> 01:16:23.210
click on this, and
then they don't really

01:16:23.210 --> 01:16:27.360
spend a lot of time reading
the news after clicking on it.

01:16:27.360 --> 01:16:30.260
And after they do
this a few times,

01:16:30.260 --> 01:16:34.070
it doesn't really correlate
with them saying that they're

01:16:34.070 --> 01:16:37.250
having a good experience.

01:16:37.250 --> 01:16:40.070
Some of how we
measure this is just

01:16:40.070 --> 01:16:42.110
by looking at how
people use the services.

01:16:42.110 --> 01:16:44.240
But I think it's also
important to balance

01:16:44.240 --> 01:16:48.140
that by having real people
come in and tell us,

01:16:48.140 --> 01:16:50.600
OK-- we show them, here are
the stories that we could have

01:16:50.600 --> 01:16:54.980
showed you, which of these
are most meaningful to you,

01:16:54.980 --> 01:16:57.230
or would make it so that you
have the best experience,

01:16:57.230 --> 01:17:00.338
and just mapping the
algorithm and what

01:17:00.338 --> 01:17:02.880
we do to that ground truth of
what people say that they want.

01:17:02.880 --> 01:17:05.960
So I think that, through
a set of things like that,

01:17:05.960 --> 01:17:09.500
we really have made large
steps to minimize things

01:17:09.500 --> 01:17:10.700
like clickbait over time.

01:17:10.700 --> 01:17:11.930
It's not like gone
from the internet.

01:17:11.930 --> 01:17:14.090
But I think we've done a
good job of minimizing it

01:17:14.090 --> 01:17:16.130
on our services.

01:17:16.130 --> 01:17:18.332
Within that though,
I do think that we

01:17:18.332 --> 01:17:19.790
need to be pretty
careful about not

01:17:19.790 --> 01:17:22.760
being paternalistic about what
makes different people feel

01:17:22.760 --> 01:17:23.690
good.

01:17:23.690 --> 01:17:25.520
So I mean, I don't
know that everyone

01:17:25.520 --> 01:17:27.662
feels good about cute animals.

01:17:27.662 --> 01:17:29.120
I mean, I can't
imagine that people

01:17:29.120 --> 01:17:30.470
would feel really bad about it.

01:17:30.470 --> 01:17:33.440
But maybe they don't have as
profound of a positive reaction

01:17:33.440 --> 01:17:36.530
to it as you just expressed.

01:17:36.530 --> 01:17:38.300
And I don't know.

01:17:38.300 --> 01:17:40.010
Maybe people who are
more into fighting

01:17:40.010 --> 01:17:41.942
would look at the
street fighting videos--

01:17:41.942 --> 01:17:44.150
assuming that they're within
our community standards.

01:17:44.150 --> 01:17:45.470
I think that there's
a level of violence

01:17:45.470 --> 01:17:47.345
that we just don't want
to be showing at all.

01:17:47.345 --> 01:17:49.790
But that's a separate question.

01:17:49.790 --> 01:17:51.860
But if they are, I
mean, then it's like--

01:17:51.860 --> 01:17:53.417
I mean, I'm pretty into MMA.

01:17:53.417 --> 01:17:55.250
I don't get a lot of
street fighting videos.

01:17:55.250 --> 01:17:57.667
But if I did, maybe I'd feel
like I was learning something

01:17:57.667 --> 01:17:59.780
from that.

01:17:59.780 --> 01:18:02.840
I think at various times
in the company's history,

01:18:02.840 --> 01:18:06.980
we've been a little bit too
paternalistic about saying,

01:18:06.980 --> 01:18:11.276
this is good content, this
is bad, you should like this,

01:18:11.276 --> 01:18:13.110
this is unhealthy for you.

01:18:13.110 --> 01:18:17.330
And I think that we want to
look at the long-term effects.

01:18:17.330 --> 01:18:19.160
You don't want to get
stuck in a short term

01:18:19.160 --> 01:18:20.330
loop of like, OK,
just because you

01:18:20.330 --> 01:18:22.130
did this today doesn't
mean it's what you

01:18:22.130 --> 01:18:23.810
aspire for yourself over time.

01:18:23.810 --> 01:18:28.280
But I think, as long as you
look at the long-term of what

01:18:28.280 --> 01:18:31.910
people both say they want and
what they do, giving people

01:18:31.910 --> 01:18:35.000
a fair amount of latitude to
like the things that they like,

01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:37.430
I just think feels like
the right set of values

01:18:37.430 --> 01:18:39.052
to bring to this.

01:18:39.052 --> 01:18:41.010
Now, of course, that
doesn't go for everything.

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:45.700
There are things that are truly
off limits and things that--

01:18:45.700 --> 01:18:49.415
like bullying, for example, or
things that are really inciting

01:18:49.415 --> 01:18:50.540
violence, things like that.

01:18:50.540 --> 01:18:52.130
I mean, we have the
whole community standards

01:18:52.130 --> 01:18:52.820
around this.

01:18:52.820 --> 01:18:55.760
But I think, except
for those things

01:18:55.760 --> 01:18:58.400
which I would hope that
most people can agree, OK,

01:18:58.400 --> 01:18:59.330
bullying is bad--

01:18:59.330 --> 01:19:02.900
I hope that 100% of
people agree with that.

01:19:02.900 --> 01:19:06.110
And not 100%, maybe 99%.

01:19:06.110 --> 01:19:11.610
Except for the things that
kind of get that very--

01:19:11.610 --> 01:19:13.808
that feel pretty extreme
and bad like that,

01:19:13.808 --> 01:19:15.350
I think you want to
give people space

01:19:15.350 --> 01:19:17.468
to like what they want to like.

01:19:17.468 --> 01:19:20.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yesterday, I
had the very good experience

01:19:20.390 --> 01:19:23.810
of learning from the Meta team
about safety protections that

01:19:23.810 --> 01:19:28.370
are in place for kids who
are using Meta Platforms.

01:19:28.370 --> 01:19:32.720
And frankly, I was really
positively surprised

01:19:32.720 --> 01:19:37.640
at the huge number of
filter-based tools and just

01:19:37.640 --> 01:19:41.480
ability to customize the
experience so that it can stand

01:19:41.480 --> 01:19:44.390
the best chance of enriching--
not just remaining neutral,

01:19:44.390 --> 01:19:48.020
but enriching their
mental health status.

01:19:48.020 --> 01:19:50.540
One thing that came about
in that conversation,

01:19:50.540 --> 01:19:54.200
however, was I realized
there are all these tools.

01:19:54.200 --> 01:19:56.485
But do people really know
that these tools exist?

01:19:56.485 --> 01:19:58.610
And I think about my own
experience with Instagram.

01:19:58.610 --> 01:20:03.560
I love watching Adam Mosseri's
Friday Q&amp;As because he explains

01:20:03.560 --> 01:20:08.490
a lot of the tools that
I didn't know existed.

01:20:08.490 --> 01:20:10.370
And if people haven't
seen that, I highly

01:20:10.370 --> 01:20:12.440
recommend they watch that.

01:20:12.440 --> 01:20:14.270
I think he takes
questions on Thursdays

01:20:14.270 --> 01:20:16.880
and answers them
most every Fridays.

01:20:16.880 --> 01:20:21.410
So if I'm not aware of the tools
without watching that, that

01:20:21.410 --> 01:20:24.862
exists for adults,
how does Meta look

01:20:24.862 --> 01:20:27.320
at the challenge of making sure
that people know that there

01:20:27.320 --> 01:20:28.250
are all these tools--

01:20:28.250 --> 01:20:30.560
I mean, dozens and dozens
of very useful tools?

01:20:30.560 --> 01:20:33.980
But I think most of us just
know the hashtag, the tag,

01:20:33.980 --> 01:20:37.370
the click, stories versus feed.

01:20:37.370 --> 01:20:39.080
We now know that--

01:20:39.080 --> 01:20:40.160
I also post to Threads.

01:20:40.160 --> 01:20:42.840
I mean, so we know the
major channels and tools.

01:20:42.840 --> 01:20:44.750
But this is like
owning a vehicle that

01:20:44.750 --> 01:20:47.450
has incredible features
that one doesn't

01:20:47.450 --> 01:20:50.630
realize can take you off road,
can allow your vehicle to fly.

01:20:50.630 --> 01:20:52.820
I mean, there's a lot there.

01:20:52.820 --> 01:20:54.260
So what do you
think could be done

01:20:54.260 --> 01:20:55.190
to get that information out?

01:20:55.190 --> 01:20:57.320
Maybe this conversation could
cue people to [INAUDIBLE]..

01:20:57.320 --> 01:20:59.600
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I mean, that's
part of the reason why I wanted

01:20:59.600 --> 01:21:00.920
to talk to you about this.

01:21:00.920 --> 01:21:04.160
I mean, I think most of the
narrative around social media

01:21:04.160 --> 01:21:06.605
is not, OK, all of
the different tools

01:21:06.605 --> 01:21:08.480
that people have to
control their experience.

01:21:08.480 --> 01:21:12.830
It's the narrative of
is this just negative

01:21:12.830 --> 01:21:14.875
for teens or something.

01:21:14.875 --> 01:21:16.250
And I think, again,
a lot of this

01:21:16.250 --> 01:21:20.615
comes down to how is the
experience being tuned.

01:21:23.150 --> 01:21:25.620
Are people using it to
connect in positive ways?

01:21:25.620 --> 01:21:28.310
And if so, I think
it's really positive.

01:21:28.310 --> 01:21:30.022
So yeah, I mean, I
think part of this

01:21:30.022 --> 01:21:32.480
is we probably just need to
get out and talk to people more

01:21:32.480 --> 01:21:34.070
about it.

01:21:34.070 --> 01:21:36.020
And then there's an
in-product aspect,

01:21:36.020 --> 01:21:39.630
which is if you're a
teen and you sign up,

01:21:39.630 --> 01:21:44.510
we take you through a pretty
extensive experience that

01:21:44.510 --> 01:21:46.160
tries to outline some of this.

01:21:46.160 --> 01:21:48.680
But that has limits, too,
because when you sign up

01:21:48.680 --> 01:21:51.650
for a new thing, if you're
bombarded with here's

01:21:51.650 --> 01:21:54.380
a list of features, you're like,
OK, I just signed up for this.

01:21:54.380 --> 01:21:57.260
I don't really understand much
about what the service is.

01:21:57.260 --> 01:22:00.140
Let me go find some
people to follow

01:22:00.140 --> 01:22:02.180
who are my friends on
here before I learn

01:22:02.180 --> 01:22:07.760
about controls to prevent people
from harassing me or something.

01:22:07.760 --> 01:22:11.870
That's why I think it's
really important to also show

01:22:11.870 --> 01:22:13.770
a bunch of these
tools in context.

01:22:13.770 --> 01:22:16.880
So if you're
looking at comments,

01:22:16.880 --> 01:22:20.670
and if you go to
delete a comment,

01:22:20.670 --> 01:22:24.633
or you go to edit something, try
to give people prompts in line.

01:22:24.633 --> 01:22:26.550
It's like, hey, did that
you can manage things

01:22:26.550 --> 01:22:29.160
in these ways around that?

01:22:29.160 --> 01:22:32.910
Or when you're in the inbox
and you're filtering something,

01:22:32.910 --> 01:22:34.230
remind people in line.

01:22:34.230 --> 01:22:37.110
So just because of
the number of people

01:22:37.110 --> 01:22:39.360
who use the products
and the level of nuance

01:22:39.360 --> 01:22:42.690
around each of the controls,
I think the vast majority

01:22:42.690 --> 01:22:46.980
of that education, I think,
needs to happen in the product.

01:22:46.980 --> 01:22:49.470
But I do think that through
conversations like this

01:22:49.470 --> 01:22:52.900
and others that we
need to be doing,

01:22:52.900 --> 01:22:55.980
I think we can create a broader
awareness that those things

01:22:55.980 --> 01:22:58.200
exist so that way at
least people are primed

01:22:58.200 --> 01:23:00.240
so that way when those things
pop up in the product people,

01:23:00.240 --> 01:23:02.657
they're like, oh yeah, I knew
that there was this control.

01:23:02.657 --> 01:23:05.235
And here's how I would use that.

01:23:05.235 --> 01:23:07.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I find
the restrict function

01:23:07.110 --> 01:23:10.920
to be very useful, more than the
block function in most cases.

01:23:10.920 --> 01:23:12.420
I do sometimes have
to block people.

01:23:12.420 --> 01:23:14.170
But the restrict
function is really useful

01:23:14.170 --> 01:23:17.100
that you could filter
specific comments.

01:23:17.100 --> 01:23:19.620
You might recognize that
someone has a tendency

01:23:19.620 --> 01:23:20.940
to be a little aggressive.

01:23:20.940 --> 01:23:22.710
And I should point out that
I actually don't really

01:23:22.710 --> 01:23:23.770
mind what people say to me.

01:23:23.770 --> 01:23:25.853
But I try and maintain
what I call classroom rules

01:23:25.853 --> 01:23:28.440
in my comment section, where
I don't like people attacking

01:23:28.440 --> 01:23:30.090
other people because I
would never tolerate that

01:23:30.090 --> 01:23:31.140
in the university classroom.

01:23:31.140 --> 01:23:32.820
I'm not going to tolerate
that in the comments section,

01:23:32.820 --> 01:23:33.630
for instance.

01:23:33.630 --> 01:23:34.547
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:23:34.547 --> 01:23:37.590
And I think that the example
that you just used about

01:23:37.590 --> 01:23:40.590
restrict versus block gets to
something about product design

01:23:40.590 --> 01:23:45.870
that's important, too, which
is that block is this very

01:23:45.870 --> 01:23:48.162
powerful tool that if someone
is giving you a hard time

01:23:48.162 --> 01:23:50.495
and you just want them to
disappear from the experience,

01:23:50.495 --> 01:23:51.330
you can do it.

01:23:51.330 --> 01:23:56.700
But the design trade-off with
that is that in order to make

01:23:56.700 --> 01:24:00.780
it so that the person is
just gone from the experience

01:24:00.780 --> 01:24:03.240
and that you don't
show up to them,

01:24:03.240 --> 01:24:05.100
they don't show up to you--

01:24:05.100 --> 01:24:07.680
inherent to that is
that they will have

01:24:07.680 --> 01:24:09.550
a sense that you blocked them.

01:24:09.550 --> 01:24:12.785
And that's why I think some
stuff like restrict or just

01:24:12.785 --> 01:24:14.160
filtering, like
I just don't want

01:24:14.160 --> 01:24:16.650
to see as much stuff
about this topic--

01:24:16.650 --> 01:24:20.440
people like using different
tools for very subtle reasons.

01:24:20.440 --> 01:24:23.580
I mean, maybe you want the
content to not show up,

01:24:23.580 --> 01:24:25.110
but you don't want
the person who's

01:24:25.110 --> 01:24:27.830
posting the content to know that
you don't want it to show up.

01:24:27.830 --> 01:24:30.330
Maybe you don't want to get the
messages in your main inbox,

01:24:30.330 --> 01:24:35.010
but you don't want to tell the
person actually that you're not

01:24:35.010 --> 01:24:36.720
friends or something like that.

01:24:36.720 --> 01:24:39.090
You actually need to give
people different tools that

01:24:39.090 --> 01:24:42.600
have different levels
of power and nuance

01:24:42.600 --> 01:24:45.930
around how the social
dynamics around using them

01:24:45.930 --> 01:24:49.170
play out in order
to really allow

01:24:49.170 --> 01:24:51.630
people to tailor the experience
in the ways that they want.

01:24:51.630 --> 01:24:53.088
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
In terms of trying

01:24:53.088 --> 01:24:57.250
to limit total amount
of time on social media,

01:24:57.250 --> 01:25:01.320
I couldn't find really
good data on this.

01:25:01.320 --> 01:25:02.820
How much time is too much?

01:25:02.820 --> 01:25:04.320
I mean, I think
it's going to depend

01:25:04.320 --> 01:25:07.128
on what one is looking at, the
age of the user, et cetera.

01:25:07.128 --> 01:25:08.170
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I agree.

01:25:08.170 --> 01:25:09.712
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
know that you have

01:25:09.712 --> 01:25:12.750
tools that cue the
user to how long

01:25:12.750 --> 01:25:15.030
they've been on
a given platform.

01:25:15.030 --> 01:25:17.640
Are there tools
to self-regulate--

01:25:17.640 --> 01:25:21.083
I'm thinking about the Greek
myth of the sirens and people

01:25:21.083 --> 01:25:23.250
tying themselves to the
mast and covering their eyes

01:25:23.250 --> 01:25:25.042
so that they're not
drawn in by the sirens.

01:25:25.042 --> 01:25:28.290
Is there a function aside from
deleting the app temporarily

01:25:28.290 --> 01:25:31.560
and then reinstalling it every
time you want to use it again?

01:25:31.560 --> 01:25:34.560
Is there a true lockout,
self-lockout function

01:25:34.560 --> 01:25:37.215
where one can lock themselves
out of access to the app?

01:25:37.215 --> 01:25:39.340
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Well, I
think we give people tools

01:25:39.340 --> 01:25:41.760
that let them manage this.

01:25:41.760 --> 01:25:43.510
And there's the tools
that you get to use.

01:25:43.510 --> 01:25:45.302
And then there's the
tools that the parents

01:25:45.302 --> 01:25:48.610
get to use to basically
see how usage works.

01:25:48.610 --> 01:25:51.460
But yeah, I think that
there's different--

01:25:51.460 --> 01:25:53.170
I think, for now,
we've mostly focused

01:25:53.170 --> 01:25:55.300
on helping people
understand this,

01:25:55.300 --> 01:25:59.380
and then give people reminders
and things like that.

01:25:59.380 --> 01:26:03.580
It's tough, though, to
answer the question that you

01:26:03.580 --> 01:26:04.990
were talking about before.

01:26:04.990 --> 01:26:07.390
Is there an amount of
time which is too much?

01:26:07.390 --> 01:26:09.910
Because it does really
get to what you're doing.

01:26:09.910 --> 01:26:12.490
If you fast forward
beyond just the

01:26:12.490 --> 01:26:15.670
apps that we have today
to an experience that

01:26:15.670 --> 01:26:17.740
is like a social
experience in the future

01:26:17.740 --> 01:26:20.410
of the augmented reality
glasses or something

01:26:20.410 --> 01:26:22.780
that we're building,
a lot of this

01:26:22.780 --> 01:26:27.610
is going to be you're
interacting with people

01:26:27.610 --> 01:26:29.290
in the way that you
would physically

01:26:29.290 --> 01:26:31.390
as if you were like
hanging out with friends

01:26:31.390 --> 01:26:33.370
or working with people.

01:26:33.370 --> 01:26:36.400
But now, they can
show up as holograms.

01:26:36.400 --> 01:26:38.980
And you can feel like you're
present right there with them,

01:26:38.980 --> 01:26:40.790
no matter where
they actually are.

01:26:40.790 --> 01:26:43.240
And the question is,
is there too much

01:26:43.240 --> 01:26:45.610
time to spend interacting
with people like that?

01:26:45.610 --> 01:26:47.860
Well, at the limit,
if we can get

01:26:47.860 --> 01:26:51.250
that experience to be
as rich and giving you

01:26:51.250 --> 01:26:56.080
as good of a sense of presence
as you would have if you were

01:26:56.080 --> 01:26:58.630
physically there
with someone, then I

01:26:58.630 --> 01:27:01.910
don't see why you would want to
restrict the amount that people

01:27:01.910 --> 01:27:05.270
use that technology
to any less than what

01:27:05.270 --> 01:27:08.630
would be the amount of time
that you'd be comfortable

01:27:08.630 --> 01:27:10.687
interacting with
people physically,

01:27:10.687 --> 01:27:12.770
which obviously is not
going to be 24 hours a day.

01:27:12.770 --> 01:27:14.510
You have to do other stuff.

01:27:14.510 --> 01:27:15.470
You have work.

01:27:15.470 --> 01:27:16.340
You need to sleep.

01:27:16.340 --> 01:27:19.430
But I think it really gets to
how you're using these things,

01:27:19.430 --> 01:27:22.460
whereas if what you're
primarily using the services for

01:27:22.460 --> 01:27:27.800
is you're getting stuck in loops
reading news or something that

01:27:27.800 --> 01:27:30.680
is really getting you into
a negative mental state,

01:27:30.680 --> 01:27:32.018
then I don't know.

01:27:32.018 --> 01:27:33.560
I mean, I think that
there's probably

01:27:33.560 --> 01:27:35.030
a relatively short
period of time

01:27:35.030 --> 01:27:38.540
that maybe that's a good thing
that you want to be doing.

01:27:38.540 --> 01:27:40.340
But again, even
then it's not zero

01:27:40.340 --> 01:27:43.388
because just because news
might make you unhappy

01:27:43.388 --> 01:27:44.930
doesn't mean that
the answer is to be

01:27:44.930 --> 01:27:47.388
unaware of negative things that
are happening in the world.

01:27:47.388 --> 01:27:49.040
I just think that
different people

01:27:49.040 --> 01:27:52.160
have different tolerances for
what they can take on that.

01:27:52.160 --> 01:27:54.260
And I think it's
generally having

01:27:54.260 --> 01:27:57.410
some awareness is probably
good, as long as it's not more

01:27:57.410 --> 01:27:59.840
than you're constitutionally
able to take.

01:27:59.840 --> 01:28:01.190
So I don't know.

01:28:01.190 --> 01:28:05.270
I try not be too paternalistic
about this as our approach.

01:28:05.270 --> 01:28:08.030
But we want to empower
people by giving them

01:28:08.030 --> 01:28:12.410
the tools, both people and,
if you're a teen, your parents

01:28:12.410 --> 01:28:15.080
to have tools to understand
what you're experiencing

01:28:15.080 --> 01:28:18.453
and how you're using these
things, and then go from there.

01:28:18.453 --> 01:28:19.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:28:19.370 --> 01:28:22.800
I think it requires of all of us
some degree of self-regulation.

01:28:22.800 --> 01:28:24.800
I like this idea of not
being too paternalistic.

01:28:24.800 --> 01:28:27.090
I mean, it seems like
the right way to go.

01:28:27.090 --> 01:28:29.240
I find myself
occasionally having

01:28:29.240 --> 01:28:31.940
to make sure that I'm not
just passively scrolling,

01:28:31.940 --> 01:28:32.780
that I'm learning.

01:28:32.780 --> 01:28:36.560
I like foraging for, organizing
and dispersing information.

01:28:36.560 --> 01:28:38.450
That's been my life's career.

01:28:38.450 --> 01:28:40.710
So I've learned so
much from social media.

01:28:40.710 --> 01:28:43.650
I find great
papers, great ideas.

01:28:43.650 --> 01:28:45.650
I think comments are a
great source of feedback.

01:28:45.650 --> 01:28:48.025
And I'm not just saying that
because you're sitting here.

01:28:48.025 --> 01:28:50.720
I mean, Instagram in particular,
but other Meta platforms

01:28:50.720 --> 01:28:53.960
have been tremendously
helpful for me to get science

01:28:53.960 --> 01:28:55.460
and health information out.

01:28:55.460 --> 01:28:58.320
One of the things that
I'm really excited about,

01:28:58.320 --> 01:29:01.220
which I only had the chance to
try for the first time today,

01:29:01.220 --> 01:29:05.180
is your new VR platform,
the newest Oculus.

01:29:05.180 --> 01:29:07.880
And then we can talk about
the glasses, the Ray-Bans.

01:29:07.880 --> 01:29:09.177
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Sure.

01:29:09.177 --> 01:29:10.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Those two experiences

01:29:10.760 --> 01:29:12.950
are still kind of blowing
my mind, especially

01:29:12.950 --> 01:29:16.293
the Ray-Ban glasses.

01:29:16.293 --> 01:29:17.960
And I have so many
questions about this.

01:29:17.960 --> 01:29:18.620
So I'll resist.

01:29:18.620 --> 01:29:19.317
But--

01:29:19.317 --> 01:29:20.900
MARK ZUCKERBERG: We
can get into that.

01:29:20.900 --> 01:29:21.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:29:21.170 --> 01:29:23.030
Well, yeah, I have some
experience with VR.

01:29:23.030 --> 01:29:25.070
My Lab has used VR.

01:29:25.070 --> 01:29:27.560
Jeremy Bailenson's
Lab at Stanford

01:29:27.560 --> 01:29:30.638
is one of the pioneering
labs of VR and mixed reality.

01:29:30.638 --> 01:29:32.930
I guess they used to call it
augmented reality, but now

01:29:32.930 --> 01:29:33.930
mixed reality.

01:29:33.930 --> 01:29:36.080
I think what's so
striking about the VR

01:29:36.080 --> 01:29:40.580
that you guys had me try today
is how well it interfaces

01:29:40.580 --> 01:29:43.247
with the real room, let's
call it, the physical room.

01:29:43.247 --> 01:29:44.330
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Physical.

01:29:44.330 --> 01:29:45.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
could still see people.

01:29:45.530 --> 01:29:46.730
I could see where
the furniture was.

01:29:46.730 --> 01:29:48.350
So I wasn't going to
bump into anything.

01:29:48.350 --> 01:29:49.517
I could see people's smiles.

01:29:49.517 --> 01:29:52.490
I could see my
water on the table

01:29:52.490 --> 01:29:57.230
while I was doing this what
felt like a real martial arts

01:29:57.230 --> 01:29:59.570
experience, except I
wasn't getting hit.

01:29:59.570 --> 01:30:01.610
Well, I was getting
hit virtually.

01:30:01.610 --> 01:30:04.430
But it's extremely engaging.

01:30:04.430 --> 01:30:07.040
And yet, on the
good side of things,

01:30:07.040 --> 01:30:10.070
it really bypasses a lot
of the early concerns

01:30:10.070 --> 01:30:11.300
that Bailenson Lab--

01:30:11.300 --> 01:30:14.120
again, Jeremy's Lab-- was
early to say that, oh, there's

01:30:14.120 --> 01:30:18.300
a limit to how much VR one
can or should use each day,

01:30:18.300 --> 01:30:21.290
even for the adult brain
because it can really

01:30:21.290 --> 01:30:24.170
disrupt your vestibular
system, your sense of balance.

01:30:24.170 --> 01:30:25.670
All of that seems
to have been dealt

01:30:25.670 --> 01:30:28.265
with in this new
iteration of VR.

01:30:28.265 --> 01:30:30.140
I didn't come out of it
feeling dizzy at all.

01:30:30.140 --> 01:30:32.348
I didn't feel like I was
reentering the room in a way

01:30:32.348 --> 01:30:33.710
that was really jarring.

01:30:33.710 --> 01:30:35.210
Going into it is
obviously, Whoa,

01:30:35.210 --> 01:30:36.293
this is a different world.

01:30:36.293 --> 01:30:39.842
But you can look to your left
and say, oh, someone just

01:30:39.842 --> 01:30:40.550
came in the door.

01:30:40.550 --> 01:30:41.120
Hey, how's it going?

01:30:41.120 --> 01:30:42.260
Hold on, I'm playing
this game, just

01:30:42.260 --> 01:30:44.420
as it was when I was a
kid playing in Nintendo

01:30:44.420 --> 01:30:45.230
and someone would walk in.

01:30:45.230 --> 01:30:46.100
It's fully engrossing.

01:30:46.100 --> 01:30:46.880
But you'd be like, hold on.

01:30:46.880 --> 01:30:47.963
And you see they're there.

01:30:47.963 --> 01:30:53.300
So first of all,
bravo, incredible.

01:30:53.300 --> 01:30:57.050
And then the next question
is, what do we even

01:30:57.050 --> 01:30:58.100
call this experience?

01:30:58.100 --> 01:30:59.970
Because it is
truly really mixed.

01:30:59.970 --> 01:31:01.663
It's a truly mixed
reality experience.

01:31:01.663 --> 01:31:02.580
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:31:02.580 --> 01:31:05.310
I mean, mixed reality
is the umbrella term

01:31:05.310 --> 01:31:08.280
that refers to the
combined experience

01:31:08.280 --> 01:31:10.300
of virtual and
augmented reality.

01:31:10.300 --> 01:31:13.320
So augmented reality is
what you're eventually

01:31:13.320 --> 01:31:16.440
going to get with some future
version of the smart glasses,

01:31:16.440 --> 01:31:20.430
where you're primarily
seeing the world,

01:31:20.430 --> 01:31:22.230
but you can put holograms in it.

01:31:22.230 --> 01:31:25.887
So we'll have a
future where you're

01:31:25.887 --> 01:31:26.970
going to walk into a room.

01:31:26.970 --> 01:31:29.190
And there are going to
be as many holograms

01:31:29.190 --> 01:31:30.990
as physical objects.

01:31:30.990 --> 01:31:34.710
If you just think about all the
paper, the art, physical games,

01:31:34.710 --> 01:31:36.300
media, your workstation--

01:31:36.300 --> 01:31:37.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If
we refer to, let's

01:31:37.230 --> 01:31:39.780
say, an MMA fight, we could just
draw it up on the table right

01:31:39.780 --> 01:31:41.880
here and just see it repeat
as opposed to us turning

01:31:41.880 --> 01:31:42.730
and looking at a screen.

01:31:42.730 --> 01:31:42.930
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:31:42.930 --> 01:31:44.680
I mean, pretty much
any screen that exists

01:31:44.680 --> 01:31:47.670
could be a hologram in the
future with smart glasses.

01:31:47.670 --> 01:31:49.830
There's nothing that
actually physically needs

01:31:49.830 --> 01:31:51.720
to be there for that
when you have glasses

01:31:51.720 --> 01:31:54.500
that can put a hologram there.

01:31:54.500 --> 01:31:56.250
And it's an interesting
thought experiment

01:31:56.250 --> 01:31:58.625
to just go around and think
about, OK, what of the things

01:31:58.625 --> 01:32:01.692
that are physical in the world
need to actually be physical.

01:32:01.692 --> 01:32:02.650
Your chair does, right?

01:32:02.650 --> 01:32:03.460
Because you're sitting on it.

01:32:03.460 --> 01:32:05.090
A hologram isn't
going to support you.

01:32:05.090 --> 01:32:06.970
But like that art
on the wall, I mean,

01:32:06.970 --> 01:32:10.840
that doesn't need to
physically be there.

01:32:10.840 --> 01:32:15.520
So I think that that's the
augmented reality experience

01:32:15.520 --> 01:32:16.760
that we're moving towards.

01:32:16.760 --> 01:32:20.590
And then we've had these
headsets that historically we

01:32:20.590 --> 01:32:22.120
think about as VR.

01:32:22.120 --> 01:32:26.020
And that has been something
that is like a fully

01:32:26.020 --> 01:32:27.530
immersive experience.

01:32:27.530 --> 01:32:30.100
But now, we're getting
something that's

01:32:30.100 --> 01:32:31.840
a hybrid in between
the two and capable

01:32:31.840 --> 01:32:34.990
of both, which is a headset
that can do both virtual reality

01:32:34.990 --> 01:32:38.180
and some of these augmented
reality experiences.

01:32:38.180 --> 01:32:40.900
And I think that
that's really powerful,

01:32:40.900 --> 01:32:44.920
both because you're going to
get new applications that allow

01:32:44.920 --> 01:32:46.240
people to collaborate together.

01:32:46.240 --> 01:32:48.730
And maybe the two of
us are here physically,

01:32:48.730 --> 01:32:51.700
but someone joins us and
it's their avatar there.

01:32:51.700 --> 01:32:54.460
Or maybe it's some
version in the future.

01:32:54.460 --> 01:32:55.980
You're having a team meeting.

01:32:55.980 --> 01:32:57.730
And you have some
people there physically.

01:32:57.730 --> 01:32:59.230
And you have some
people dialing in.

01:32:59.230 --> 01:33:01.522
And they're basically like
a hologram, there virtually.

01:33:01.522 --> 01:33:03.617
But then you also
have some AI personas

01:33:03.617 --> 01:33:05.200
that are on your
team that are helping

01:33:05.200 --> 01:33:05.950
you do different things.

01:33:05.950 --> 01:33:08.283
And they can be embodied as
avatars and around the table

01:33:08.283 --> 01:33:09.040
meeting with you.

01:33:09.040 --> 01:33:09.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Are people are going

01:33:09.610 --> 01:33:12.740
to be doing first dates that
are physically separated?

01:33:12.740 --> 01:33:14.600
I could imagine that
some people would--

01:33:14.600 --> 01:33:16.672
is it even worth leaving
the house type date?

01:33:16.672 --> 01:33:17.630
And then they find out.

01:33:17.630 --> 01:33:19.370
And then they meet
for the first time.

01:33:19.370 --> 01:33:20.662
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I mean, maybe.

01:33:20.662 --> 01:33:25.882
I think dating has physical
aspects to it, too.

01:33:25.882 --> 01:33:26.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:33:26.840 --> 01:33:28.460
Some people might
not be-- they want

01:33:28.460 --> 01:33:29.835
to know whether
or not it's worth

01:33:29.835 --> 01:33:32.060
the effort to head out or not.

01:33:32.060 --> 01:33:33.827
They want to bridge
the divide, right?

01:33:33.827 --> 01:33:35.160
MARK ZUCKERBERG: It is possible.

01:33:35.160 --> 01:33:37.460
I mean, I know
some of my friends

01:33:37.460 --> 01:33:42.230
who are dating basically
say that in order

01:33:42.230 --> 01:33:45.680
to make sure that they have
a safe experience, if they're

01:33:45.680 --> 01:33:47.540
going on a first
date, they'll schedule

01:33:47.540 --> 01:33:50.565
something that's shorter and
maybe in the middle of the day.

01:33:50.565 --> 01:33:51.440
So maybe it's coffee.

01:33:51.440 --> 01:33:53.232
So that way, if they
don't like the person,

01:33:53.232 --> 01:33:55.340
they can just get out
before going and scheduling

01:33:55.340 --> 01:33:56.983
a dinner or a real, full date.

01:33:56.983 --> 01:33:57.650
So I don't know.

01:33:57.650 --> 01:33:59.480
Maybe in the future,
people will have

01:33:59.480 --> 01:34:01.790
that experience where
you can feel like you're

01:34:01.790 --> 01:34:02.840
kind of sitting there.

01:34:02.840 --> 01:34:05.998
And it's and it's even easier,
and lighter weight and safer.

01:34:05.998 --> 01:34:07.790
And if you're not having
a good experience,

01:34:07.790 --> 01:34:11.450
you can just teleport
out of there and be gone.

01:34:11.450 --> 01:34:14.690
But yeah, I think that this
will be an interesting question

01:34:14.690 --> 01:34:17.470
in the future.

01:34:17.470 --> 01:34:20.050
There are clearly a lot of
things that are only possible

01:34:20.050 --> 01:34:21.700
physically that--

01:34:21.700 --> 01:34:23.722
or are so much
better physically.

01:34:23.722 --> 01:34:25.180
And then there are
all these things

01:34:25.180 --> 01:34:27.760
that we're building up that
can be digital experiences.

01:34:27.760 --> 01:34:31.420
But it's this weird
artifact of how

01:34:31.420 --> 01:34:34.000
this stuff has been developed
that the digital world

01:34:34.000 --> 01:34:36.250
and the physical world
exist in these completely

01:34:36.250 --> 01:34:37.017
different planes.

01:34:37.017 --> 01:34:39.100
When you want to interact
with the digital world--

01:34:39.100 --> 01:34:39.820
we do it all the time.

01:34:39.820 --> 01:34:41.230
But we pull out a small screen.

01:34:41.230 --> 01:34:42.550
Or we have a big screen.

01:34:42.550 --> 01:34:43.600
And just basically,
we're interacting

01:34:43.600 --> 01:34:45.475
with the digital world
through these screens.

01:34:45.475 --> 01:34:49.270
But I think if we
fast forward a decade

01:34:49.270 --> 01:34:54.220
or more, I think one of the
really interesting questions

01:34:54.220 --> 01:34:56.133
about what is the
world that we're

01:34:56.133 --> 01:34:58.300
going to live in, I think
it's going to increasingly

01:34:58.300 --> 01:35:00.610
be this mesh of the
physical and digital worlds

01:35:00.610 --> 01:35:05.020
that will allow us to feel, A,
that the world that we're in

01:35:05.020 --> 01:35:06.970
is just a lot richer
because there can be all

01:35:06.970 --> 01:35:09.470
these things that people create
that are just so much easier

01:35:09.470 --> 01:35:11.770
to do digitally than physically.

01:35:11.770 --> 01:35:18.470
But B, you're going to have a
real physical sense of presence

01:35:18.470 --> 01:35:20.450
with these things and
not feel like interacting

01:35:20.450 --> 01:35:22.370
in the digital world
is taking you away

01:35:22.370 --> 01:35:24.860
from the physical world,
which today is just

01:35:24.860 --> 01:35:27.620
so much viscerally
richer and more powerful.

01:35:27.620 --> 01:35:32.270
I think the digital world
will be embedded in that

01:35:32.270 --> 01:35:35.250
and will feel just as
vivid in a lot of ways.

01:35:35.250 --> 01:35:36.770
So that's why I
always think-- when

01:35:36.770 --> 01:35:38.937
you were saying before, you
felt like you could look

01:35:38.937 --> 01:35:40.817
around and see the real room.

01:35:40.817 --> 01:35:42.650
I actually think there's
an interesting kind

01:35:42.650 --> 01:35:44.930
of philosophical distinction
between the real room

01:35:44.930 --> 01:35:48.020
and the physical room,
which historically I

01:35:48.020 --> 01:35:50.300
think people would have said
those are the same thing.

01:35:50.300 --> 01:35:51.920
But I actually
think, in the future,

01:35:51.920 --> 01:35:54.650
the real room is going
to be the combination

01:35:54.650 --> 01:35:56.817
of the physical world with
all the digital artifacts

01:35:56.817 --> 01:35:59.358
and objects that are in there
that you can interact with them

01:35:59.358 --> 01:36:02.000
and feel present, whereas the
physical world is just the part

01:36:02.000 --> 01:36:03.200
that's physically there.

01:36:03.200 --> 01:36:05.510
And I think it's possible
to build a real world that's

01:36:05.510 --> 01:36:07.135
the sum of these two
that will actually

01:36:07.135 --> 01:36:09.602
be more profound experience
than what we have today.

01:36:09.602 --> 01:36:11.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, I was struck

01:36:11.060 --> 01:36:13.730
by the smoothness of the
interface between the VR

01:36:13.730 --> 01:36:15.200
and the physical room.

01:36:15.200 --> 01:36:17.555
Your team had me try a--

01:36:17.555 --> 01:36:20.710
I guess it was an exercise
class in the [INAUDIBLE]..

01:36:20.710 --> 01:36:23.567
But it was essentially
like hitting mitts boxing,

01:36:23.567 --> 01:36:24.650
so hitting targets boxing.

01:36:24.650 --> 01:36:25.520
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
Yeah, super natural.

01:36:25.520 --> 01:36:27.895
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, and it
comes at a fairly fast pace

01:36:27.895 --> 01:36:28.700
that then picks up.

01:36:28.700 --> 01:36:29.780
It's got some tutorial.

01:36:29.780 --> 01:36:30.920
It's very easy to use.

01:36:30.920 --> 01:36:32.630
And it certainly got
my heart rate up.

01:36:32.630 --> 01:36:34.640
And I'm in at
least decent shape.

01:36:34.640 --> 01:36:37.280
And I have to be
honest, I've never

01:36:37.280 --> 01:36:40.550
once desired to do any of
these on-screen fitness things.

01:36:40.550 --> 01:36:44.890
I mean, I can't think of
anything more aversive than a--

01:36:44.890 --> 01:36:47.510
I don't want to insult
any particular products,

01:36:47.510 --> 01:36:49.820
but riding a stationary
bike while looking

01:36:49.820 --> 01:36:51.980
at a screen pretending
I'm on a road outside.

01:36:51.980 --> 01:36:54.838
I can't think of
anything worse for me.

01:36:54.838 --> 01:36:56.630
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I do
like the leaderboard.

01:36:56.630 --> 01:36:58.400
Maybe I'm just a very
competitive person.

01:36:58.400 --> 01:37:00.440
If you're going to be
running on a treadmill,

01:37:00.440 --> 01:37:02.390
at least give me a
leaderboard so I can beat

01:37:02.390 --> 01:37:03.500
the people who are ahead of me.

01:37:03.500 --> 01:37:05.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I like
moving outside and certainly

01:37:05.720 --> 01:37:07.467
an exercise class
or aerobics class,

01:37:07.467 --> 01:37:08.550
as they used to call them.

01:37:08.550 --> 01:37:12.980
But the experience I tried
today was extremely engaging.

01:37:12.980 --> 01:37:15.410
And I've done enough
boxing to at least know

01:37:15.410 --> 01:37:17.330
how to do a little bit of it.

01:37:17.330 --> 01:37:18.380
And I really enjoyed it.

01:37:18.380 --> 01:37:19.130
It gets your heart rate up.

01:37:19.130 --> 01:37:20.750
And I completely
forgot that I was

01:37:20.750 --> 01:37:25.370
doing an on-screen experience
in part because, I believe,

01:37:25.370 --> 01:37:28.200
I was still in
that physical room.

01:37:28.200 --> 01:37:31.640
And I think there's
something about the mesh

01:37:31.640 --> 01:37:36.530
of the physical room and
the virtual experience that

01:37:36.530 --> 01:37:38.850
makes it neither of
one world or the other.

01:37:38.850 --> 01:37:40.850
I mean, I really felt at
the interface of those.

01:37:40.850 --> 01:37:42.920
And I certainly got
presence, this feeling

01:37:42.920 --> 01:37:45.050
of forgetting that I was
in a virtual experience

01:37:45.050 --> 01:37:46.717
and got my heart rate
up pretty quickly.

01:37:46.717 --> 01:37:49.050
We had to stop because we
were going to start recording.

01:37:49.050 --> 01:37:51.650
But I would do that for a good
45 minutes in the morning.

01:37:51.650 --> 01:37:54.770
And there's no amount of
money you could pay me truly

01:37:54.770 --> 01:37:57.380
to look at a screen
while pedaling on a bike

01:37:57.380 --> 01:37:58.580
or running on a treadmill.

01:37:58.580 --> 01:38:01.537
So again, bravo, I think
it's going to be very useful.

01:38:01.537 --> 01:38:03.620
It's going to get people
moving their bodies more,

01:38:03.620 --> 01:38:05.060
which certainly--

01:38:05.060 --> 01:38:09.020
social media, up until now,
and a lot of technologies

01:38:09.020 --> 01:38:13.160
have been accused of limiting
the amount of physical activity

01:38:13.160 --> 01:38:15.980
that both children and
adults are engaged in.

01:38:15.980 --> 01:38:17.660
And we know we need
physical activity.

01:38:17.660 --> 01:38:19.855
You're a big proponent
of and practitioner

01:38:19.855 --> 01:38:20.730
of physical activity.

01:38:20.730 --> 01:38:23.510
So is this a major goal
of Meta, to get people

01:38:23.510 --> 01:38:26.270
moving their bodies more
and getting their heart

01:38:26.270 --> 01:38:28.313
rates up and so on?

01:38:28.313 --> 01:38:30.230
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I think
we want to enable it.

01:38:30.230 --> 01:38:31.850
And I think it's good.

01:38:31.850 --> 01:38:38.680
But I think it comes more from a
philosophical view of the world

01:38:38.680 --> 01:38:41.290
than it is necessarily--

01:38:41.290 --> 01:38:43.300
I mean, I don't go
into building products

01:38:43.300 --> 01:38:45.740
to try to shape
people's behavior.

01:38:45.740 --> 01:38:49.900
I believe in empowering
people to do what they want

01:38:49.900 --> 01:38:53.570
and be the best version of
themselves that they can be.

01:38:53.570 --> 01:38:54.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So no agenda?

01:38:54.820 --> 01:38:58.360
MARK ZUCKERBERG: That said,
I do believe that there's

01:38:58.360 --> 01:39:00.280
the previous
generation of computers

01:39:00.280 --> 01:39:02.170
were devices for your mind.

01:39:02.170 --> 01:39:07.420
And I think that we are
not brains and tanks.

01:39:07.420 --> 01:39:10.090
I think that there's a
philosophical view of people

01:39:10.090 --> 01:39:14.500
of like, OK, you are
primarily what you think about

01:39:14.500 --> 01:39:15.710
or your values or something.

01:39:15.710 --> 01:39:17.500
It's like, no, you
are that and you

01:39:17.500 --> 01:39:19.310
are a physical manifestation.

01:39:19.310 --> 01:39:22.060
And people were very physical.

01:39:22.060 --> 01:39:28.840
And I think building a computer
for your whole body and not

01:39:28.840 --> 01:39:33.520
just for your mind is very
fitting with this worldview

01:39:33.520 --> 01:39:36.243
that the actual essence
of you, if you want

01:39:36.243 --> 01:39:37.660
to be present with
another person,

01:39:37.660 --> 01:39:42.220
if you want to be fully engaged
in experience is not just--

01:39:42.220 --> 01:39:45.550
it's not just a video conference
call that looks at your face

01:39:45.550 --> 01:39:47.800
and where you can share ideas.

01:39:47.800 --> 01:39:51.130
It's something that you
can engage your whole body.

01:39:51.130 --> 01:39:53.710
So, yeah I mean, I
think being physical

01:39:53.710 --> 01:39:55.110
is very important to me.

01:39:55.110 --> 01:40:01.840
I mean, that's a lot of the
most fun stuff that I get to do.

01:40:01.840 --> 01:40:04.270
It's a really important
part of how I personally

01:40:04.270 --> 01:40:07.840
balance my energy
levels and just get

01:40:07.840 --> 01:40:10.570
a diversity of experiences
because I could spend all

01:40:10.570 --> 01:40:13.070
my time running the company.

01:40:13.070 --> 01:40:16.690
But I think it's good for people
to do some different things

01:40:16.690 --> 01:40:19.330
and compete in different areas
or learn different things.

01:40:19.330 --> 01:40:22.960
And all of that is good.

01:40:22.960 --> 01:40:28.660
If people want to do really
intense workouts with the work

01:40:28.660 --> 01:40:32.980
that we're doing with Quest
or with eventual AR glasses,

01:40:32.980 --> 01:40:33.800
great.

01:40:33.800 --> 01:40:37.780
But even if you don't want to
do a really intense workout,

01:40:37.780 --> 01:40:40.570
I think just having a computing
environment and platform which

01:40:40.570 --> 01:40:44.050
is inherently physical captures
more of the essence of what

01:40:44.050 --> 01:40:47.650
we are as people than any of
the previous computing platforms

01:40:47.650 --> 01:40:49.100
that we've had to date.

01:40:49.100 --> 01:40:50.808
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
was even thinking just

01:40:50.808 --> 01:40:54.850
of the simple task of getting
better range of motion a.k.a.

01:40:54.850 --> 01:40:55.900
flexibility.

01:40:55.900 --> 01:40:58.360
I could imagine, inside
of the VR experience,

01:40:58.360 --> 01:41:01.930
leaning into a stretch, standard
type of lunge-type stretch,

01:41:01.930 --> 01:41:04.450
but actually seeing a
meter of are you are you

01:41:04.450 --> 01:41:06.100
approaching new
levels of flexibility

01:41:06.100 --> 01:41:07.517
in that moment
where it's actually

01:41:07.517 --> 01:41:10.210
measuring some
kinesthetic elements

01:41:10.210 --> 01:41:14.040
on the body in the joints,
whereas normally, you

01:41:14.040 --> 01:41:16.540
might have to do that in front
of a camera, which then would

01:41:16.540 --> 01:41:18.998
give you the data on a screen
that you'd look at afterwards

01:41:18.998 --> 01:41:22.960
or hire an expensive coach or
looking at form and resistance

01:41:22.960 --> 01:41:23.690
training.

01:41:23.690 --> 01:41:25.523
So you're actually
lifting physical weights.

01:41:25.523 --> 01:41:27.898
But it's telling you whether
or not you're breaking form.

01:41:27.898 --> 01:41:29.540
I mean, there's just
so much that could

01:41:29.540 --> 01:41:30.420
be done inside of there.

01:41:30.420 --> 01:41:32.003
And then my mind
just starts to spiral

01:41:32.003 --> 01:41:35.120
into, wow, this is very
likely to transform

01:41:35.120 --> 01:41:37.403
what we think of as,
quote unquote, "exercise."

01:41:37.403 --> 01:41:38.820
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
Yeah, I think so.

01:41:38.820 --> 01:41:40.570
I think there's still
a bunch of questions

01:41:40.570 --> 01:41:43.160
that need to get answered.

01:41:43.160 --> 01:41:46.190
I don't think most people
are going to necessarily want

01:41:46.190 --> 01:41:49.640
to install a lot of
sensors or cameras

01:41:49.640 --> 01:41:50.760
to track their whole body.

01:41:50.760 --> 01:41:52.790
So we're just over
time getting better

01:41:52.790 --> 01:41:56.330
from the sensors that are on
the headsets of being able to do

01:41:56.330 --> 01:41:58.220
very good hand tracking.

01:41:58.220 --> 01:42:00.050
So we have this
research demo where

01:42:00.050 --> 01:42:03.020
you now, just with the hand
tracking from the headset,

01:42:03.020 --> 01:42:03.680
you can type.

01:42:03.680 --> 01:42:05.852
It just projects a little
keyboard onto your table.

01:42:05.852 --> 01:42:06.560
And you can type.

01:42:06.560 --> 01:42:09.230
And people type like 100
words a minute with that.

01:42:09.230 --> 01:42:10.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: With
a virtual keyboard?

01:42:10.280 --> 01:42:11.197
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:42:11.197 --> 01:42:13.460
We're starting to be able to--

01:42:13.460 --> 01:42:18.440
using some modern AI
techniques, be able to simulate

01:42:18.440 --> 01:42:21.380
and understand where
your torso's position is.

01:42:21.380 --> 01:42:23.180
Even though you
can't always see it,

01:42:23.180 --> 01:42:24.835
you can see it a
bunch of the time.

01:42:24.835 --> 01:42:26.210
And if you fuse
together what you

01:42:26.210 --> 01:42:30.020
do see with the accelerometer
and understanding

01:42:30.020 --> 01:42:31.940
how the thing is
moving, you can kind of

01:42:31.940 --> 01:42:35.540
understand what the body
position is going to be.

01:42:35.540 --> 01:42:38.340
But some things are
still going to be hard.

01:42:38.340 --> 01:42:41.420
So you mentioned boxing.

01:42:41.420 --> 01:42:44.600
That one works pretty well
because we understand your head

01:42:44.600 --> 01:42:45.150
position.

01:42:45.150 --> 01:42:47.030
We understand your hands.

01:42:47.030 --> 01:42:50.270
And now, we're increasingly
understanding your body

01:42:50.270 --> 01:42:51.548
position.

01:42:51.548 --> 01:42:53.090
But let's say you
want to expand that

01:42:53.090 --> 01:42:56.250
to Muay Thai or kickboxing.

01:42:56.250 --> 01:42:56.750
OK.

01:42:56.750 --> 01:42:58.760
So legs, that's a
different part of tracking.

01:42:58.760 --> 01:43:01.850
That's harder because that's
out of the field of view

01:43:01.850 --> 01:43:03.180
more of the time.

01:43:03.180 --> 01:43:05.150
But there's also the
element of resistance.

01:43:05.150 --> 01:43:07.070
So you can throw a
punch, and retract it,

01:43:07.070 --> 01:43:11.420
and shadow box and do
that without upsetting

01:43:11.420 --> 01:43:13.320
your physical balance that much.

01:43:13.320 --> 01:43:15.440
But if you want to
throw a roundhouse kick

01:43:15.440 --> 01:43:17.180
and there's no
one there, then, I

01:43:17.180 --> 01:43:20.270
mean, the standard way that you
do it when you're shadowboxing

01:43:20.270 --> 01:43:22.190
is you basically
do a little 360.

01:43:22.190 --> 01:43:23.543
But I don't know.

01:43:23.543 --> 01:43:24.710
Is that going to feel great?

01:43:24.710 --> 01:43:27.230
I mean, I think there's
a question about what

01:43:27.230 --> 01:43:29.103
that experience should be.

01:43:29.103 --> 01:43:30.770
And then if you want
to go even further,

01:43:30.770 --> 01:43:34.740
if you want to get
grappling to work,

01:43:34.740 --> 01:43:36.447
I'm not even sure
how you would do

01:43:36.447 --> 01:43:39.030
that without having resistance
of understanding what the force

01:43:39.030 --> 01:43:40.165
is applied to you would be.

01:43:40.165 --> 01:43:41.790
And then you get
into, OK, maybe you're

01:43:41.790 --> 01:43:44.070
going to have some
kind of bodysuit that

01:43:44.070 --> 01:43:45.870
can apply haptics.

01:43:45.870 --> 01:43:49.350
But I'm not even sure that even
a pretty advanced haptic system

01:43:49.350 --> 01:43:52.260
is going to be able to be
quite good enough to simulate

01:43:52.260 --> 01:43:55.860
the actual forces that would be
applied to you in a grappling

01:43:55.860 --> 01:43:56.410
scenario.

01:43:56.410 --> 01:43:58.650
So this is part of what's
fun about technology,

01:43:58.650 --> 01:44:01.432
though, is you keep on
getting new capabilities.

01:44:01.432 --> 01:44:03.390
And then you need to
figure out what things you

01:44:03.390 --> 01:44:04.120
can do with them.

01:44:04.120 --> 01:44:06.600
So I think it's really
neat that we can do boxing.

01:44:06.600 --> 01:44:08.190
And we can do the
supernatural thing.

01:44:08.190 --> 01:44:10.080
And there's a bunch
of awesome cardio,

01:44:10.080 --> 01:44:11.893
and dancing and
things like that.

01:44:11.893 --> 01:44:13.560
And then there's also
still so much more

01:44:13.560 --> 01:44:17.670
to do that I'm excited
to get to over time.

01:44:17.670 --> 01:44:19.380
But it's a long journey.

01:44:19.380 --> 01:44:22.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And what
about things like painting,

01:44:22.230 --> 01:44:23.820
and art and music?

01:44:23.820 --> 01:44:28.440
I imagine-- of course,
different mediums--

01:44:28.440 --> 01:44:29.972
I like to draw with
pen and pencil.

01:44:29.972 --> 01:44:32.430
But I could imagine trying to
learn how to paint virtually.

01:44:32.430 --> 01:44:35.118
And of course, you could
print out a physical version

01:44:35.118 --> 01:44:35.910
of that at the end.

01:44:35.910 --> 01:44:38.080
This doesn't have to depart
from the physical world.

01:44:38.080 --> 01:44:39.310
It could end in
the physical world.

01:44:39.310 --> 01:44:40.893
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Did
you see the demo,

01:44:40.893 --> 01:44:43.380
the piano demo where you--

01:44:43.380 --> 01:44:46.040
either you're there
with a physical keyboard

01:44:46.040 --> 01:44:47.570
or it could be a
virtual keyboard.

01:44:47.570 --> 01:44:51.960
But the app basically
highlights what keys

01:44:51.960 --> 01:44:55.260
you need to press in
order to play the song.

01:44:55.260 --> 01:44:58.140
So it's basically like
you're looking at your piano.

01:44:58.140 --> 01:45:01.955
And it's teaching you how to
play a song that you choose.

01:45:01.955 --> 01:45:03.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
An actual piano?

01:45:03.330 --> 01:45:03.720
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:45:03.720 --> 01:45:05.845
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But it's
illuminating certain keys

01:45:05.845 --> 01:45:06.870
in the virtual space.

01:45:06.870 --> 01:45:07.787
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:45:07.787 --> 01:45:10.080
And it could either be a
virtual piano or a keyboard

01:45:10.080 --> 01:45:11.850
if you don't have a
piano or keyboard.

01:45:11.850 --> 01:45:15.150
Or it could use your
actual keyboard.

01:45:15.150 --> 01:45:18.840
So yeah, I think
stuff like that is

01:45:18.840 --> 01:45:23.700
going to be really fascinating
for education and expression.

01:45:23.700 --> 01:45:26.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And excuse
me, but for broadening access

01:45:26.490 --> 01:45:28.110
to expensive equipment.

01:45:28.110 --> 01:45:30.578
I mean, a piano is
no small expense.

01:45:30.578 --> 01:45:31.620
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Exactly.

01:45:31.620 --> 01:45:33.578
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And it
takes up a lot of space

01:45:33.578 --> 01:45:34.495
and needs to be tuned.

01:45:34.495 --> 01:45:36.453
You can think of all
these things, the kid that

01:45:36.453 --> 01:45:38.040
has very little
income or their family

01:45:38.040 --> 01:45:39.457
has very little
income could learn

01:45:39.457 --> 01:45:41.383
to play a virtual piano
at a much lower cost.

01:45:41.383 --> 01:45:42.300
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:45:42.300 --> 01:45:43.290
And it gets back
to the question I

01:45:43.290 --> 01:45:45.870
was asking before about this
thought experiment of how

01:45:45.870 --> 01:45:48.180
many of the things
that we physically have

01:45:48.180 --> 01:45:51.050
today actually need
to be physical.

01:45:51.050 --> 01:45:52.750
The piano doesn't.

01:45:52.750 --> 01:45:56.440
Maybe there's some
premium where--

01:45:56.440 --> 01:46:00.910
maybe it's a somewhat better,
more tactile experience

01:46:00.910 --> 01:46:02.320
to have a physical one.

01:46:02.320 --> 01:46:05.230
But for people who don't
have the space for it,

01:46:05.230 --> 01:46:07.133
or who can't afford
to buy a piano,

01:46:07.133 --> 01:46:08.800
or just aren't sure
that they would want

01:46:08.800 --> 01:46:10.480
to make that investment at
the beginning of learning how

01:46:10.480 --> 01:46:12.680
to play piano, I
think, in the future,

01:46:12.680 --> 01:46:15.130
you'll have the option
of just buying an app

01:46:15.130 --> 01:46:20.110
or a hologram piano which
will be a lot more affordable.

01:46:20.110 --> 01:46:24.820
And I think that's going to
unlock a ton of creativity too

01:46:24.820 --> 01:46:29.350
because instead of the
market for piano makers

01:46:29.350 --> 01:46:33.220
being constrained to like a
relatively small set of experts

01:46:33.220 --> 01:46:35.800
who have perfected
that craft, you're

01:46:35.800 --> 01:46:40.990
going to have kids or developers
all around the world designing

01:46:40.990 --> 01:46:44.143
crazy designs for potential
keyboards and pianos

01:46:44.143 --> 01:46:46.060
that look nothing like
what we've seen before,

01:46:46.060 --> 01:46:49.690
but maybe bring even
more joy or even more

01:46:49.690 --> 01:46:51.520
fun into the world
where you have fewer

01:46:51.520 --> 01:46:52.820
of these physical constraints.

01:46:52.820 --> 01:46:56.230
So I think there's going to be
a lot of wild stuff to explore.

01:46:56.230 --> 01:46:56.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
There's definitely

01:46:56.770 --> 01:46:58.562
going to be a lot of
wild stuff to explore.

01:46:58.562 --> 01:47:03.640
I just had this
idea/image in my mind

01:47:03.640 --> 01:47:05.980
of what you were talking
about merged with our earlier

01:47:05.980 --> 01:47:07.990
conversation when
Priscilla was here.

01:47:07.990 --> 01:47:10.150
I could imagine a time
not too long from now

01:47:10.150 --> 01:47:13.420
where you're using mixed reality
to run experiments in the lab,

01:47:13.420 --> 01:47:15.640
literally mixing
virtual solutions,

01:47:15.640 --> 01:47:18.075
getting potential outcomes,
and then picking the best

01:47:18.075 --> 01:47:20.200
one to then go actually do
in the real world, which

01:47:20.200 --> 01:47:25.043
is very both financially
costly and time-wise costly.

01:47:25.043 --> 01:47:25.960
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:47:25.960 --> 01:47:32.410
I mean, people are already using
VR for surgery and education

01:47:32.410 --> 01:47:34.030
on it.

01:47:34.030 --> 01:47:38.578
And there's some study that
was done that basically tried

01:47:38.578 --> 01:47:40.870
to do a controlled experiment
of people who learned how

01:47:40.870 --> 01:47:45.700
to do a specific surgery
through just the normal textbook

01:47:45.700 --> 01:47:50.020
and lecture method
versus you show the knee

01:47:50.020 --> 01:47:53.470
and you have it be a
large, blown-up model.

01:47:53.470 --> 01:47:55.720
And people can manipulate
it and practice

01:47:55.720 --> 01:47:58.090
where they would make the cuts.

01:47:58.090 --> 01:48:00.870
And like the people in
that class did better.

01:48:03.580 --> 01:48:05.890
Yeah, I think that it's
going to be profound

01:48:05.890 --> 01:48:07.103
for a lot of different areas.

01:48:07.103 --> 01:48:09.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And the last
example that leaps to mind--

01:48:09.520 --> 01:48:12.100
I think social media
and online culture

01:48:12.100 --> 01:48:14.980
has been accused of creating
a lot of real world--

01:48:14.980 --> 01:48:17.450
let's call it physical world
social anxiety for people.

01:48:17.450 --> 01:48:20.800
But I could imagine practicing
a social interaction.

01:48:20.800 --> 01:48:22.922
Or a kid that has a
lot of social anxiety

01:48:22.922 --> 01:48:24.880
or that needs to advocate
for themselves better

01:48:24.880 --> 01:48:27.043
learning how to do
that progressively

01:48:27.043 --> 01:48:28.960
through a virtual
interaction, and then taking

01:48:28.960 --> 01:48:32.350
that to the real world because,
in my very recent experience

01:48:32.350 --> 01:48:35.800
today, it's so blended
now with real experience

01:48:35.800 --> 01:48:37.270
that the kid that
feels terrified

01:48:37.270 --> 01:48:39.550
of advocating for
themselves, or just talking

01:48:39.550 --> 01:48:41.050
to another human
being, or an adult,

01:48:41.050 --> 01:48:43.467
or being in a new circumstance
of a room full of kids, you

01:48:43.467 --> 01:48:45.910
could really experience
that in silico

01:48:45.910 --> 01:48:48.550
first and get comfortable,
let the nervous system

01:48:48.550 --> 01:48:51.480
attenuate a bit, and then take
it into the, quote unquote,

01:48:51.480 --> 01:48:52.550
"physical world."

01:48:52.550 --> 01:48:54.050
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
Yeah, I think we'll

01:48:54.050 --> 01:48:55.570
see experiences like that.

01:48:55.570 --> 01:48:58.070
I mean, I also think that
some of the social dynamics

01:48:58.070 --> 01:49:01.580
around how people
interact in this kind

01:49:01.580 --> 01:49:05.310
of blended digital world will
be more nuanced in other ways.

01:49:05.310 --> 01:49:09.320
So I'm sure that there will
be new anxieties that people

01:49:09.320 --> 01:49:14.480
develop too, just like teens
today need to navigate dynamics

01:49:14.480 --> 01:49:18.920
around texting
constantly that we just

01:49:18.920 --> 01:49:21.200
didn't have when we were kids.

01:49:21.200 --> 01:49:22.908
So I think it will
help with some things.

01:49:22.908 --> 01:49:25.367
I think that there will be new
issues that hopefully we can

01:49:25.367 --> 01:49:26.900
help people work through too.

01:49:26.900 --> 01:49:28.970
But overall, yeah, I
think it's going to be

01:49:28.970 --> 01:49:30.593
really powerful and positive.

01:49:30.593 --> 01:49:32.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Let's
talk about the glasses.

01:49:32.510 --> 01:49:33.080
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Sure.

01:49:33.080 --> 01:49:34.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This was wild.

01:49:34.670 --> 01:49:36.380
Put on a Ray-Bans--

01:49:36.380 --> 01:49:38.340
I like the way they look.

01:49:38.340 --> 01:49:39.120
They're clear.

01:49:39.120 --> 01:49:42.840
They look like any
other Ray-Ban glasses,

01:49:42.840 --> 01:49:46.560
except that I could
call out to the glasses.

01:49:46.560 --> 01:49:49.140
I could just say,
hey Meta, I want

01:49:49.140 --> 01:49:51.330
to listen to the
Bach variations--

01:49:51.330 --> 01:49:53.460
the Goldberg Variations of Bach.

01:49:53.460 --> 01:49:56.670
And Meta responded.

01:49:56.670 --> 01:49:58.590
And no one around me could hear.

01:49:58.590 --> 01:50:01.837
But I could hear with
exquisite clarity.

01:50:01.837 --> 01:50:04.170
And by the way, I'm not getting
paid to say any of this.

01:50:04.170 --> 01:50:06.060
I'm just still
blown away by this.

01:50:06.060 --> 01:50:09.610
Folks, I want a
these very badly.

01:50:09.610 --> 01:50:12.090
I could hear, OK, I'm
selecting those now--

01:50:12.090 --> 01:50:12.998
or that music now.

01:50:12.998 --> 01:50:14.790
And then I could hear
it in the background.

01:50:14.790 --> 01:50:16.582
But then I could still
have a conversation.

01:50:16.582 --> 01:50:20.160
So this was neither headphones
in nor headphones out.

01:50:20.160 --> 01:50:21.870
And I could say,
wait, pause the music.

01:50:21.870 --> 01:50:23.520
And it would pause.

01:50:23.520 --> 01:50:25.770
And the best part was I
didn't have to, quote unquote,

01:50:25.770 --> 01:50:27.330
"leave the room" mentally.

01:50:27.330 --> 01:50:29.130
I didn't even have
to take out a phone.

01:50:29.130 --> 01:50:32.190
It was all interfaced through
this very local environment

01:50:32.190 --> 01:50:33.322
in and around the head.

01:50:33.322 --> 01:50:35.280
And as a neuroscientist,
I'm fascinated by this

01:50:35.280 --> 01:50:37.200
because, of course, all of
our perceptions-- auditory,

01:50:37.200 --> 01:50:37.980
visual et cetera--

01:50:37.980 --> 01:50:41.880
are occurring inside the casing
of this thing we call a skull.

01:50:41.880 --> 01:50:46.000
But maybe you could
comment on the origin

01:50:46.000 --> 01:50:48.320
of that design for you,
the ideas behind that,

01:50:48.320 --> 01:50:50.320
and where you think it
could go because I'm sure

01:50:50.320 --> 01:50:52.715
I'm just scratching the surface.

01:50:52.715 --> 01:50:54.090
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
The real product

01:50:54.090 --> 01:50:56.550
that we want to
eventually get to is

01:50:56.550 --> 01:50:59.940
this full augmented
reality product

01:50:59.940 --> 01:51:04.500
in a stylish and comfortable
normal glasses form factor.

01:51:04.500 --> 01:51:06.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not a dorky
VR headset, so to speak?

01:51:06.930 --> 01:51:07.530
MARK ZUCKERBERG: No, I mean--

01:51:07.530 --> 01:51:08.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Because
the VR headset does

01:51:08.340 --> 01:51:10.260
feel kind of big on the face.

01:51:10.260 --> 01:51:11.610
MARK ZUCKERBERG: There's
going to be a place for that,

01:51:11.610 --> 01:51:13.200
too, just like you
have your laptop

01:51:13.200 --> 01:51:15.167
and you have your workstation.

01:51:15.167 --> 01:51:17.250
Or maybe the better analogy
is you have your phone

01:51:17.250 --> 01:51:19.350
and you have your workstation.

01:51:19.350 --> 01:51:22.773
These AR glasses are going to
be like your phone in that you

01:51:22.773 --> 01:51:23.940
have something on your face.

01:51:23.940 --> 01:51:27.330
And you will, I think,
be able to, if you want,

01:51:27.330 --> 01:51:29.550
wear it for a lot of
the day and interact

01:51:29.550 --> 01:51:32.502
with it very frequently.

01:51:32.502 --> 01:51:33.960
I don't think that
people are going

01:51:33.960 --> 01:51:36.813
to be walking around the
world wearing VR headsets.

01:51:36.813 --> 01:51:37.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Let's hope.

01:51:37.980 --> 01:51:40.397
MARK ZUCKERBERG: But yeah,
that's certainly not the future

01:51:40.397 --> 01:51:42.750
that I'm hoping we get to.

01:51:42.750 --> 01:51:47.100
But I do think that there
is a place for having--

01:51:47.100 --> 01:51:48.570
because it's a
bigger form factor,

01:51:48.570 --> 01:51:50.050
it has more compute power.

01:51:50.050 --> 01:51:53.890
So just like your workstation
or your bigger computer

01:51:53.890 --> 01:51:55.967
can do more than
your phone can do,

01:51:55.967 --> 01:51:57.550
there's a place for
that when you want

01:51:57.550 --> 01:51:59.508
to settle into an intense task.

01:51:59.508 --> 01:52:01.300
If you have a doctor
who's doing a surgery,

01:52:01.300 --> 01:52:03.217
I would want them doing
it through the headset

01:52:03.217 --> 01:52:07.390
not through the phone equivalent
or the lower powered glasses.

01:52:07.390 --> 01:52:09.653
But just like phones
are powerful enough

01:52:09.653 --> 01:52:12.070
to do a lot of things, I think
the glasses will eventually

01:52:12.070 --> 01:52:14.420
get there, too.

01:52:14.420 --> 01:52:17.810
Now, that said, there's a
bunch of really hard technology

01:52:17.810 --> 01:52:21.980
problems to address in order
to be able to get to this point

01:52:21.980 --> 01:52:26.240
where you can put full
holograms in the world.

01:52:26.240 --> 01:52:29.210
You're basically
miniaturizing a supercomputer

01:52:29.210 --> 01:52:35.090
and putting it into a glasses
so that the glasses still

01:52:35.090 --> 01:52:36.650
look stylish and normal.

01:52:36.650 --> 01:52:40.460
And that's a really
hard technology problem.

01:52:40.460 --> 01:52:43.310
Making things small
is really hard.

01:52:43.310 --> 01:52:48.170
A holographic display
is different from what

01:52:48.170 --> 01:52:52.100
our industry has optimized
for for 30 or 40 years now,

01:52:52.100 --> 01:52:53.540
building screens.

01:52:53.540 --> 01:52:55.940
There's a whole
industrial process

01:52:55.940 --> 01:53:00.110
around that goes into phones,
and TVs, and computers,

01:53:00.110 --> 01:53:03.480
and increasingly so many things
that have different screens.

01:53:03.480 --> 01:53:05.480
There's a whole pipeline
that's gotten very good

01:53:05.480 --> 01:53:06.920
at making that kind of screen.

01:53:06.920 --> 01:53:10.560
And the holographic
displays are just

01:53:10.560 --> 01:53:14.010
a completely different thing
because it's not a screen.

01:53:14.010 --> 01:53:16.050
It's a thing that
you can shoot light

01:53:16.050 --> 01:53:18.960
into through a laser or some
other kind of projector.

01:53:18.960 --> 01:53:21.550
And it can place that as
an object in the world.

01:53:21.550 --> 01:53:24.720
So that's going to need to be
this whole other industrial

01:53:24.720 --> 01:53:28.540
process that gets built up to
doing that in an efficient way.

01:53:28.540 --> 01:53:32.460
So all that said,
we're basically

01:53:32.460 --> 01:53:35.950
taking two different approaches
towards building this at once.

01:53:35.950 --> 01:53:41.320
One is we are trying to keep
in mind what is the long-term

01:53:41.320 --> 01:53:42.190
thing that--

01:53:42.190 --> 01:53:43.720
it's not super far off.

01:53:43.720 --> 01:53:46.240
Within a few years,
I think we'll

01:53:46.240 --> 01:53:49.822
have something that's a first
version of this full vision

01:53:49.822 --> 01:53:50.780
that I'm talking about.

01:53:50.780 --> 01:53:52.905
I mean, we have something
that's working internally

01:53:52.905 --> 01:53:55.750
that we use as a dev kit.

01:53:55.750 --> 01:54:01.130
But that one, that's
a big challenge.

01:54:01.130 --> 01:54:03.980
It's going to be more expensive.

01:54:03.980 --> 01:54:07.190
And it's harder to get
all the pieces working.

01:54:07.190 --> 01:54:09.170
The other approach has
been, all right, let's

01:54:09.170 --> 01:54:11.180
start with what
we know we can put

01:54:11.180 --> 01:54:14.060
into a pair of
stylish sunglasses

01:54:14.060 --> 01:54:18.670
today and just make
them as smart as we can.

01:54:18.670 --> 01:54:22.600
So for the first
version, we worked with--

01:54:22.600 --> 01:54:24.640
we did this collaboration
with Ray-Ban

01:54:24.640 --> 01:54:27.130
because that's well-accepted.

01:54:27.130 --> 01:54:29.110
These are well-designed glasses.

01:54:29.110 --> 01:54:29.800
They're classic.

01:54:29.800 --> 01:54:32.020
People have used
them for decades.

01:54:32.020 --> 01:54:34.605
For the first version, we
got a sensor on the front,

01:54:34.605 --> 01:54:36.730
so you could capture moments
without having to take

01:54:36.730 --> 01:54:37.980
your phone out of your pocket.

01:54:37.980 --> 01:54:40.570
So you got photos and videos.

01:54:40.570 --> 01:54:42.195
You had the speaker
and the microphone,

01:54:42.195 --> 01:54:44.860
so you can listen to music.

01:54:44.860 --> 01:54:47.050
You could communicate with it.

01:54:47.050 --> 01:54:51.015
But that was the
first version of it.

01:54:51.015 --> 01:54:52.390
We had a lot of
the basics there.

01:54:52.390 --> 01:54:54.070
But we saw how people used it.

01:54:54.070 --> 01:54:56.230
And we tuned it.

01:54:56.230 --> 01:55:00.190
We made the camera twice as
good for this new version

01:55:00.190 --> 01:55:00.850
that we made.

01:55:00.850 --> 01:55:02.860
The audio is a lot
crisper for the use cases

01:55:02.860 --> 01:55:05.170
that we saw that people actually
used, which is-- some of it

01:55:05.170 --> 01:55:06.087
is listening to music.

01:55:06.087 --> 01:55:09.250
But a lot of it is people want
to take calls on their glasses.

01:55:09.250 --> 01:55:12.190
They want to listen to podcasts.

01:55:12.190 --> 01:55:14.710
But the biggest thing that
I think is interesting

01:55:14.710 --> 01:55:19.580
is the ability to get AI running
on it, which it doesn't just

01:55:19.580 --> 01:55:20.660
run on the glasses.

01:55:20.660 --> 01:55:23.660
It also kind of proxies
through your phone.

01:55:23.660 --> 01:55:28.607
But I mean, with all
the advances in LLMs--

01:55:28.607 --> 01:55:30.440
we talked about this a
bit in the first part

01:55:30.440 --> 01:55:33.230
of the conversation.

01:55:33.230 --> 01:55:35.715
Having the ability to have
your Meta AI assistant

01:55:35.715 --> 01:55:37.340
that you can just
talk to and basically

01:55:37.340 --> 01:55:40.430
ask any question
throughout the day is--

01:55:40.430 --> 01:55:42.050
I think it'd be
really fascinating.

01:55:42.050 --> 01:55:45.980
And like you were
saying about how

01:55:45.980 --> 01:55:49.813
we process the world as
people, eventually, I

01:55:49.813 --> 01:55:51.230
think you're going
to want your AI

01:55:51.230 --> 01:55:55.012
assistant to be able to see what
you see and hear what you hear.

01:55:55.012 --> 01:55:55.970
Maybe not all the time.

01:55:55.970 --> 01:55:57.762
But you're going to
want to be able to tell

01:55:57.762 --> 01:56:00.500
it to go into a mode where it
can see what you see and hear

01:56:00.500 --> 01:56:01.280
what you hear.

01:56:01.280 --> 01:56:05.980
And what's the
device design that

01:56:05.980 --> 01:56:08.175
best positions an
AI assistant to be

01:56:08.175 --> 01:56:09.550
able to see what
you see and hear

01:56:09.550 --> 01:56:11.290
what you hear so it
can best help you?

01:56:11.290 --> 01:56:13.630
Well, that's glasses,
where it basically

01:56:13.630 --> 01:56:15.760
has a sensor to be able
to see what you see

01:56:15.760 --> 01:56:20.380
and a microphone that is
close to your ears that

01:56:20.380 --> 01:56:23.470
can hear what you hear.

01:56:23.470 --> 01:56:25.960
The other design goal
is, like you said,

01:56:25.960 --> 01:56:28.750
to keep you present
in the world.

01:56:28.750 --> 01:56:31.870
So I think one of the
issues with phones

01:56:31.870 --> 01:56:35.840
is they pull you away from
what's physically happening

01:56:35.840 --> 01:56:36.340
around you.

01:56:36.340 --> 01:56:38.632
And I don't think that the
next generation of computing

01:56:38.632 --> 01:56:39.293
will do that.

01:56:39.293 --> 01:56:40.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm
chuckling to myself

01:56:40.960 --> 01:56:41.680
because I have a friend.

01:56:41.680 --> 01:56:43.180
He's a very well
known photographer.

01:56:43.180 --> 01:56:46.180
And he was laughing about
how people go to a concert.

01:56:46.180 --> 01:56:48.573
And everyone's filming
the concert on their phone

01:56:48.573 --> 01:56:50.740
so that they can be the
person that posts the thing.

01:56:50.740 --> 01:56:52.720
But there are literally
millions of other people

01:56:52.720 --> 01:56:54.053
who posted the exact same thing.

01:56:54.053 --> 01:56:58.330
But somehow, it feels important
to post our unique experience.

01:56:58.330 --> 01:57:00.490
With glasses, that
would essentially

01:57:00.490 --> 01:57:03.610
smooth that gap completely.

01:57:03.610 --> 01:57:05.860
You could just worry about
it later, download it then.

01:57:05.860 --> 01:57:08.290
There are issues, I
realize, with glasses

01:57:08.290 --> 01:57:11.023
because they are so seamless
with everyday experience,

01:57:11.023 --> 01:57:12.940
even though you and I
aren't wearing them now.

01:57:12.940 --> 01:57:16.060
It's very common for
people to wear glasses--

01:57:16.060 --> 01:57:17.747
issues of recording and consent.

01:57:17.747 --> 01:57:18.580
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:57:18.580 --> 01:57:21.065
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Like if I go
to a locker room at my gym,

01:57:21.065 --> 01:57:23.440
I'm assuming that the people
with glasses aren't filming.

01:57:23.440 --> 01:57:27.040
Whereas right now, because
there's a sharp transition when

01:57:27.040 --> 01:57:30.370
there's a phone in the room
and someone's pointing it,

01:57:30.370 --> 01:57:35.450
people generally say, no phones
in locker rooms and recording.

01:57:35.450 --> 01:57:36.860
So that's just one instance.

01:57:36.860 --> 01:57:37.510
I mean, there are
other instances.

01:57:37.510 --> 01:57:39.220
MARK ZUCKERBERG: We have
the whole privacy light.

01:57:39.220 --> 01:57:39.800
Did you get--

01:57:39.800 --> 01:57:41.275
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I didn't
get a chance to explore that.

01:57:41.275 --> 01:57:42.192
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:57:42.192 --> 01:57:44.560
So anytime that it's
active, that the camera

01:57:44.560 --> 01:57:49.950
sensor is active, it's basically
pulsing a white bright light.

01:57:49.950 --> 01:57:50.950
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

01:57:50.950 --> 01:57:53.982
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Which is, by
the way, more than cameras do.

01:57:53.982 --> 01:57:54.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:57:54.940 --> 01:57:56.230
Someone could be
holding a phone.

01:57:56.230 --> 01:57:57.147
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:57:57.147 --> 01:58:00.430
I mean, phones aren't showing
a light, bright sensor

01:58:00.430 --> 01:58:02.013
when you're taking a photo.

01:58:02.013 --> 01:58:03.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
People oftentimes

01:58:03.430 --> 01:58:05.225
will pretend they're texting
and they're actually recording.

01:58:05.225 --> 01:58:07.610
I actually saw an instance
of this in a barber shop

01:58:07.610 --> 01:58:09.170
once, where someone
was recording

01:58:09.170 --> 01:58:11.330
and they were pretending
that they were texting.

01:58:11.330 --> 01:58:12.290
And it was interesting.

01:58:12.290 --> 01:58:15.380
There was a pretty intense
interaction that ensued.

01:58:15.380 --> 01:58:17.870
And it was like, wow, it's
pretty easy for people

01:58:17.870 --> 01:58:20.313
to feign texting while
actually recording.

01:58:20.313 --> 01:58:21.230
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

01:58:21.230 --> 01:58:24.740
So I think when
you're evaluating

01:58:24.740 --> 01:58:30.070
a risk with a new technology,
the bar shouldn't be is it

01:58:30.070 --> 01:58:32.290
possible to do anything bad.

01:58:32.290 --> 01:58:36.190
It's does this new
technology make it easier

01:58:36.190 --> 01:58:38.950
to do something bad than
what people already had.

01:58:38.950 --> 01:58:42.580
And I think because you have
this privacy light that is just

01:58:42.580 --> 01:58:44.480
broadcasting to everyone
around you, hey,

01:58:44.480 --> 01:58:46.450
this thing is recording now--

01:58:46.450 --> 01:58:50.620
I think that makes it
actually less discreet

01:58:50.620 --> 01:58:52.720
to do it through the
glasses than what you could

01:58:52.720 --> 01:58:56.230
do with a phone already, which
I think is basically the bar

01:58:56.230 --> 01:58:58.910
that we wanted to get over
from a design perspective.

01:58:58.910 --> 01:58:59.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Thank
you for pointing out

01:58:59.800 --> 01:59:01.050
that it has the privacy light.

01:59:01.050 --> 01:59:03.010
I didn't get long
enough in the experience

01:59:03.010 --> 01:59:04.420
to explore all the features.

01:59:04.420 --> 01:59:08.650
But again, I can think
of a lot of uses--

01:59:08.650 --> 01:59:11.200
being able to look at a
restaurant from the outside

01:59:11.200 --> 01:59:16.420
and see the menu, get a
status on how crowded it is.

01:59:16.420 --> 01:59:17.908
As much as I love--

01:59:17.908 --> 01:59:19.450
I don't want to call
out-- let's just

01:59:19.450 --> 01:59:23.680
say app-based map functions
that allow you to navigate

01:59:23.680 --> 01:59:25.360
and the audio is OK.

01:59:25.360 --> 01:59:28.450
It's nice to have a conversation
with somebody on the phone

01:59:28.450 --> 01:59:29.320
or in the vehicle.

01:59:29.320 --> 01:59:31.893
And it'd be great if the road
was traced where I should turn.

01:59:31.893 --> 01:59:33.310
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
Yeah, absolutely.

01:59:33.310 --> 01:59:34.893
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
These kinds of things

01:59:34.893 --> 01:59:37.090
seem like it's going to be
straightforward for Meta

01:59:37.090 --> 01:59:37.970
engineers to create.

01:59:37.970 --> 01:59:39.610
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah, in
a version, we'll have it

01:59:39.610 --> 01:59:41.652
so it'll also have the
holographic display, where

01:59:41.652 --> 01:59:43.040
it can show you the directions.

01:59:43.040 --> 01:59:45.100
But I think that there
will basically just

01:59:45.100 --> 01:59:49.040
be different price points
that pack different amounts

01:59:49.040 --> 01:59:50.000
of technology.

01:59:50.000 --> 01:59:51.650
The holographic
display part, I think,

01:59:51.650 --> 01:59:53.840
is going to be more
expensive than doing

01:59:53.840 --> 01:59:58.250
one that just has the AI, but
is primarily communicating

01:59:58.250 --> 02:00:00.050
with you through audio.

02:00:00.050 --> 02:00:05.090
So I mean, the current
Ray-Ban Meta glasses are $299.

02:00:05.090 --> 02:00:07.925
I think when we have one
that has a display in it,

02:00:07.925 --> 02:00:09.800
it'll probably be some
amount more than that.

02:00:09.800 --> 02:00:11.340
But it'll also be more powerful.

02:00:11.340 --> 02:00:13.460
So I think that
people will choose

02:00:13.460 --> 02:00:16.640
what they want to use based
on what the capabilities are

02:00:16.640 --> 02:00:19.820
that they want and
what they can afford.

02:00:19.820 --> 02:00:23.090
But a lot of our goal
in building things

02:00:23.090 --> 02:00:29.260
is we try to make things that
can be accessible to everyone.

02:00:29.260 --> 02:00:34.330
Our game as a company isn't to
build things and then charge

02:00:34.330 --> 02:00:35.800
a premium price for it.

02:00:35.800 --> 02:00:39.790
We try to build things that
then everyone can use, and then

02:00:39.790 --> 02:00:42.580
become more useful because a
very large number of people

02:00:42.580 --> 02:00:44.450
are using them.

02:00:44.450 --> 02:00:47.380
So it's just a very
different approach.

02:00:47.380 --> 02:00:50.830
We're not like Apple or some
of these companies that just

02:00:50.830 --> 02:00:53.830
try to make something and
then sell it for as much

02:00:53.830 --> 02:00:57.020
as they can, which, I mean,
they're a great company.

02:00:57.020 --> 02:01:01.060
So I mean, I think that
model is fine, too.

02:01:01.060 --> 02:01:02.980
But our approach
is going to be we

02:01:02.980 --> 02:01:04.420
want stuff that
can be affordable

02:01:04.420 --> 02:01:06.295
so that way everyone in
the world can use it.

02:01:06.295 --> 02:01:07.878
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Long lines of health,

02:01:07.878 --> 02:01:10.030
I think the glasses will
also potentially solve

02:01:10.030 --> 02:01:12.670
a major problem in
a real way, which

02:01:12.670 --> 02:01:15.020
is the following for
both children and adults.

02:01:15.020 --> 02:01:18.430
It's very clear that viewing
objects in particular screens

02:01:18.430 --> 02:01:21.460
up close for too many hours
per day leads to myopia.

02:01:21.460 --> 02:01:24.100
It literally changes the
length of length of the eyeball

02:01:24.100 --> 02:01:26.180
and nearsightedness.

02:01:26.180 --> 02:01:28.820
And on the positive
side, we know,

02:01:28.820 --> 02:01:31.400
based on some really
large clinical trials,

02:01:31.400 --> 02:01:33.110
that kids who spend--

02:01:33.110 --> 02:01:37.820
and adults who spend two hours
a day or more out of doors

02:01:37.820 --> 02:01:40.610
don't experience that and maybe
even reverse their myopia.

02:01:40.610 --> 02:01:42.890
And it has something to do
with exposure to sunlight.

02:01:42.890 --> 02:01:45.140
But it has a lot to do
with long view, viewing

02:01:45.140 --> 02:01:47.690
things at a distance greater
than three or four feet away.

02:01:47.690 --> 02:01:49.640
And with the glasses,
I realize, one

02:01:49.640 --> 02:01:53.600
could actually do digital
work out of doors.

02:01:53.600 --> 02:01:56.240
It could measure and
tell you how much time

02:01:56.240 --> 02:01:58.970
you've spent looking at things
up close versus far away.

02:01:58.970 --> 02:02:02.180
I mean, this is just another
example that leaps to mind.

02:02:02.180 --> 02:02:04.550
But in accessing
the visual system,

02:02:04.550 --> 02:02:06.497
you're effectively
accessing the whole brain

02:02:06.497 --> 02:02:08.330
because it's the only
two bits of brain that

02:02:08.330 --> 02:02:10.038
are outside the cranial
vault. So it just

02:02:10.038 --> 02:02:12.770
seems like putting technology
right at the level of the eyes,

02:02:12.770 --> 02:02:15.110
seeing what the
eyes see, has just

02:02:15.110 --> 02:02:16.683
got to be the best way to go.

02:02:16.683 --> 02:02:17.600
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

02:02:17.600 --> 02:02:24.270
Well, multimodal, I think, is--
you want the visual sensation.

02:02:24.270 --> 02:02:28.050
But you also want
text or language.

02:02:28.050 --> 02:02:29.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sure.

02:02:29.100 --> 02:02:31.440
That all can be brought to
the level of the eyes, right?

02:02:31.440 --> 02:02:33.190
MARK ZUCKERBERG: What
do you mean by that?

02:02:33.190 --> 02:02:33.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, I mean, I think

02:02:33.900 --> 02:02:36.240
what we're describing
here is essentially

02:02:36.240 --> 02:02:39.270
taking the phone, the
computer, and bringing it

02:02:39.270 --> 02:02:40.560
all to the level of the eyes.

02:02:40.560 --> 02:02:41.610
And of course, one would like--

02:02:41.610 --> 02:02:42.450
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Oh,
Physically at your eyes?

02:02:42.450 --> 02:02:43.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Physically
at your eyes, right?

02:02:43.800 --> 02:02:44.010
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah.

02:02:44.010 --> 02:02:45.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And one
would like more kinesthetic

02:02:45.630 --> 02:02:47.520
information, as you mentioned
before-- where the legs are,

02:02:47.520 --> 02:02:48.720
maybe even lung function.

02:02:48.720 --> 02:02:50.360
Hey, have you taken
enough steps today?

02:02:50.360 --> 02:02:52.860
But that all can be-- if it can
be figured out on the phone,

02:02:52.860 --> 02:02:56.370
it can be-- by the phone, it can
be figured out by the glasses.

02:02:56.370 --> 02:02:58.140
But there's additional
information there,

02:02:58.140 --> 02:03:00.630
such as what are you
focusing on in your world.

02:03:00.630 --> 02:03:03.270
How much of your time is spent
looking at things far away

02:03:03.270 --> 02:03:03.960
versus up close?

02:03:03.960 --> 02:03:05.890
How much social time
did you have today?

02:03:05.890 --> 02:03:08.298
It's really tricky to
get that with a phone.

02:03:08.298 --> 02:03:09.840
If my phone were
right in front of us

02:03:09.840 --> 02:03:11.215
as if we were at
a standard lunch

02:03:11.215 --> 02:03:12.900
nowadays, certainly
in Silicon Valley,

02:03:12.900 --> 02:03:14.775
and then we're peering
at our phones, I mean,

02:03:14.775 --> 02:03:17.520
how much real direct attention
and was in the conversation

02:03:17.520 --> 02:03:18.990
at hand versus something else?

02:03:18.990 --> 02:03:20.640
You can get issues
of where are you

02:03:20.640 --> 02:03:23.798
placing your attention
by virtue of where

02:03:23.798 --> 02:03:24.840
you're placing your eyes.

02:03:24.840 --> 02:03:26.970
And I think that information
is not accessible

02:03:26.970 --> 02:03:29.050
with a phone in your
pocket or in front of you.

02:03:29.050 --> 02:03:33.300
Yeah, I mean, a little bit, but
not nearly as rich and complete

02:03:33.300 --> 02:03:35.280
information as one
gets when you're really

02:03:35.280 --> 02:03:37.320
pulling the data from
the level of vision

02:03:37.320 --> 02:03:39.510
and what kids and
adults are actually

02:03:39.510 --> 02:03:41.703
looking at and attending to.

02:03:41.703 --> 02:03:42.870
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah, yeah.

02:03:42.870 --> 02:03:44.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It
seems extremely valuable.

02:03:44.850 --> 02:03:47.430
You get autonomic information,
size of the pupils.

02:03:47.430 --> 02:03:49.320
So you get information
about internal states.

02:03:49.320 --> 02:03:53.070
MARK ZUCKERBERG: I mean, there's
internal sensor and outside.

02:03:53.070 --> 02:03:57.990
So the sensor on the Ray-Ban
Meta glasses is external.

02:03:57.990 --> 02:04:01.950
So it basically allows
you to see what you see--

02:04:01.950 --> 02:04:04.410
sorry, the AI system to
see what you're seeing.

02:04:04.410 --> 02:04:06.030
There's a separate
set of things which

02:04:06.030 --> 02:04:12.270
are eye tracking, which are
also very powerful for enabling

02:04:12.270 --> 02:04:13.800
a lot of interfaces.

02:04:13.800 --> 02:04:16.170
So if you want to
just look at something

02:04:16.170 --> 02:04:19.650
and select it by looking
at it with your eyes

02:04:19.650 --> 02:04:23.280
rather than having to drag
a controller over or pick up

02:04:23.280 --> 02:04:25.870
a hologram or
anything like that,

02:04:25.870 --> 02:04:28.370
you can do that
with eye tracking.

02:04:28.370 --> 02:04:33.675
So that's a pretty profound and
cool experience, too, as well

02:04:33.675 --> 02:04:35.050
as just understanding
what you're

02:04:35.050 --> 02:04:37.930
looking at so that way you're
not wasting compute power

02:04:37.930 --> 02:04:39.925
drawing pixels and
high resolution

02:04:39.925 --> 02:04:42.640
in a part of the world
that's going to be

02:04:42.640 --> 02:04:45.050
in your peripheral vision.

02:04:45.050 --> 02:04:49.380
So yeah, all of
these things, there

02:04:49.380 --> 02:04:51.750
are interesting design
and technology trade-offs,

02:04:51.750 --> 02:04:55.710
where if you want the external
sensor, that's one thing.

02:04:55.710 --> 02:04:57.697
If you also want
the eye tracking,

02:04:57.697 --> 02:04:59.280
now that's a different
set of sensors.

02:04:59.280 --> 02:05:02.310
Each one of these
consumes compute,

02:05:02.310 --> 02:05:04.080
which consumes battery.

02:05:04.080 --> 02:05:05.138
They take up more space.

02:05:05.138 --> 02:05:07.680
So it's like, where are the eye
tracking sensors going to be?

02:05:07.680 --> 02:05:09.263
It's like, well, you
want to make sure

02:05:09.263 --> 02:05:12.570
that the rim of the glasses is
actually quite thin because--

02:05:12.570 --> 02:05:17.520
I mean, there's a variance
of how thick can glasses

02:05:17.520 --> 02:05:21.640
be before they look more
like goggles than glasses.

02:05:21.640 --> 02:05:23.393
So I think that there's
this whole space.

02:05:23.393 --> 02:05:25.560
And I think people are going
to end up choosing what

02:05:25.560 --> 02:05:26.740
product makes sense for them.

02:05:26.740 --> 02:05:28.698
Maybe they want something
that's more powerful,

02:05:28.698 --> 02:05:30.450
that has more of the
sensors, but it's

02:05:30.450 --> 02:05:31.950
going to be a little
more expensive,

02:05:31.950 --> 02:05:33.750
maybe like slightly thicker.

02:05:33.750 --> 02:05:35.580
Or maybe you want
a more basic thing

02:05:35.580 --> 02:05:39.330
that just looks very similar
to what Ray-Ban glasses are

02:05:39.330 --> 02:05:42.780
that people have been wearing
for decades but has AI in it

02:05:42.780 --> 02:05:45.082
and you can capture
moments without having

02:05:45.082 --> 02:05:47.040
to take your phone out
and send them to people.

02:05:47.040 --> 02:05:52.210
In the latest version, we got
the ability in to live stream.

02:05:52.210 --> 02:05:55.570
I think that that's pretty
crazy, that now you can be--

02:05:55.570 --> 02:05:58.420
going back to your concert case
or whatever else you're doing,

02:05:58.420 --> 02:06:02.560
you can be doing
sports or watching

02:06:02.560 --> 02:06:04.150
your kids play something.

02:06:04.150 --> 02:06:05.710
And you can be watching.

02:06:05.710 --> 02:06:10.390
And you can be live streaming
it to your family group,

02:06:10.390 --> 02:06:11.470
so people can see it.

02:06:11.470 --> 02:06:15.163
I think that stuff is--

02:06:15.163 --> 02:06:17.080
I think that's pretty
cool, that you basically

02:06:17.080 --> 02:06:21.190
have a normal looking glasses at
this point that can live stream

02:06:21.190 --> 02:06:22.720
and has an AI assistant.

02:06:22.720 --> 02:06:26.020
So the stuff is making
a lot faster progress

02:06:26.020 --> 02:06:28.970
in a lot of ways than
I would have thought.

02:06:28.970 --> 02:06:29.870
And I don't know.

02:06:29.870 --> 02:06:30.970
I think people are going
to like this version.

02:06:30.970 --> 02:06:32.980
But there's a lot
more still to do.

02:06:32.980 --> 02:06:34.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think
it's super exciting.

02:06:34.210 --> 02:06:35.620
And I see a lot of technologies.

02:06:35.620 --> 02:06:37.203
This one's particularly
exciting to me

02:06:37.203 --> 02:06:39.100
because of how smooth
the interface is

02:06:39.100 --> 02:06:42.280
and for all the reasons
that you just mentioned.

02:06:42.280 --> 02:06:45.370
What's happening with and
what can we expect around

02:06:45.370 --> 02:06:48.370
AI interfaces and
maybe even avatars

02:06:48.370 --> 02:06:50.290
of people within social media?

02:06:50.290 --> 02:06:53.950
Are we not far off
from a day where

02:06:53.950 --> 02:06:56.530
there are multiple
versions of me

02:06:56.530 --> 02:06:58.145
and you on the
internet or people?

02:06:58.145 --> 02:07:00.020
For instance, I get
asked a lot of questions.

02:07:00.020 --> 02:07:02.645
I don't have the opportunity to
respond to all those questions.

02:07:02.645 --> 02:07:04.598
But with things
like ChatGPT, people

02:07:04.598 --> 02:07:06.640
are trying to generate
answers to those questions

02:07:06.640 --> 02:07:07.690
on other platforms.

02:07:07.690 --> 02:07:09.310
Will I have the
opportunity to soon

02:07:09.310 --> 02:07:11.290
have an AI version of
myself where people

02:07:11.290 --> 02:07:14.560
can ask me questions about
what I recommend for sleep

02:07:14.560 --> 02:07:17.895
and circadian rhythm, fitness,
mental health, et cetera based

02:07:17.895 --> 02:07:19.270
on content I've
already generated

02:07:19.270 --> 02:07:22.450
that will be accurate so they
could just ask my avatar?

02:07:22.450 --> 02:07:24.820
MARK ZUCKERBERG: Yeah,
this is something

02:07:24.820 --> 02:07:26.500
that I think a lot
of creators are going

02:07:26.500 --> 02:07:31.580
to want that we're
trying to build

02:07:31.580 --> 02:07:35.000
and I think we'll probably
have a version of next year.

02:07:35.000 --> 02:07:37.623
But there's a bunch
of constraints

02:07:37.623 --> 02:07:39.790
that I think we need to
make sure that we get right.

02:07:39.790 --> 02:07:43.210
So for one, I think it's
really important that--

02:07:43.210 --> 02:07:45.670
it's not that there's a
bunch of versions of you.

02:07:45.670 --> 02:07:50.540
It's that if anyone is creating
an AI assistant version of you,

02:07:50.540 --> 02:07:52.625
it should be something
that you control.

02:07:52.625 --> 02:07:55.000
I think there are some platforms
that are out there today

02:07:55.000 --> 02:07:57.560
that just let people like make--

02:07:57.560 --> 02:08:01.943
I don't know-- an AI bought
of me or other figures.

02:08:01.943 --> 02:08:03.110
And it's like, I don't know.

02:08:03.110 --> 02:08:05.895
I mean, we have
platform policies for--

02:08:08.470 --> 02:08:12.538
and for decades,
since the beginning

02:08:12.538 --> 02:08:14.830
of the company at this point,
which is almost 20 years,

02:08:14.830 --> 02:08:18.580
that basically don't
allow impersonation.

02:08:18.580 --> 02:08:21.100
Real identity is like
one of the core aspects

02:08:21.100 --> 02:08:23.950
that our company was started on.

02:08:23.950 --> 02:08:25.910
You want to authentically
be yourself.

02:08:25.910 --> 02:08:31.060
So yeah, I think if
you're almost any creator,

02:08:31.060 --> 02:08:34.660
being able to engage
your community--

02:08:34.660 --> 02:08:37.870
and there's just going
to be more demand

02:08:37.870 --> 02:08:40.670
to interact with you than
you have hours in the day.

02:08:40.670 --> 02:08:42.700
So there are both
people who out there

02:08:42.700 --> 02:08:44.440
who would benefit from
being able to talk

02:08:44.440 --> 02:08:46.520
to an AI version of you.

02:08:46.520 --> 02:08:48.700
And I think you,
and other creators,

02:08:48.700 --> 02:08:51.460
would benefit from being able
to keep your community engaged

02:08:51.460 --> 02:08:54.430
and service that demand that
people have to engage with you.

02:08:54.430 --> 02:08:59.890
But you're going to want to
know that that AI version of you

02:08:59.890 --> 02:09:02.833
or assistant is going
to represent you

02:09:02.833 --> 02:09:04.000
the way that you would want.

02:09:04.000 --> 02:09:06.490
And there are a
lot of things that

02:09:06.490 --> 02:09:09.590
are awesome about
these modern LLMs.

02:09:09.590 --> 02:09:13.310
But having perfect
predictability

02:09:13.310 --> 02:09:15.170
about how it's going
to represent something

02:09:15.170 --> 02:09:17.010
is not one of the
current strengths.

02:09:17.010 --> 02:09:19.090
So I think that
there's some work that

02:09:19.090 --> 02:09:20.090
needs to get done there.

02:09:20.090 --> 02:09:22.838
I don't think it needs to be
100% perfect all the time.

02:09:22.838 --> 02:09:25.130
But you need to have very
good confidence, I would say,

02:09:25.130 --> 02:09:27.320
that it's going to represent
you the way that you'd

02:09:27.320 --> 02:09:29.290
want for you to
want to turn it on,

02:09:29.290 --> 02:09:31.040
which, again, you
should have control over

02:09:31.040 --> 02:09:32.640
whether you turn it on.

02:09:32.640 --> 02:09:35.300
So we wanted to start in
a different place, which

02:09:35.300 --> 02:09:39.830
I think is a somewhat easier
problem, which is creating

02:09:39.830 --> 02:09:43.760
new characters for AI personas.

02:09:43.760 --> 02:09:46.400
So that way, it's not--

02:09:46.400 --> 02:09:51.380
we built one of the
AIs is like a chef.

02:09:51.380 --> 02:09:55.520
And they can help you
come up with things

02:09:55.520 --> 02:09:58.970
that you could cook and
can help you cook them.

02:09:58.970 --> 02:10:00.995
There's a couple
of people that are

02:10:00.995 --> 02:10:02.870
interested in different
types of fitness that

02:10:02.870 --> 02:10:05.660
can help you plan
out your workouts

02:10:05.660 --> 02:10:10.330
or help with recovery or
different things like that.

02:10:10.330 --> 02:10:14.958
There's an AI that's
focused on DIY crafts.

02:10:14.958 --> 02:10:16.750
There's somebody who's
a travel expert that

02:10:16.750 --> 02:10:19.270
can help you make travel
plans or give you ideas.

02:10:19.270 --> 02:10:20.800
But the key thing
about all of these

02:10:20.800 --> 02:10:24.760
is they're not modeled
off of existing people.

02:10:24.760 --> 02:10:30.250
So they don't have to have
100% fidelity to making sure

02:10:30.250 --> 02:10:33.623
that they never say something
that a real person who they're

02:10:33.623 --> 02:10:36.040
modeled after would never say
because they're just made up

02:10:36.040 --> 02:10:36.950
characters.

02:10:36.950 --> 02:10:42.380
So I think that that's a
somewhat easier problem.

02:10:42.380 --> 02:10:47.860
And we actually got a bunch
of different well-known people

02:10:47.860 --> 02:10:49.733
to play those characters
because we thought

02:10:49.733 --> 02:10:50.900
that would make it more fun.

02:10:50.900 --> 02:10:53.410
So there's like Snoop Dogg
is the dungeon master.

02:10:53.410 --> 02:10:54.850
So you can drop
him into a thread

02:10:54.850 --> 02:10:56.810
and play text-based games.

02:10:56.810 --> 02:11:01.060
And I do this with my daughter
when I tuck her in at night.

02:11:01.060 --> 02:11:04.960
And she just loves storytelling.

02:11:04.960 --> 02:11:08.092
And it's like Snoop Dogg,
as the dungeon master,

02:11:08.092 --> 02:11:10.050
will come up with here's
what's happening next.

02:11:10.050 --> 02:11:12.350
And she's like, OK, I
turn into a mermaid.

02:11:12.350 --> 02:11:14.030
And then I like
swim across the bay.

02:11:14.030 --> 02:11:17.273
And I go and find the
treasure chest and unlock it.

02:11:17.273 --> 02:11:19.190
And it's like, and then
Snoop Dogg just always

02:11:19.190 --> 02:11:21.080
will have a next
version of the--

02:11:21.080 --> 02:11:22.550
a next iteration on the story.

02:11:22.550 --> 02:11:24.540
So I mean, it's
stuff that's fun.

02:11:24.540 --> 02:11:26.132
But it's not
actually Snoop Dogg.

02:11:26.132 --> 02:11:28.340
He's just the actor who's
playing the dungeon master,

02:11:28.340 --> 02:11:29.340
which makes it more fun.

02:11:29.340 --> 02:11:31.910
So I think that's probably
the right place to start,

02:11:31.910 --> 02:11:36.320
is you can build versions
of these characters

02:11:36.320 --> 02:11:38.847
that people can interact
with doing different things.

02:11:38.847 --> 02:11:40.430
But I think where
you want to get over

02:11:40.430 --> 02:11:45.260
time is to the place where any
creator or any small business

02:11:45.260 --> 02:11:49.910
can very easily just create an
AI assistant that can represent

02:11:49.910 --> 02:11:54.050
them and interact with your
community or customers,

02:11:54.050 --> 02:11:58.250
if you're a business,
and basically just help

02:11:58.250 --> 02:12:00.930
you grow your enterprise.

02:12:00.930 --> 02:12:03.590
So I think that's
going to be cool.

02:12:03.590 --> 02:12:04.640
It's a long-term project.

02:12:04.640 --> 02:12:09.350
I think we'll have more progress
on it to report on next year.

02:12:09.350 --> 02:12:11.398
But I think that's coming.

02:12:11.398 --> 02:12:13.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm
super excited about it

02:12:13.190 --> 02:12:15.830
because we hear a lot
about the downsides of AI.

02:12:15.830 --> 02:12:18.920
I mean, I think people are now
coming around to the reality

02:12:18.920 --> 02:12:20.448
that AI is neither good nor bad.

02:12:20.448 --> 02:12:21.740
It can be used for good or bad.

02:12:21.740 --> 02:12:24.020
And there are a lot of
life-enhancing spaces

02:12:24.020 --> 02:12:26.180
that it's going to show
up and really, really

02:12:26.180 --> 02:12:30.950
improve the way that we engage
socially, what we learn,

02:12:30.950 --> 02:12:32.903
and that mental health
and physical health

02:12:32.903 --> 02:12:34.320
don't have to
suffer and, in fact,

02:12:34.320 --> 02:12:36.248
can be enhanced by the
sorts of technologies

02:12:36.248 --> 02:12:37.290
we've been talking about.

02:12:37.290 --> 02:12:39.900
So I know you're extremely busy.

02:12:39.900 --> 02:12:43.490
I so appreciate the
large amount of time

02:12:43.490 --> 02:12:46.250
you've given me today to sort
through all these things.

02:12:46.250 --> 02:12:46.850
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
Yeah, it's been fun.

02:12:46.850 --> 02:12:48.975
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And to
talk with you and Priscilla

02:12:48.975 --> 02:12:52.490
and to hear what's happening
and where things are headed,

02:12:52.490 --> 02:12:53.930
the future certainly is bright.

02:12:53.930 --> 02:12:55.880
I share in your optimism.

02:12:55.880 --> 02:12:59.070
And it's been only strengthened
by today's conversation.

02:12:59.070 --> 02:13:00.920
So thank you so much.

02:13:00.920 --> 02:13:02.720
And keep doing
what you're doing.

02:13:02.720 --> 02:13:06.180
And on behalf of myself
and everyone listening,

02:13:06.180 --> 02:13:08.630
thank you because, regardless
of what people say,

02:13:08.630 --> 02:13:11.730
we all use these
platforms excitedly.

02:13:11.730 --> 02:13:14.340
And it's clear that
there's a ton of intention,

02:13:14.340 --> 02:13:21.540
and care, and thought about what
could be in the positive sense.

02:13:21.540 --> 02:13:23.460
And that's really
worth highlighting.

02:13:23.460 --> 02:13:24.960
MARK ZUCKERBERG:
Awesome, thank you.

02:13:24.960 --> 02:13:25.627
I appreciate it.

